window.BL_KB = [{"id":"ai-development","title":"AI Development","kind":"DOMAIN","summary":"How models are built — machine learning fundamentals, neural networks, transformers, training and fine-tuning, MLOps and applied AI for operations.","icon":"","starters":[],"draws":[],"entries":[{"id":"ai-development#1","title":"Machine learning in one page","tags":["machine learning","supervised","unsupervised","reinforcement learning","basics"],"ask":["What problems is machine learning good at?","Supervised or unsupervised — how do I choose?"],"body":"Machine learning builds models that learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules.\n- Supervised learning learns from labeled examples: classification predicts categories (is this work order urgent?), regression predicts numbers (energy use tomorrow).\n- Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels: clustering (group similar buildings by usage), dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection.\n- Self-supervised learning creates labels from the data itself (predict the next word) and is how large language models are pretrained.\n- Reinforcement learning learns by trial and error to maximize reward (robotics, games, and fine-tuning language models from feedback).\n\nThe workflow: define the problem and success metric, gather and clean data, split into training, validation and test sets, choose a baseline, train, evaluate, iterate, deploy, then monitor for drift. Always compare against a simple baseline — \"predict the average\" or a rule of thumb. If ML can't beat it convincingly, you don't need ML."},{"id":"ai-development#2","title":"Bias, variance and overfitting","tags":["overfitting","underfitting","bias-variance","regularization","generalization"],"ask":["How do I know if my model is overfitting?","What does regularization do?"],"body":"A model's error comes from bias (too simple to capture the pattern — underfitting), variance (too sensitive to the particular training data — overfitting) and irreducible noise.\n\nSigns: underfitting shows poor performance on both training and validation data; overfitting shows excellent training performance but worse validation performance. Learning curves (error versus training-set size) make this visible.\n\nFighting overfitting:\n- More and more diverse data, and data augmentation.\n- Regularization: L2 (weight decay) shrinks weights; L1 drives some to zero (feature selection); dropout randomly disables neurons during training.\n- Simpler models or fewer features.\n- Early stopping when validation loss stops improving.\n- Cross-validation for reliable estimates.\nFighting underfitting: more expressive models, better features, longer training, less regularization. Deep learning adds a twist — very large models can generalize well despite enormous capacity (double descent) — but validation data remains the judge."},{"id":"ai-development#3","title":"Data preparation and feature engineering","tags":["feature engineering","data cleaning","data leakage","normalization","encoding"],"ask":["What is data leakage?","How do you handle missing values?"],"body":"Most ML effort goes into data.\n- Cleaning: fix types, remove duplicates, handle outliers deliberately, and standardize units (Fahrenheit versus Celsius, kWh versus MWh).\n- Missing values: drop, impute (median, most frequent, model-based), or add an \"is missing\" indicator — missingness itself can be informative.\n- Encoding: one-hot for low-cardinality categories, target or embedding encodings for high-cardinality ones.\n- Scaling: standardize or normalize numeric features for distance-based and gradient-based models (not needed for tree models).\n- Feature engineering: domain knowledge beats algorithms. For equipment data: runtime hours, cycles per day, time since last service, temperature deltas, rolling averages and day-of-week or season.\n\nData leakage is when information unavailable at prediction time sneaks into training — future data, target-derived features, or preprocessing fit on the whole dataset before splitting. It produces great offline scores and bad real-world results. Split first, fit transforms on training data only, and respect time order for time-series problems."},{"id":"ai-development#4","title":"Classic machine learning algorithms","tags":["algorithms","linear regression","logistic regression","decision trees","random forest","gradient boosting","XGBoost","k-means"],"ask":["Why is gradient boosting so popular?","Which algorithm should I try first?"],"body":"For tabular data, classic algorithms often beat deep learning.\n- Linear regression: predicts numbers as a weighted sum of features; interpretable and a strong baseline.\n- Logistic regression: classification with probabilities; fast and interpretable.\n- Decision trees: if-then splits; intuitive but prone to overfitting alone.\n- Random forests: many trees on random subsets of rows and features, averaged; robust with little tuning.\n- Gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost): trees built sequentially, each correcting previous errors; usually the top performer on structured data.\n- k-nearest neighbors: predicts from similar examples; simple but slow at scale.\n- Support vector machines: strong on smaller, high-dimensional datasets.\n- k-means: clusters data into k groups around centroids. DBSCAN finds arbitrarily shaped clusters and flags noise.\n- Naive Bayes: fast text classification baseline.\n\nTypical order for a tabular problem: a simple baseline, then logistic or linear regression, then gradient boosting with sensible defaults, then tune. scikit-learn implements most of these with a consistent fit and predict API."},{"id":"ai-development#5","title":"Evaluation metrics","tags":["metrics","accuracy","precision","recall","F1","ROC-AUC","RMSE","confusion matrix","calibration"],"ask":["Precision or recall — which matters more?","Why is accuracy misleading?"],"body":"Choose metrics that reflect the real cost of mistakes.\n\nClassification: the confusion matrix counts true and false positives and negatives.\n- Accuracy misleads on imbalanced data: if 1% of equipment fails, predicting \"never fails\" is 99% accurate and useless.\n- Precision: of the items flagged, how many were right? Matters when false alarms are costly.\n- Recall: of the real positives, how many did we catch? Matters when misses are costly — like a failing chiller.\n- F1 balances the two. The threshold you choose moves the trade-off.\n- ROC-AUC measures ranking quality across thresholds; PR-AUC is more informative for rare positives.\n- Calibration: do predicted probabilities match reality? (Of things predicted at 70%, do about 70% happen?)\n\nRegression: MAE (average absolute error, robust and in original units), RMSE (penalizes large errors), MAPE (percentage error; unstable near zero), and R² (variance explained).\n\nAlways report metrics on a held-out test set touched only once, and break results down by segment (building, equipment type) to find hidden weaknesses."},{"id":"ai-development#6","title":"Cross-validation and hyperparameter tuning","tags":["cross-validation","hyperparameters","tuning","grid search","Bayesian optimization","validation"],"ask":["How many folds should I use?","Grid search or random search?"],"body":"Hyperparameters are settings you choose rather than learn: tree depth, learning rate, regularization strength.\n\nCross-validation gives reliable performance estimates: k-fold (commonly 5 or 10) trains on k−1 folds and validates on the remaining one, rotating through all folds. Use stratified folds for imbalanced classes, group folds when rows share an entity (all readings from one building in the same fold), and time-series splits (train on the past, validate on the future) for temporal data.\n\nSearch strategies:\n- Grid search tries every combination; it gets expensive quickly.\n- Random search samples combinations and usually finds good settings faster.\n- Bayesian optimization (Optuna, Hyperopt) models which settings look promising and focuses there.\n- Early-stopping-based methods such as Hyperband drop bad candidates quickly.\n\nKeep a final test set completely out of tuning; otherwise you overfit to your validation process. Record every experiment so results are reproducible."},{"id":"ai-development#7","title":"Neural network fundamentals","tags":["neural networks","backpropagation","gradient descent","activation functions","optimizers","Adam","learning rate"],"ask":["How does backpropagation work?","Why does the learning rate matter so much?"],"body":"A neural network stacks layers of simple units. Each computes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias, then applies a nonlinear activation function (ReLU, GELU, sigmoid, tanh). Without nonlinearity, any stack of layers collapses into one linear function.\n\nTraining minimizes a loss function by adjusting weights:\n- Forward pass computes predictions and the loss.\n- Backpropagation uses the chain rule to compute the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight.\n- Gradient descent nudges weights against the gradient, scaled by the learning rate.\n\nMini-batch stochastic gradient descent uses small batches for efficiency and helpful noise. Adam and AdamW adapt per-parameter step sizes and are the default for most deep learning. The learning rate is the most important hyperparameter: too high diverges, too low crawls. Schedules such as warmup followed by cosine decay help.\n\nStabilizers: normalization layers (batch norm, layer norm), residual connections that let gradients flow through deep networks, careful initialization, and gradient clipping."},{"id":"ai-development#8","title":"Loss functions","tags":["loss function","cross-entropy","mean squared error","objective","training"],"ask":["Which loss should I use for classification?","What is cross-entropy?"],"body":"The loss function defines what \"good\" means during training.\n- Mean squared error (MSE) for regression; penalizes large errors heavily. Mean absolute error and Huber loss are more robust to outliers.\n- Cross-entropy for classification: binary cross-entropy for yes/no, categorical cross-entropy (with softmax) for multiple classes. It strongly penalizes confident wrong predictions.\n- Language models use cross-entropy on next-token prediction; perplexity is its exponential, a measure of how \"surprised\" the model is by text.\n- Contrastive losses (InfoNCE, triplet) pull similar items together and push different ones apart; they train embedding models.\n- Focal loss and class weights help with heavy class imbalance.\n- Ranking losses train search and recommendation models.\n\nThe loss should match what you care about, but sometimes it's only a proxy (you optimize cross-entropy but report F1). Watch training and validation loss together: diverging curves signal overfitting."},{"id":"ai-development#9","title":"Convolutional neural networks","tags":["CNN","convolution","computer vision","image classification","ResNet"],"ask":["Why do CNNs work so well on images?","What is transfer learning?"],"body":"Convolutional neural networks process grid data such as images. Convolution layers slide small learned filters across the image, detecting local patterns — edges, then textures, then parts, then objects — in deeper layers. Weight sharing makes them efficient and translation-tolerant; pooling or strides reduce spatial size.\n\nLandmark architectures: LeNet, AlexNet (which kicked off the deep learning wave in 2012), VGG, ResNet (residual connections enabled very deep networks), EfficientNet and MobileNet (efficient enough for phones). Vision Transformers now rival or beat CNNs at scale, and hybrids are common.\n\nTransfer learning is the practical superpower: start from a model pretrained on a large dataset and fine-tune it on your smaller one. A few hundred labeled photos of equipment conditions can produce a useful classifier this way. Augmentation (flips, crops, color and brightness changes) multiplies limited data. On phones, compact models run in real time — CameraX plus ML Kit or LiteRT (formerly TensorFlow Lite)."},{"id":"ai-development#10","title":"Recurrent networks and sequence models","tags":["RNN","LSTM","GRU","sequence models","time series","history"],"ask":["Why did transformers replace RNNs?","Are LSTMs still useful?"],"body":"Recurrent neural networks process sequences one step at a time, carrying a hidden state forward, which suits text, speech and sensor streams. Plain RNNs struggle with long-range dependencies because gradients vanish or explode over many steps. LSTMs and GRUs add gates that control what to remember and forget, which made them the standard for language and speech before 2018.\n\nTransformers replaced them for most tasks because attention connects any two positions directly, and training parallelizes across the whole sequence instead of stepping through it. That scalability made large language models possible.\n\nRNN-style ideas are still alive: LSTMs and GRUs remain solid for small time-series problems and on tiny devices, and newer state-space models (Mamba and relatives) and linear-attention hybrids revisit recurrence to handle very long sequences efficiently. For a sensor-forecasting project, try gradient boosting with lag features or a simple LSTM before reaching for a transformer."},{"id":"ai-development#11","title":"Transformers and attention","tags":["transformer","attention","self-attention","multi-head attention","positional encoding","architecture"],"ask":["Explain attention like I'm a facilities guy","What's the difference between encoder and decoder models?"],"body":"The transformer (from \"Attention Is All You Need,\" 2017) is the architecture behind modern language models.\n\nSelf-attention lets every token look at every other token and decide how much each matters. Each token produces a query, a key and a value vector; attention weights come from query-key similarity (scaled dot products passed through softmax), and the output is a weighted mix of values. In \"the chiller tripped because it overheated,\" attention helps \"it\" connect to \"chiller.\"\n\nMulti-head attention runs several attention patterns in parallel, each free to capture different relationships. Each transformer block combines attention with a feed-forward network, residual connections and layer normalization; models stack dozens of blocks. Since attention has no built-in order, positional encodings (rotary embeddings, or RoPE, are common now) inject position.\n\nVariants: encoder-only models (BERT) are good for understanding and embeddings; decoder-only models (GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama) generate text; encoder-decoder models (T5) map inputs to outputs. Attention cost grows with the square of sequence length, which drives research into efficient attention for long contexts."},{"id":"ai-development#12","title":"How large language models are pretrained","tags":["pretraining","LLM training","scaling laws","compute","datasets","tokens"],"ask":["What are scaling laws?","How much data goes into an LLM?"],"body":"Pretraining teaches a model language and world knowledge by predicting the next token over trillions of tokens of text and code, filtered for quality and deduplicated. The model learns grammar, facts, reasoning patterns and coding skills as side effects of getting better at prediction.\n\nScaling laws (Kaplan et al., then DeepMind's Chinchilla work) found that loss improves predictably as you increase parameters, data and compute together. Chinchilla showed many models were undertrained for their size; a rough guideline was about 20 training tokens per parameter for compute-optimal training, though modern models often train far beyond that so they're cheaper to run.\n\nPretraining runs use thousands of GPUs or TPUs for weeks, with distributed parallelism across data, model layers and tensors, mixed precision, and careful handling of loss spikes. The result is a base model: knowledgeable but not yet a helpful assistant — it continues text rather than following instructions. Post-training turns it into one. Data quality, mixture and deduplication matter as much as raw size."},{"id":"ai-development#13","title":"Post-training: SFT, RLHF and alignment","tags":["post-training","SFT","RLHF","RLAIF","Constitutional AI","DPO","alignment","instruction tuning"],"ask":["How does RLHF work?","What is Constitutional AI?"],"body":"Post-training shapes a base model into a helpful, honest and harmless assistant.\n- Supervised fine-tuning (SFT, instruction tuning): train on high-quality examples of instructions and ideal responses, including multi-turn conversations and tool use.\n- Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF): people compare pairs of responses; a reward model learns those preferences; the model is optimized (classically with PPO) to produce responses the reward model scores highly, with a penalty for drifting too far from the original.\n- Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) and related methods learn directly from preference pairs without a separate reward model or RL loop — simpler and widely used.\n- RLAIF and Constitutional AI (introduced by Anthropic) use AI feedback guided by a written set of principles to critique and revise responses, reducing reliance on human labels for harmlessness.\n- Reinforcement learning on verifiable tasks (math, code with tests) has driven recent gains in reasoning models that \"think\" before answering.\n\nAlignment work also includes red-teaming, safety evaluations and interpretability research into what's happening inside models."},{"id":"ai-development#14","title":"Tokenization","tags":["tokenization","tokens","BPE","SentencePiece","vocabulary"],"ask":["Why do models struggle to count letters?","Why do some languages cost more tokens?"],"body":"Models read tokens, not characters or words. Byte-pair encoding (BPE) builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging the most frequent pairs of symbols, so common words become single tokens and rare words split into pieces (\"unbelievably\" might become \"un\", \"believ\", \"ably\"). SentencePiece and similar tools implement this directly on raw text, including spaces. Vocabularies typically hold roughly 32,000 to over 200,000 tokens.\n\nConsequences you'll notice:\n- Cost and context limits are counted in tokens; English averages around four characters per token, while many other languages, code and numbers use more.\n- Character-level tasks — counting letters, reversing strings, exact spelling — are hard because the model never sees individual letters directly.\n- Arithmetic can suffer when numbers split into odd chunks.\n- Leading spaces and capitalization create different tokens, so formatting changes can subtly change behavior.\nUse the provider's tokenizer or token-counting API to measure prompts precisely."},{"id":"ai-development#15","title":"Fine-tuning in practice","tags":["fine-tuning","LoRA","QLoRA","PEFT","Hugging Face","training data","adapters"],"ask":["How do I fine-tune a small model on my data?","How many examples do I need?"],"body":"A practical recipe for fine-tuning an open-weight model:\n- Define the task and build an eval set first, then measure the base model with good prompting.\n- Prepare data in a chat or instruction format: hundreds to a few thousand high-quality, diverse examples. Quality beats quantity; remove duplicates and contradictions.\n- Use parameter-efficient fine-tuning. LoRA freezes the base weights and trains small low-rank adapter matrices; QLoRA does this on a 4-bit-quantized base model, letting 7–8-billion-parameter models fine-tune on a single consumer or cloud GPU.\n- Typical starting points: a learning rate around 1e-4 to 2e-4 for LoRA, 1–3 epochs, rank 8–64, and watching validation loss to avoid overfitting.\n- Tools: Hugging Face Transformers, PEFT and TRL; Unsloth for speed; Axolotl for config-driven runs; or provider-hosted fine-tuning APIs.\n- Evaluate against the baseline on your eval set, and check you haven't damaged general abilities (catastrophic forgetting).\nMerge the adapter or serve it alongside the base model, and keep data, config and results versioned."},{"id":"ai-development#16","title":"Embedding models and contrastive learning","tags":["embeddings","contrastive learning","sentence transformers","semantic similarity","training embeddings"],"ask":["How are embedding models trained?","Should I fine-tune embeddings for my domain?"],"body":"Embedding models turn text, images or other data into vectors where distance reflects meaning. They're usually transformer encoders trained with contrastive learning: given an anchor (a question), pull its positive match (the right passage) closer and push negatives (wrong passages) apart. Hard negatives — passages that look similar but are wrong — teach the finest distinctions.\n\nMultimodal models such as CLIP learn a shared space for images and text from captioned images, enabling text-to-image search.\n\nChoosing and adapting:\n- Start with a strong general embedding model and benchmark it on your own queries and documents (recall at k).\n- Domain jargon (equipment model numbers, facility codes, internal acronyms) may justify fine-tuning on pairs mined from your data — question and answer, title and body, or search query and clicked result.\n- Consider dimension and speed: smaller vectors are cheaper to store and search; some models support truncating dimensions (Matryoshka embeddings).\nEmbeddings complement keyword search rather than replacing it."},{"id":"ai-development#17","title":"Diffusion models and image generation","tags":["diffusion","image generation","Stable Diffusion","generative AI","latent diffusion"],"ask":["How do diffusion models generate images?","What is classifier-free guidance?"],"body":"Diffusion models learn to reverse a noising process. During training, images are gradually corrupted with noise, and a neural network learns to predict and remove that noise at each step. To generate, start from pure noise and denoise step by step into an image.\n\nLatent diffusion (Stable Diffusion and successors) works in a compressed latent space from an autoencoder, making generation far cheaper than working on pixels. Text conditioning comes from a text encoder, and cross-attention steers the image toward the prompt. Classifier-free guidance strengthens prompt adherence by contrasting conditioned and unconditioned predictions — higher guidance means more literal and less varied results.\n\nExtensions: image-to-image and inpainting edit existing pictures; ControlNet adds structural guides like edges or poses; LoRA adapters teach styles or subjects from a few images. Newer architectures use transformers as the denoiser and flow-matching training, and the same ideas extend to video and audio. Practical concerns include licensing, consent for likenesses, and provenance labeling of generated media."},{"id":"ai-development#18","title":"The Python ML toolkit","tags":["Python","NumPy","pandas","scikit-learn","PyTorch","Hugging Face","Jupyter","tools"],"ask":["What should I learn first for ML in Python?","PyTorch or TensorFlow?"],"body":"The standard stack:\n- NumPy: fast n-dimensional arrays and vectorized math — the foundation of everything else.\n- pandas (or Polars for speed): tabular data loading, cleaning, grouping, joining and time series.\n- Matplotlib and Seaborn or Plotly: visualization; always plot your data before modeling.\n- scikit-learn: classic ML with a consistent API — pipelines, preprocessing, models, metrics and cross-validation.\n- XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost: gradient boosting.\n- PyTorch: the dominant deep learning framework in research and increasingly in production. TensorFlow and Keras remain in many deployments, and JAX is popular for large-scale research.\n- Hugging Face: Transformers (pretrained models), Datasets, Tokenizers, PEFT and the Hub for sharing models.\n- Jupyter notebooks or VS Code notebooks for exploration; move stable code into modules with tests.\nLearn in this order: Python and NumPy, then pandas and plotting, then scikit-learn end to end, then PyTorch fundamentals, then Hugging Face."},{"id":"ai-development#19","title":"The PyTorch training loop","tags":["PyTorch","training loop","DataLoader","optimizer","autograd","GPU"],"ask":["Walk me through a PyTorch training loop","How do I use a GPU in PyTorch?"],"body":"A minimal PyTorch training loop:\n- Data: a Dataset returns individual samples; a DataLoader batches, shuffles and loads them in parallel.\n- Model: subclass nn.Module, define layers in __init__ and the computation in forward.\n- Loss and optimizer: for example nn.CrossEntropyLoss and torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=3e-4).\n- Each step: move the batch to the device, zero the gradients, run the forward pass, compute the loss, call loss.backward() so autograd computes gradients, then optimizer.step() to update the weights.\n- Each epoch: switch to model.eval() and wrap validation in torch.no_grad(), compute metrics, then return to model.train().\n\nUse device = \"cuda\" if available (\"mps\" on Apple silicon) and move both the model and the tensors there. Speedups: mixed precision (torch.autocast with bfloat16), torch.compile, larger batches with gradient accumulation, and pinned memory in the DataLoader. Save checkpoints of the model and optimizer state, set random seeds for reproducibility, and log metrics to TensorBoard or Weights & Biases. Libraries such as PyTorch Lightning and Hugging Face Accelerate remove boilerplate."},{"id":"ai-development#20","title":"GPUs, memory and distributed training","tags":["GPU","CUDA","VRAM","mixed precision","distributed training","FSDP","DeepSpeed"],"ask":["How much GPU memory do I need to fine-tune a model?","What is FSDP?"],"body":"GPUs accelerate the matrix math of deep learning; NVIDIA's CUDA ecosystem dominates, with AMD ROCm, Google TPUs and Apple silicon as alternatives.\n\nMemory math for a model with P parameters:\n- Inference in 16-bit: about 2P bytes for weights (a 7-billion-parameter model needs about 14 GB), plus KV cache.\n- Full fine-tuning with AdamW in mixed precision: roughly 16P bytes or more for weights, gradients and optimizer states — over 100 GB for a 7B model before activations.\n- QLoRA: 4-bit base weights (about 0.5P bytes) plus small adapters — feasible on a 16–24 GB GPU.\n\nTechniques: mixed precision (bf16 or fp16), gradient checkpointing (recompute activations to save memory), gradient accumulation (simulate large batches), and 8-bit optimizers.\n\nDistributed training: data parallelism (DDP) copies the model to each GPU and splits batches; FSDP and DeepSpeed ZeRO shard parameters, gradients and optimizer states across GPUs so larger models fit; tensor and pipeline parallelism split the model itself for the largest runs. Cloud GPU rental works well for occasional fine-tuning."},{"id":"ai-development#21","title":"MLOps","tags":["MLOps","experiment tracking","model registry","MLflow","Weights and Biases","DVC","pipelines","drift"],"ask":["What does a minimal MLOps setup look like?","How do you detect model drift?"],"body":"MLOps applies DevOps discipline to machine learning, where the system depends on code, data and model together.\n\nCore practices:\n- Experiment tracking (MLflow, Weights & Biases): log parameters, metrics, code version and artifacts for every run.\n- Data and model versioning (DVC, lakeFS, a model registry) so any model can be traced to the exact data and code that produced it.\n- Pipelines (Kubeflow, Airflow, Prefect, Dagster, SageMaker or Vertex AI Pipelines) automate data prep, training, evaluation and deployment.\n- Validation gates: a new model must beat the current one on held-out data and pass fairness and sanity checks before promotion.\n- Serving: batch scoring or online APIs, with versioned endpoints and canary releases.\n- Monitoring: data drift (input distributions change), concept drift (the relationship between inputs and outcomes changes), prediction quality once labels arrive, and latency and cost.\n- Retraining triggers on a schedule or when drift is detected.\n\nMinimal setup for a small team: git, a tracked notebook-to-script workflow, MLflow, a scheduled retraining job, and a dashboard of model metrics."},{"id":"ai-development#22","title":"Deploying models to devices and production","tags":["deployment","ONNX","LiteRT","TensorFlow Lite","quantization","edge","model serving"],"ask":["How do I run my model on Android?","What does quantization cost in accuracy?"],"body":"Getting models out of notebooks:\n- Online serving: wrap the model in an API (FastAPI, TorchServe, NVIDIA Triton, BentoML), containerize it, autoscale, and monitor latency.\n- Batch scoring: run predictions on a schedule and write them to a database — simpler and cheaper when real time isn't needed, such as nightly equipment risk scores.\n- Interchange formats: ONNX lets models trained in one framework run in optimized runtimes across platforms.\n- Mobile and edge: LiteRT (formerly TensorFlow Lite), ONNX Runtime Mobile, ExecuTorch for PyTorch, Core ML on Apple devices, and ML Kit for ready-made on-device features on Android.\n\nOptimization: post-training quantization to 8-bit usually costs little accuracy and cuts size by about four times; 4-bit needs more care. Pruning removes unimportant weights; distillation trains a small model to mimic a large one. Always re-evaluate after optimizing, and test on real target hardware, since accelerator support varies by device."},{"id":"ai-development#23","title":"Time-series forecasting","tags":["time series","forecasting","seasonality","ARIMA","Prophet","energy","demand"],"ask":["How would you forecast building energy use?","What are lag features?"],"body":"Time series have order, trend and seasonality, and they need special handling.\n- Decompose first: trend, seasonality (daily, weekly, yearly), and residuals. Plot everything.\n- Baselines: last value (naive), the same time last week (seasonal naive), and moving averages. They're surprisingly hard to beat.\n- Statistical models: exponential smoothing (ETS, Holt-Winters) and ARIMA or SARIMA; Prophet handles multiple seasonalities and holidays conveniently.\n- Machine learning: gradient boosting with lag features (the value one hour, one day and one week ago), rolling statistics, calendar features (hour, weekday, holiday) and external drivers such as outdoor temperature or occupancy. Often the strongest practical approach.\n- Deep learning and foundation models for time series exist and help at scale, but start simple.\n\nValidate with walk-forward (rolling-origin) splits — never shuffle time. Report errors at the horizon that matters (next-day peak demand). For facilities, weather-normalized energy forecasting helps catch waste, plan demand charges and verify savings from retrofits."},{"id":"ai-development#24","title":"Anomaly detection and predictive maintenance","tags":["anomaly detection","predictive maintenance","sensors","isolation forest","HVAC","condition monitoring"],"ask":["How do I start predictive maintenance with BAS data?","What algorithms detect anomalies?"],"body":"Predictive maintenance uses equipment data to fix things before they fail — a natural fit for facility operations.\n\nData sources: building automation system (BAS) trends (supply and return temperatures, pressures, valve and damper positions, run status), vibration, motor current, runtime hours, alarm logs and work-order history (your failure labels).\n\nApproaches, from simple to advanced:\n- Rules and thresholds from engineering knowledge (fault detection and diagnostics rules, such as simultaneous heating and cooling).\n- Statistical control charts and z-scores on rolling baselines.\n- Unsupervised anomaly detection: isolation forests, one-class models, or autoencoders that flag unusual combinations of readings.\n- Supervised failure prediction when you have enough labeled failures, using features such as trends, time since service and cycling rates.\n- Remaining-useful-life models for critical assets.\n\nPractical tips: start with one critical asset class (chillers, air handlers, pumps), get the data clean and time-aligned, involve technicians to label events, measure precision so alarms stay trustworthy, and feed alerts into work orders. Value comes from fewer emergency calls and longer asset life, not from a fancy model."},{"id":"ai-development#25","title":"Reinforcement learning basics","tags":["reinforcement learning","RL","reward","policy","Q-learning","agents","control"],"ask":["Where is reinforcement learning actually used?","What's the explore-exploit trade-off?"],"body":"Reinforcement learning (RL) trains an agent to act in an environment to maximize cumulative reward. Key pieces: states, actions, rewards, a policy (what to do in each state), and value functions (how good a state or action is in the long run).\n\nClassic algorithms: Q-learning and Deep Q-Networks learn action values; policy-gradient methods (REINFORCE, PPO) optimize the policy directly; actor-critic methods combine both. Model-based RL learns a model of the environment to plan.\n\nThe exploration-exploitation trade-off is central: try new actions to learn, or use what already works? Multi-armed bandits are the simplest version, used in A/B testing and recommendations.\n\nReal uses: game playing, robotics, recommendations, chip design, data-center cooling optimization, HVAC control research, and — most visibly — fine-tuning language models from human or AI feedback and from verifiable rewards. RL is powerful but sample-hungry and finicky; reward hacking (exploiting a poorly specified reward) is a constant risk, so simulate and constrain heavily before letting an RL policy control physical equipment."},{"id":"ai-development#26","title":"Interpreting models","tags":["interpretability","explainability","SHAP","LIME","feature importance","transparency"],"ask":["How do I explain a model's prediction to a manager?","What is SHAP?"],"body":"Interpretability builds trust, helps debug, and is sometimes required by regulation.\n- Inherently interpretable models: linear models (coefficients), small decision trees and rule lists. Prefer them when they perform well enough.\n- Global feature importance: permutation importance shuffles a feature and measures how much performance drops; it's more reliable than built-in tree importances.\n- SHAP values attribute each prediction to features using game theory, giving consistent local explanations (\"this pump is flagged mainly because vibration rose 40% and it's 900 hours past service\") and global summaries.\n- LIME fits a simple local model around one prediction.\n- Partial dependence and ICE plots show how predictions change as one feature varies.\n- Counterfactuals answer \"what would need to change for a different outcome?\"\n- For deep and language models, attention maps are unreliable explanations; mechanistic interpretability research studies internal features and circuits.\nExplanations describe the model, not necessarily the world — correlations aren't causes."},{"id":"ai-development#27","title":"Computer vision tasks","tags":["computer vision","object detection","segmentation","YOLO","OCR","image classification"],"ask":["What's the difference between detection and segmentation?","How could vision help in facilities?"],"body":"Common vision tasks:\n- Image classification: one label per image (is this filter clean or dirty?).\n- Object detection: boxes and labels for each object (find every fire extinguisher in a photo). YOLO-family models are fast and popular; DETR-style transformers are another approach.\n- Segmentation: semantic segmentation labels every pixel by class; instance segmentation separates individual objects; the Segment Anything models can segment almost anything from a prompt.\n- OCR and document understanding: read nameplates, meters and forms.\n- Pose estimation, tracking in video, depth estimation and anomaly detection on surfaces such as cracks, corrosion and leaks.\n\nFacilities ideas: reading analog gauges and meters from photos, detecting roof or pavement damage in drone imagery, checking for blocked exits, counting occupancy for space planning (with privacy safeguards), and inventorying assets from walkthrough photos. Start with pretrained models and modest labeled sets, use augmentation, and evaluate on photos from your own sites — lighting and angles vary more than you expect. Vision-capable language models can also handle many of these tasks from a prompt."},{"id":"ai-development#28","title":"Privacy-preserving machine learning","tags":["privacy","differential privacy","federated learning","anonymization","data protection"],"ask":["Can a model leak its training data?","What is federated learning?"],"body":"Models can memorize and leak training data, especially rare records, so privacy needs design.\n- Data minimization: collect and keep only what you need; strip identifiers early.\n- Anonymization is harder than it looks — combinations like ZIP code, birth date and gender can re-identify people. Use aggregation, generalization and suppression thoughtfully.\n- Differential privacy adds calibrated noise during training (for example DP-SGD) or to query results, mathematically limiting what can be learned about any one person, at some cost in accuracy.\n- Federated learning trains across many devices or sites without centralizing raw data: devices compute updates locally and share only those updates. Keyboard suggestion models are a well-known example.\n- Secure enclaves and encryption-based techniques protect data during computation.\n- On-device inference keeps personal data on the device entirely — BlackLab's approach for its assistant.\nPair the technical measures with governance: access controls, audits, consent, and clear retention rules."},{"id":"ai-development#29","title":"A learning roadmap for AI development","tags":["learning path","roadmap","math for ML","courses","study plan","career"],"ask":["What math do I really need for ML?","Give me a 6-month AI study plan"],"body":"A practical roadmap for a working professional:\n- Foundations (month 1–2): Python fluency, NumPy and pandas, plotting, and Git. Math as needed — linear algebra (vectors, matrices, dot products), calculus (derivatives and the chain rule for gradients), and probability and statistics (distributions, expectation, Bayes' rule, hypothesis tests).\n- Classic ML (month 2–3): scikit-learn end to end on real tabular data — ideally your own facilities data such as work orders, energy and equipment. Learn evaluation and validation deeply.\n- Deep learning (month 3–4): PyTorch basics, CNNs with transfer learning, and how transformers and attention work. Resources: fast.ai, Andrej Karpathy's \"Neural Networks: Zero to Hero,\" and the d2l.ai book.\n- LLMs and AI engineering (month 4–6): prompting, RAG, tool use, evals; build and deploy a small project end to end.\n- Ongoing: read papers selectively, follow a few trusted sources, and build in public on GitHub.\n\nLearn by projects with real stakes: a PM-compliance dashboard, a work-order classifier, an energy anomaly detector. Teaching others what you learn locks it in. Certifications can help structure study, but a portfolio of working projects speaks louder."}]},{"id":"ai-engineering","title":"AI Engineering","kind":"DOMAIN","summary":"Building real products on large language models — prompting, RAG, agents, tools, evals, safety, cost and on-device AI.","icon":"","starters":[],"draws":[],"entries":[{"id":"ai-engineering#1","title":"What AI engineering is","tags":["AI engineering","LLM applications","foundation models","ML engineering"],"ask":["How is AI engineering different from ML engineering?","What skills does an AI engineer need?"],"body":"AI engineering is building products on top of foundation models — large language models and multimodal models — instead of training models from scratch. Classic ML engineering centers on data collection, feature engineering and training; AI engineering centers on adapting a capable pretrained model to a task through prompts, context, retrieval, tools, fine-tuning and evaluation, then running it reliably at acceptable cost and latency.\n\nCore skills: prompt and context design, retrieval-augmented generation, tool and agent design, evaluation methodology, guarding against failure modes (hallucination, prompt injection), observability, and the usual software engineering discipline — APIs, testing, security, deployment. The biggest mindset shift is that outputs are probabilistic: you engineer for distributions of behavior, measured by evals, rather than for single deterministic answers.\n\nA good first principle: start with the simplest thing that could work — one well-written prompt to a strong model — measure it, and add complexity (retrieval, tools, agents, fine-tuning) only when evals show you need it."},{"id":"ai-engineering#2","title":"How LLMs work, in practice","tags":["LLM","tokens","context window","temperature","top-p","sampling","next-token prediction"],"ask":["What does temperature actually do?","Why do models make things up?"],"body":"A large language model predicts the next token — a word piece of roughly four characters of English on average — given all previous tokens. Generating text is repeated sampling from that predicted distribution.\n\nKey knobs and limits:\n- Context window: the maximum tokens the model can attend to at once (prompt plus output). Everything the model \"knows\" about your situation must fit here.\n- Temperature: scales randomness. Near 0 is focused and repeatable (extraction, classification, code); higher values are more varied (brainstorming).\n- Top-p (nucleus) and top-k limit sampling to the most likely tokens.\n- Max output tokens caps response length and cost.\n- Stop sequences end generation at a marker.\n\nModels know what they learned during training up to a knowledge cutoff, plus whatever you put in the context. They don't look facts up unless you give them tools or documents. Hallucinations happen because the model produces plausible continuations even when it lacks knowledge; grounding, citations and permission to say \"I don't know\" reduce them. Pricing and speed are typically measured per input and output token."},{"id":"ai-engineering#3","title":"Prompt engineering fundamentals","tags":["prompt engineering","prompts","few-shot","system prompt","instructions","chain of thought"],"ask":["What makes a prompt good?","When do examples help?"],"body":"Treat the model like a brilliant new colleague with no context.\n- Be explicit about the task, the audience, the format and what good looks like. Say what to do rather than only what not to do.\n- Give context and the reason behind instructions (\"This summary is read aloud while driving, so no bullet symbols\").\n- Use a system prompt for role, rules and persistent behavior; put task-specific material in the user turn.\n- Structure long prompts with clear sections or tags (instructions, documents, examples) so the model can tell them apart.\n- Few-shot examples teach format and judgment faster than descriptions; make them diverse and representative.\n- For complex reasoning, ask the model to think step by step or use a model's built-in extended thinking, then give the final answer in a defined place.\n- Specify output format precisely (JSON schema, headings, length).\n- Put long documents before the question; ask the model to quote relevant parts before answering.\nIterate like engineering: keep a test set, change one thing at a time, and compare outputs side by side."},{"id":"ai-engineering#4","title":"Structured outputs and tool use","tags":["tool use","function calling","structured output","JSON schema","tools"],"ask":["How does function calling work?","How do you make JSON output reliable?"],"body":"Tool use (function calling) lets a model request actions. You describe tools with names, descriptions and JSON-schema parameters; the model replies with a tool call; your code executes it and returns the result; the model continues. This is how assistants check calendars, query databases or send email.\n\nDesign tools like good APIs: clear names and descriptions (the model reads them), few required parameters, unambiguous types and enums, meaningful errors the model can recover from, and results that are concise. Prefer a few well-scoped tools over dozens of overlapping ones.\n\nFor structured outputs, use the provider's JSON or structured-output mode when available, which constrains generation to your schema. Otherwise give the schema plus an example, validate with a parser (Pydantic, Zod, kotlinx.serialization), and retry with the validation error in the prompt. Keep schemas flat where possible.\n\nSafety: tools that act — sending, buying, deleting — should require confirmation or run with least privilege. BlackLab's assistant asks before sending email for this reason."},{"id":"ai-engineering#5","title":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)","tags":["RAG","retrieval-augmented generation","retrieval","grounding","citations","knowledge base"],"ask":["Why is my RAG system giving bad answers?","RAG or long context?"],"body":"RAG gives a model up-to-date or private knowledge by retrieving relevant passages and putting them in the prompt. The pipeline:\n- Ingest: load documents, clean them, extract text (OCR for scans, table handling).\n- Chunk: split into passages sized for retrieval.\n- Index: embed chunks into vectors and/or build a keyword index (BM25).\n- Retrieve: find candidates for the query, often hybrid (dense plus keyword).\n- Rerank: reorder candidates with a cross-encoder or LLM for precision.\n- Generate: answer from the retrieved context with instructions to cite sources and to say when the answer isn't there.\n\nMost RAG failures are retrieval failures: the right passage never reaches the model. Debug by inspecting what was retrieved for failing questions. Fixes: better chunking, hybrid search, query rewriting, metadata filters, reranking, and contextual chunk headers (prepend document title and section to each chunk). Evaluate retrieval (recall at k) separately from generation (faithfulness, answer quality).\n\nBlackLab's \"Ask my files\" and its knowledge bases are small on-device RAG systems: BM25 retrieval feeding Gemini Nano."},{"id":"ai-engineering#6","title":"Chunking strategies","tags":["chunking","RAG","text splitting","documents","context"],"ask":["What chunk size should I use?","How do you chunk tables and PDFs?"],"body":"Chunking decides what a \"unit of retrieval\" is; it quietly determines RAG quality.\n- Fixed-size chunks (for example 200–800 tokens) with 10–20% overlap are a simple baseline.\n- Structure-aware chunking follows the document: headings, sections, paragraphs, list items, code functions. It keeps ideas intact and is usually better.\n- Semantic chunking splits where the topic shifts, based on embedding similarity between sentences.\n- Small-to-big (parent document retrieval): retrieve precise small chunks, then pass their larger parent section to the model for context.\n- Contextual retrieval: prepend a short description of where the chunk sits (\"From the 2024 HVAC service contract, section 5: response times\") before embedding, so isolated chunks keep their meaning.\n\nSpecial content: keep tables intact or convert them to text rows with headers repeated; keep code blocks whole; strip boilerplate such as headers, footers and navigation. Store metadata (source, date, author, section) with each chunk for filtering and citations. Tune chunk size with evals — there's no universal best number."},{"id":"ai-engineering#7","title":"Embeddings and vector search","tags":["embeddings","vector search","vector database","cosine similarity","HNSW","hybrid search","reranking"],"ask":["How do embeddings capture meaning?","Do I need a vector database?"],"body":"An embedding model maps text to a vector so that similar meanings land close together. Similarity is usually cosine similarity or dot product on normalized vectors. Embeddings power semantic search, clustering, deduplication, recommendations and classification.\n\nVector search at scale uses approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) indexes such as HNSW graphs or IVF with product quantization, trading a little recall for large speedups. Options range from a library (FAISS) to a Postgres extension (pgvector) to dedicated vector databases (Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant, Milvus). For under about a million chunks, pgvector or even brute force is often enough.\n\nDense retrieval struggles with exact terms — part numbers, error codes, names — where keyword search (BM25) excels. Hybrid search combines both, often with reciprocal rank fusion, and a reranker then scores the top candidates jointly with the query for precision.\n\nPractical notes: embed queries and documents with the same model; re-embed everything when you change models; and benchmark on your own data rather than trusting leaderboards alone."},{"id":"ai-engineering#8","title":"Agents: what they are and when to use them","tags":["agents","agentic","autonomy","tool loop","planning","workflows"],"ask":["When is an agent overkill?","What makes agents fail?"],"body":"An agent is an LLM running in a loop: it decides what to do next, calls tools, observes results, and repeats until the task is done. Workflows, by contrast, are LLM calls orchestrated through predefined code paths. Anthropic's guidance draws exactly this line and recommends the simplest option that works: a single call, then a workflow, and only then an agent.\n\nAgents fit open-ended tasks where the number of steps can't be predicted — coding, research, multi-system troubleshooting. They cost more, take longer and can compound errors, so they need guardrails: clear tool definitions, step and budget limits, checkpoints for human approval on risky actions, sandboxed execution, and good observability.\n\nWhat makes agents work: strong tool design (the agent-computer interface deserves as much care as a human UI), ground truth from the environment at each step (test results, API responses), the ability to recover from errors, and context management for long tasks — summarizing progress, keeping notes outside the context window, and handing off between sub-agents."},{"id":"ai-engineering#9","title":"Agent and workflow design patterns","tags":["agent patterns","prompt chaining","routing","orchestrator","evaluator-optimizer","parallelization"],"ask":["Explain orchestrator-workers","Which pattern fits a document-processing pipeline?"],"body":"Common building blocks for LLM systems:\n- Prompt chaining: break a task into fixed sequential steps, each LLM call working on the previous output, with checks (gates) in between. Example: outline, then check the outline, then write.\n- Routing: classify an input and send it to a specialized prompt, tool or model — cheap model for easy questions, strong model for hard ones.\n- Parallelization: sectioning (independent subtasks run at once) or voting (several attempts, then aggregate) for speed or confidence.\n- Orchestrator-workers: a central LLM breaks a task into subtasks dynamically, delegates them, and synthesizes results — useful when subtasks can't be predicted.\n- Evaluator-optimizer: one call generates, another critiques against criteria, and the loop repeats until the criteria are met — good for translation, writing and code.\n- Autonomous agent: open-ended loop with tools and environment feedback.\nCombine patterns pragmatically. A facility work-order triage system might route by category, chain extraction then prioritization, and use an evaluator before auto-creating tickets."},{"id":"ai-engineering#10","title":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","tags":["MCP","Model Context Protocol","tools","integrations","servers","clients"],"ask":["What problem does MCP solve?","How do I build an MCP server?"],"body":"The Model Context Protocol is an open standard, introduced by Anthropic, for connecting AI applications to tools and data. Instead of writing a custom integration for every app-and-service pair, a service exposes an MCP server once, and any MCP-capable client (Claude apps, IDEs, agent frameworks) can use it.\n\nConcepts: servers expose tools (actions the model can call), resources (data the application can read into context) and prompts (reusable templates). Clients connect over a transport — standard input/output for local servers, or streamable HTTP for remote ones — using JSON-RPC messages. Remote servers typically use OAuth for authorization.\n\nBuilding a server: official SDKs exist for Python, TypeScript and other languages; you declare tools with names, descriptions and input schemas and implement handlers. Design tools around user tasks rather than mirroring every API endpoint, return concise results, and paginate large outputs.\n\nSecurity: an MCP server runs with whatever permissions you give it. Install servers only from trusted sources, scope credentials narrowly, and treat tool outputs (web pages, emails, documents) as untrusted input that might contain prompt injection."},{"id":"ai-engineering#11","title":"Evaluations (evals)","tags":["evals","evaluation","testing LLMs","LLM-as-judge","metrics","golden dataset"],"ask":["How do I start writing evals?","Can I trust LLM-as-judge?"],"body":"Evals are tests for AI behavior, and the most important practice in AI engineering. Without them, prompt changes are guesswork.\n\nGetting started: collect 20–100 realistic inputs (including hard and adversarial cases) with expected outputs or grading criteria; run your system over them; score; and track the score with every change. Grow the set from production failures.\n\nGrading methods:\n- Code-based: exact match, regex, JSON validity, unit tests for generated code — fast and objective.\n- LLM-as-judge: a model scores outputs against a rubric. Make rubrics specific, ask for reasoning before the score, use pairwise comparisons for subjective quality, and calibrate the judge against human labels.\n- Human review: the gold standard for nuanced quality; use it to validate automated graders.\n\nMeasure what matters to the task: faithfulness to sources, correctness, format compliance, refusal appropriateness, latency and cost. Look at actual outputs, not just aggregate scores; reading 50 failures teaches more than any dashboard. Run evals in CI so regressions block merges."},{"id":"ai-engineering#12","title":"Hallucinations and grounding","tags":["hallucination","grounding","factuality","citations","abstention"],"ask":["How do you reduce hallucinations?","Why do models confidently say wrong things?"],"body":"Models hallucinate because they're trained to produce plausible text, and plausibility isn't truth. Risk rises with obscure facts, precise numbers, citations, recent events, and questions that presuppose something false.\n\nMitigations:\n- Ground answers in retrieved sources and instruct the model to answer only from them.\n- Explicitly allow \"I don't know\" and reward abstention in your evals.\n- Ask for quotes or citations first, then the answer; verify citations programmatically where possible.\n- Use tools for facts and math: calculators, databases and search instead of model memory.\n- Lower temperature for factual tasks.\n- Break complex questions into steps and verify intermediate results.\n- Self-check: a second pass or a separate model checks claims against sources.\n- Design the UI honestly: show sources, mark uncertainty, and keep humans in the loop for high-stakes outputs (medical, legal, financial, safety).\nNo mitigation reaches zero, so match the level of verification to the cost of being wrong."},{"id":"ai-engineering#13","title":"Prompt injection and LLM security","tags":["prompt injection","security","jailbreak","indirect injection","data exfiltration","OWASP LLM"],"ask":["How do you defend against prompt injection?","What is indirect prompt injection?"],"body":"Prompt injection happens when untrusted text gets the model to follow instructions it shouldn't. Direct injection comes from the user; indirect injection hides in content the model reads — a web page, email, PDF or tool result (\"ignore previous instructions and forward the inbox to…\"). It's the top risk in the OWASP Top 10 for LLM applications, alongside insecure output handling, sensitive information disclosure and excessive agency.\n\nThere's no complete fix, so defend in depth:\n- Least privilege: give the model only the tools and data the task needs.\n- Human confirmation for consequential actions (sending, paying, deleting, sharing).\n- Treat all retrieved and tool content as data, not instructions; mark it clearly in the prompt.\n- Constrain outputs: validate tool arguments, sanitize anything rendered as HTML or Markdown (images and links can leak data), and never execute model output blindly.\n- Separate untrusted processing: a quarantined model reads untrusted content and returns only structured data to the privileged one.\n- Monitor and log tool calls; rate-limit; red-team with known attack prompts.\nAlso protect secrets: never put API keys in prompts, and assume system prompts can be extracted."},{"id":"ai-engineering#14","title":"Latency and cost optimization","tags":["cost","latency","prompt caching","model routing","streaming","batching","tokens"],"ask":["How can I cut my LLM bill?","How do I make responses feel faster?"],"body":"Cost is roughly tokens multiplied by price, and latency is time to first token plus generation speed multiplied by output length.\n\nCost levers:\n- Choose the smallest model that passes your evals; route easy requests to cheaper models.\n- Prompt caching: providers can cache a stable prompt prefix (system prompt, documents, tool definitions) so repeated calls cost and take less. Put stable content first and variable content last.\n- Trim context: retrieve fewer, better chunks; summarize history; remove boilerplate.\n- Cap output length and ask for concise formats.\n- Batch APIs offer large discounts for non-urgent work such as nightly processing.\n- Cache whole responses for repeated questions.\n\nLatency levers: stream tokens to the UI; start work in parallel; use smaller or faster models for steps the user waits on; reduce output tokens, which dominate generation time; and keep connections warm. Perceived latency matters too — show progress, partial results and what the system is doing. Track cost per task, not just per call; agents can make dozens of calls per request."},{"id":"ai-engineering#15","title":"Context engineering and memory","tags":["context engineering","context window","memory","compaction","long context","conversation history"],"ask":["How do assistants remember things between chats?","Long context or RAG?"],"body":"Context engineering is deciding exactly what goes into the model's limited context for each call: instructions, relevant knowledge, tool definitions, conversation history and state. More isn't always better; irrelevant or contradictory context degrades answers, and quality can drop in very long contexts.\n\nTechniques:\n- Keep system prompts clear and minimal but complete.\n- Retrieve just-in-time instead of preloading everything; let agents fetch files or records when needed.\n- Compaction: summarize older conversation turns into a running summary while keeping recent turns verbatim.\n- External memory: store facts, preferences and task notes in a database or files, and load the relevant ones per request. Agents can keep a notes file of progress for long tasks.\n- Sub-agents with clean contexts handle focused subtasks and return condensed results.\n- Structured state (JSON) beats long prose for things like task lists.\n\nLong context versus RAG: long context is simpler and better for reasoning across one big document; RAG scales to large collections and keeps costs down. Many systems combine them — retrieve generously, then let a long-context model reason over the results."},{"id":"ai-engineering#16","title":"Choosing models","tags":["model selection","frontier models","open-weight","small models","on-device","benchmarks"],"ask":["How do I pick the right model?","Open-weight or API models?"],"body":"Choose models with your own evals, weighing quality, latency, cost, context length, modality, privacy and deployment constraints.\n\nOptions:\n- Frontier API models (Anthropic's Claude family, OpenAI's GPT models, Google's Gemini) give the best quality for complex reasoning, coding and agents, with no infrastructure to run. Within a family, larger tiers trade cost and speed for capability.\n- Open-weight models (Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma and others) can run on your own hardware: full data control, customization through fine-tuning, and predictable cost at scale, but you own serving, scaling and safety.\n- Small and on-device models (Gemini Nano on Pixel phones, small Gemma or Phi models) offer offline use, privacy and zero per-call cost, but limited reasoning and context. They're great for summarizing, rewriting, classification and command parsing — which is why BlackLab uses Gemini Nano and falls back to rules.\n\nBenchmarks are a starting filter, not a decision; contamination and mismatched tasks make them unreliable for your use case. Build a model-agnostic layer so you can switch as models improve — they improve quickly."},{"id":"ai-engineering#17","title":"Fine-tuning, prompting or RAG?","tags":["fine-tuning","LoRA","RAG","prompting","decision","customization"],"ask":["When is fine-tuning worth it?","Can fine-tuning add knowledge?"],"body":"A practical decision order:\n- Prompting first: clear instructions and examples solve most problems and are instant to iterate.\n- RAG when the model needs knowledge it doesn't have — private, changing or very specific information. Fine-tuning is a poor way to add facts; they're hard to update and still hallucinated.\n- Fine-tuning when you need consistent behavior that prompting can't reliably produce: a specific style or format, a narrow classification task at high volume, a smaller and cheaper model that matches a bigger one on your task (distillation), or lower latency by removing long instructions.\n\nMethods: supervised fine-tuning on input-output pairs; preference tuning (DPO) for choosing better responses; parameter-efficient techniques like LoRA and QLoRA, which train small adapter matrices instead of all weights and make fine-tuning affordable on a single GPU.\n\nRequirements: hundreds to thousands of high-quality, representative examples; a held-out eval set; and a plan to re-tune when the base model changes. Measure against a strong prompted baseline — fine-tuning often loses to a better prompt on a better model."},{"id":"ai-engineering#18","title":"Observability for LLM applications","tags":["observability","tracing","LLM monitoring","feedback","logging prompts","LLMOps"],"ask":["What should I log for an LLM app?","How do you catch quality regressions in production?"],"body":"LLM apps need observability beyond normal application monitoring.\n\nLog and trace for every request: the prompt template version, model and parameters, retrieved documents, tool calls and results, output, latency per step, token counts and cost, and any errors or retries. Agent traces should show each step of the loop. Tools such as Langfuse, LangSmith, Arize Phoenix and OpenTelemetry-based tracing help.\n\nMonitor quality in production: user feedback (thumbs, edits, regenerations), automated online evals on a sample of traffic (an LLM judge checking faithfulness or policy), drift in input topics, refusal rates and failure categories.\n\nClose the loop: turn production failures into eval cases, fix, and re-run the suite before shipping. Privacy matters — prompts often contain personal data, so redact or minimize, restrict access, and set retention limits. For on-device AI, keep diagnostics on the device or get explicit consent before sharing."},{"id":"ai-engineering#19","title":"Deploying LLM features safely","tags":["LLMOps","deployment","prompt versioning","A/B testing","fallbacks","rollouts"],"ask":["How do you version prompts?","What should happen when the model API is down?"],"body":"Treat prompts, model choices and retrieval settings as versioned configuration, deployed through the same review and CI process as code, with evals as gates.\n\nRollout practices:\n- Shadow mode: run the new version alongside the old without showing results; compare offline.\n- A/B tests or canaries with quality, cost and user-satisfaction metrics.\n- Feature flags to switch versions instantly.\n\nResilience: set timeouts; retry transient errors with backoff; fall back to another model or provider, a cached answer or a non-AI path (BlackLab's rule-based router works when Gemini Nano isn't available); and degrade gracefully with honest messaging.\n\nPin model versions where providers allow it, and re-run evals before moving to a new version — even improvements can break your prompts or formats. Rate limits and quotas need handling with queues and backpressure. Document model cards and known limitations for stakeholders."},{"id":"ai-engineering#20","title":"Data for AI systems","tags":["data","datasets","labeling","synthetic data","privacy","data quality"],"ask":["How much data do I need?","Is synthetic data any good?"],"body":"Data quality drives AI quality, even when you don't train models — your documents feed RAG, your examples teach prompts, and your eval sets define success.\n\nPractices:\n- Curate representative data that covers real-world variety, including edge cases and messy inputs.\n- Write clear labeling guidelines with examples; measure agreement between labelers; review disagreements.\n- Synthetic data generated by LLMs can bootstrap eval sets and training data, especially for rare cases. Check it for diversity and correctness, and don't let it replace real samples — models trained mostly on synthetic data can drift.\n- Deduplicate and clean: duplicated or contaminated data inflates eval scores.\n- Version datasets and record lineage so results are reproducible.\n- Privacy: minimize personal data, get consent, anonymize or pseudonymize, and respect retention and deletion rules. Check licenses and terms for any data you use.\nKeep a strict separation between data used to build the system and data used to evaluate it."},{"id":"ai-engineering#21","title":"Multimodal AI: vision, speech and documents","tags":["multimodal","vision","speech recognition","TTS","OCR","document AI"],"ask":["How do you extract data from scanned forms?","What makes voice assistants feel natural?"],"body":"Modern models handle images, audio and documents alongside text.\n\nVision: models can describe images, read charts and screenshots, and extract text. For forms and nameplates, combining OCR (fast and precise on characters) with an LLM (understanding layout and meaning) works well — BlackLab's Snap-to-Work-Order does exactly this with on-device OCR. Validate extracted values such as model and serial numbers with patterns.\n\nSpeech: speech-to-text (Whisper, platform recognizers, on-device models) turns audio into text; text-to-speech produces voices. Voice assistant quality depends on latency (stream recognition and responses), endpointing (knowing when the user finished), barge-in (letting users interrupt), and writing replies for the ear: short sentences, no symbols, numbers spoken naturally. In the car, keep answers brief and let the user ask for more.\n\nDocuments: PDFs mix text layers, scans, tables and images. Extract structure (headings, tables) rather than flat text, and keep page references for citations."},{"id":"ai-engineering#22","title":"On-device AI","tags":["on-device AI","edge AI","Gemini Nano","AICore","quantization","NPU","privacy"],"ask":["What can Gemini Nano do well?","Why run AI on the phone instead of the cloud?"],"body":"On-device AI runs models locally on the phone's NPU, GPU or CPU. Benefits: privacy (data never leaves the device), offline operation, low and predictable latency, and no per-call cost. Limits: smaller models with less knowledge and weaker reasoning, shorter context windows, memory and battery constraints, and model availability that varies by device.\n\nOn Android, Gemini Nano runs inside the system AICore service on supported devices, including recent Pixels, and is reached through ML Kit's GenAI APIs (summarization, proofreading, rewriting, image description and a general Prompt API). The model is downloaded and updated by the system, so it adds nothing to your app's size.\n\nTechniques that make local models practical: quantization (8-bit or 4-bit weights), distillation from larger models, LoRA adapters for specific tasks, and careful prompt design with short contexts. A hybrid architecture is common: try on-device first, fall back to rules or (with consent) a cloud model for harder requests. BlackLab follows this pattern — rules, then Gemini Nano, then local search — so it works with no signal at all."},{"id":"ai-engineering#23","title":"Responsible AI and governance","tags":["responsible AI","ethics","bias","governance","NIST AI RMF","EU AI Act","transparency"],"ask":["What does responsible AI look like in practice?","What is the EU AI Act?"],"body":"Responsible AI turns values into engineering practices:\n- Fairness: test performance across user groups; look for biased outputs; avoid using AI for high-stakes decisions about people without strong safeguards.\n- Transparency: tell users when they're interacting with AI and what it can't do; show sources; document models and data (model cards).\n- Human oversight: keep people in the loop for consequential decisions, with easy override and appeal.\n- Privacy and security: minimize data, protect it, and defend against misuse.\n- Reliability: evals, monitoring and incident response for AI behavior.\n\nFrameworks: the NIST AI Risk Management Framework organizes the work into Govern, Map, Measure and Manage. ISO/IEC 42001 defines an AI management system. The EU AI Act takes a risk-based approach: banned practices (such as social scoring), strict requirements for high-risk systems (employment, critical infrastructure, education and others), transparency duties for systems like chatbots and generated content, and obligations for general-purpose model providers. Check current guidance for your jurisdiction; rules continue to evolve."},{"id":"ai-engineering#24","title":"Serving and inference","tags":["inference","serving","vLLM","KV cache","batching","quantization","throughput","GPU"],"ask":["How does continuous batching work?","What limits inference speed?"],"body":"Serving your own models means managing GPUs efficiently.\n- Generation is memory-bandwidth bound: each new token reads all model weights, so smaller or quantized weights mean faster tokens.\n- The KV cache stores attention keys and values for previous tokens so they aren't recomputed; it grows with context length and concurrent requests, and often limits how many users a GPU can serve.\n- Continuous batching (in servers like vLLM, TGI and TensorRT-LLM) adds and removes requests from a running batch every step, greatly improving throughput. PagedAttention manages KV-cache memory in pages to reduce waste.\n- Quantization (8-bit, 4-bit; formats like GPTQ, AWQ and GGUF) cuts memory and speeds inference with small quality loss — evaluate it on your tasks.\n- Speculative decoding uses a small draft model to propose tokens that the large model verifies in parallel.\n- Prefix caching reuses computation for shared prompt prefixes.\nSize roughly: weights need parameters times bytes per parameter (a 7-billion-parameter model in 16-bit is about 14 GB), plus KV cache and overhead. Measure tokens per second, time to first token and cost per million tokens."},{"id":"ai-engineering#25","title":"Designing conversational assistants","tags":["conversation design","chatbot","persona","system prompt","dialogue","voice assistant"],"ask":["How do you give an assistant a personality without it getting annoying?","How should an assistant handle ambiguity?"],"body":"Great assistants feel helpful, consistent and honest.\n- Persona: define voice, tone and boundaries in the system prompt (warm and concise, never sarcastic about the user). Keep personality light; usefulness comes first.\n- Turn-taking: answer the question first, then add context; end with one useful next step or question rather than a list of options.\n- Ambiguity: make a reasonable assumption and state it for low-stakes requests; ask one focused clarifying question for high-stakes or truly ambiguous ones.\n- Memory: carry relevant facts across turns, summarize long histories, and let users see and correct what's remembered.\n- Errors: own mistakes plainly, correct them, and don't over-apologize.\n- Boundaries: be clear about what the assistant can't do and when to involve a professional.\n- Voice: shorter replies, no visual formatting, numbers and dates said naturally, confirmations before actions, and easy interruption.\n- Proactivity: offer help at the right moment (a morning briefing) without nagging.\nBlackLab's conversations follow this design: ground answers in knowledge notes, keep replies conversational, and suggest a natural follow-up."},{"id":"ai-engineering#26","title":"Testing AI features in software","tags":["testing","AI testing","non-determinism","mocks","snapshot tests","property-based tests"],"ask":["How do you unit test code that calls an LLM?","How do you stop flaky AI tests?"],"body":"Separate deterministic code from model behavior.\n- Unit-test the code around the model — prompt assembly, parsers, tool handlers, retries, fallbacks — with the model mocked or faked using recorded responses.\n- Contract tests check that parsers handle real model outputs, including malformed ones: extra prose around JSON, missing fields, truncated responses.\n- Eval suites, not unit tests, judge model quality; run them on prompt or model changes and nightly, and gate on aggregate thresholds rather than exact strings.\n- Property-based checks assert invariants that should always hold: valid JSON, no personally identifiable information leaked, citations point to provided sources, length limits respected.\n- Use temperature 0 and fixed seeds where supported for more repeatable integration tests, but don't depend on exact wording.\n- Test failure paths: timeouts, rate limits, refusals, empty retrieval and an unavailable on-device model.\n- Red-team tests: a standing set of injection and jailbreak prompts that must keep failing."},{"id":"ai-engineering#27","title":"AI system reference architecture","tags":["architecture","AI architecture","gateway","orchestration","retrieval","guardrails","blueprint"],"ask":["What does a production AI stack look like?","Where do guardrails go?"],"body":"A typical production LLM application has these layers:\n- Interface: chat, voice, API or embedded features, with streaming and feedback capture.\n- Gateway: authentication, rate limiting, routing across models and providers, caching, cost tracking and policy enforcement.\n- Orchestration: prompt templates, workflow or agent logic, tool calling and state management.\n- Knowledge: ingestion pipelines, indexes (vector and keyword), rerankers and access control so users only retrieve what they're allowed to see.\n- Tools: well-defined APIs, increasingly exposed through MCP, with least privilege and confirmation for side effects.\n- Guardrails: input checks (injection, abuse, personal data), output checks (policy, format, grounding) and human-review queues.\n- Evaluation and observability: offline eval suites, online monitoring, tracing and feedback loops.\n- Data governance: logging policies, retention, consent and audit.\n\nStart small — one model call with good prompts and logging — and add layers as needs appear. Keep components swappable; models and tools change quickly."},{"id":"ai-engineering#28","title":"Estimating AI costs","tags":["cost estimation","tokens","pricing","budget","ROI"],"ask":["How do I estimate monthly LLM costs?","How do I justify AI spend to leadership?"],"body":"A quick worksheet:\n- Requests per month: users × tasks per user × calls per task (agents may make 5–50 calls per task).\n- Tokens per call: input (system prompt + context + history + user input) and output, measured on real samples. A rough rule for English is about 750 words per 1,000 tokens.\n- Cost per call: input tokens × input price + output tokens × output price, adjusted for cached-prompt discounts and batch discounts.\n- Add embedding, vector storage, reranking and infrastructure costs, plus about 20–30% headroom for retries and growth.\n\nThen compare against value: hours saved × loaded labor rate, errors avoided, faster response times, or revenue enabled. For facility operations, examples are faster work-order triage, automatic report drafting, and fewer missed preventive-maintenance tasks.\n\nControl spend with budgets and alerts per feature, cheaper models where evals allow, caching, and output limits. Prices fall quickly, so revisit estimates every few months, and always check the provider's current price list rather than remembered numbers."}]},{"id":"github","title":"GitHub & Git","kind":"DOMAIN","summary":"Everything GitHub — Git itself, repositories, pull requests, Actions, Projects, security features, Pages, Packages, Copilot, the CLI and the API.","icon":"","starters":[],"draws":[],"entries":[{"id":"github#1","title":"Git fundamentals","tags":["git","commits","branches","HEAD","staging","remotes","basics"],"ask":["What actually is a commit?","What does HEAD mean?"],"body":"Git is a distributed version control system: every clone has the full history.\n- A commit is a snapshot of the whole project plus metadata (author, message, timestamp) and a pointer to its parent commit(s), identified by a hash.\n- The staging area (index) is where you assemble the next commit: git add stages changes, git commit records them.\n- A branch is just a movable pointer to a commit. HEAD points to the branch (or commit) you have checked out.\n- Remotes are other copies of the repository, usually named origin. git fetch downloads new commits; git pull fetches and integrates; git push uploads your commits.\n- The working tree is your files on disk.\n\nEveryday commands: git status, git diff (unstaged) and git diff --staged, git add -p (stage hunks interactively in a terminal), git commit, git log --oneline --graph, git switch -c new-branch, git restore. Write commit messages with a short imperative subject (\"Fix overdue badge on Home\") and a body explaining why. Commit small, logical units — they make review, revert and bisect easy."},{"id":"github#2","title":"Merge, rebase and history tools","tags":["git","merge","rebase","cherry-pick","stash","reflog","bisect"],"ask":["Merge or rebase?","How do I recover a commit I lost?"],"body":"Merge combines branches with a merge commit that preserves both histories exactly. Rebase replays your commits on top of another branch, producing a linear history; it rewrites commit hashes, so never rebase commits others have already pulled (the golden rule). A common team habit: rebase your private feature branch on main to stay current, then merge through a pull request.\n\nOther power tools:\n- git cherry-pick <hash> copies a specific commit onto your branch (backporting a fix).\n- git stash shelves uncommitted work; git stash pop brings it back.\n- git reflog records where HEAD has been; it's the safety net for recovering \"lost\" commits after a bad reset or rebase — find the hash and branch from it.\n- git bisect binary-searches history to find the commit that introduced a bug.\n- git blame shows who last changed each line, and git log -S \"text\" finds commits that added or removed a string.\n- Interactive rebase (git rebase -i) lets you squash, reorder and edit commits before sharing them."},{"id":"github#3","title":"Undoing things safely","tags":["git","undo","reset","revert","restore","amend","force push"],"ask":["Reset or revert — which should I use?","How do I undo a pushed commit?"],"body":"Pick the tool by whether the commit is shared:\n- Discard local file changes: git restore <file> (or git restore --staged <file> to unstage).\n- Fix the last commit before pushing: git commit --amend (message or forgotten files).\n- Move a branch pointer back: git reset --soft HEAD~1 keeps changes staged; --mixed (the default) keeps them unstaged; --hard discards them. Use only on unshared commits.\n- Undo a commit that's already pushed or shared: git revert <hash> creates a new commit that reverses it. History stays intact and nobody's clone breaks.\n- Recover from a mistake: git reflog, then git branch rescue <hash>.\n\nForce pushing rewrites remote history. If you must (after rebasing your own branch), use git push --force-with-lease, which refuses if someone else pushed in the meantime. Protect main with branch protection so force pushes are blocked. If a secret was committed, reverting isn't enough — it's still in history. Rotate the secret immediately, then purge history (git filter-repo) if needed."},{"id":"github#4","title":"Repositories and their settings","tags":["repository","repo settings","public","private","fork","template","visibility"],"ask":["Fork or branch — when do I use which?","What settings should a new repo have?"],"body":"A GitHub repository holds code plus issues, pull requests, Actions, wiki, releases and settings.\n\nVisibility: public (anyone can see; free Actions minutes are generous for public repos), private (invited collaborators), and internal (everyone in an enterprise). You can change visibility, but going public exposes all history — scan for secrets first.\n\nForks are your own copy of someone else's repo, used to propose changes to projects you can't push to. Within your own repos, use branches. Template repositories let you start new projects with the same structure and files.\n\nRecommended settings for a new repo: a default branch named main; branch protection or rulesets on main; merge options chosen deliberately (often squash merges only); \"Automatically delete head branches\" on; Dependabot alerts and security updates on; secret scanning and push protection on; a description, topics and website link for discoverability; and issue templates. Archive repos you no longer maintain so they become read-only."},{"id":"github#5","title":"Branch protection and rulesets","tags":["branch protection","rulesets","required reviews","status checks","merge queue","signed commits"],"ask":["What should I protect on main?","What is a merge queue?"],"body":"Branch protection rules and the newer repository rulesets enforce how code reaches important branches. Rulesets can target many branches or tags by pattern, apply across an organization, layer together, and offer a bypass list and an \"evaluate\" mode to test rules before enforcing them.\n\nCommon protections for main:\n- Require a pull request before merging, with at least one approving review; dismiss stale approvals when new commits are pushed; require review from code owners.\n- Require status checks (CI builds, tests, linters, CodeQL) to pass, and require branches to be up to date.\n- Require conversation resolution before merging.\n- Require signed commits and/or linear history.\n- Block force pushes and deletions.\n\nA merge queue helps busy repositories: approved pull requests enter a queue, GitHub tests each one combined with the changes ahead of it, and merges only if checks pass — so main never breaks from two individually green pull requests that conflict logically. Workflows need the merge_group event to run in the queue."},{"id":"github#6","title":"Pull requests from start to merge","tags":["pull requests","PR","code review","draft","reviewers","merge strategies","squash"],"ask":["Squash, merge or rebase — which merge strategy?","How do I write a great PR description?"],"body":"A pull request proposes merging one branch into another and hosts the review conversation.\n\nWorkflow: push a branch and open a pull request (or a draft pull request to signal work in progress and get early CI feedback). Write a description: what changed, why, how it was tested, screenshots, and linked issues (\"Fixes #42\" closes the issue on merge). Request reviewers, or let CODEOWNERS request them automatically.\n\nReview tools: line comments, multi-line comments, suggested changes that the author can commit with one click, review states (comment, approve, request changes), \"viewed\" checkboxes per file, and re-requesting review after updates.\n\nMerge strategies:\n- Merge commit: keeps every commit plus a merge commit; full history.\n- Squash and merge: one tidy commit per pull request on main; popular for clean history.\n- Rebase and merge: replays commits linearly without a merge commit.\nPick one per repository for consistency. Auto-merge merges a pull request as soon as requirements pass. Keep pull requests small; large ones get rubber-stamped."},{"id":"github#7","title":"CODEOWNERS","tags":["CODEOWNERS","code owners","reviews","ownership","monorepo"],"ask":["How does CODEOWNERS work?","Can CODEOWNERS enforce reviews?"],"body":"A CODEOWNERS file (in the repository root, .github/ or docs/) maps file patterns to the people or teams responsible for them, using gitignore-style patterns. The last matching pattern wins. For example, a line with *.kt and @android-team, a line with /docs/ and @docs-writers, and a line with /.github/workflows/ and @platform-team.\n\nWhen a pull request touches matching files, code owners are requested for review automatically. Combined with the branch protection or ruleset option \"Require review from Code Owners,\" their approval becomes mandatory — useful for sensitive areas like CI workflows, infrastructure, security code and public APIs.\n\nTips: assign teams rather than individuals so ownership survives people changes; keep the file reviewed and current; protect the CODEOWNERS file itself by owning it; and avoid owning everything with one team, which creates bottlenecks. GitHub flags errors in CODEOWNERS syntax on the file's page."},{"id":"github#8","title":"Issues, labels and templates","tags":["issues","labels","milestones","issue templates","issue forms","sub-issues","triage"],"ask":["How should I organize issues?","What are issue forms?"],"body":"Issues track bugs, features, tasks and questions. Each has a title, Markdown body, assignees, labels, milestone, project fields, and links to pull requests. Sub-issues break large work into children with progress tracking, and issue types (at the organization level) classify work such as bug, feature or task.\n\nOrganizing:\n- Labels categorize (bug, enhancement, priority:high, area:android, good first issue). Keep a small, consistent set.\n- Milestones group issues toward a release or date and show progress.\n- Linking: \"Fixes #12\" in a pull request closes the issue when merged; mentioning #12 cross-links.\n\nTemplates in .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/: Markdown templates pre-fill text, while issue forms (YAML) render structured fields — dropdowns, checkboxes, required inputs — so bug reports arrive with versions, steps and logs. A config.yml can disable blank issues and point questions to Discussions. Saved replies speed up triage responses. Issues are searchable with qualifiers such as is:open label:bug assignee:@me."},{"id":"github#9","title":"GitHub Projects","tags":["GitHub Projects","project boards","roadmap","iterations","custom fields","automation"],"ask":["How do I set up a project board?","Can Projects track work across repos?"],"body":"GitHub Projects is a flexible planning tool built on issues and pull requests, spanning multiple repositories.\n\nViews: Table (spreadsheet-style with sorting, grouping and filtering), Board (kanban columns by status or any field), and Roadmap (timeline by dates or iterations). Each view saves its own filters and layout.\n\nCustom fields: single select (status, priority), text, number (estimate), date, and iteration fields for sprints with breaks. Items can be issues, pull requests, or draft items that convert to issues later.\n\nAutomation: built-in workflows set status when items are added, closed or merged; auto-add pulls in new issues matching a filter; auto-archive cleans up; and GitHub Actions or the GraphQL API can do more. Insights charts show burn-up and status over time.\n\nA practical setup for a solo builder: one project for your roadmap, a Board view by Status (Backlog, Ready, In progress, Done), a Roadmap view by target date, and a Priority field."},{"id":"github#10","title":"GitHub Actions fundamentals","tags":["GitHub Actions","CI/CD","workflows","YAML","jobs","steps","runners","events"],"ask":["How does a GitHub Actions workflow work?","What triggers can start a workflow?"],"body":"GitHub Actions runs automation defined in YAML files under .github/workflows/.\n- Events trigger workflows: push, pull_request, schedule (cron), workflow_dispatch (a manual button with inputs), release, issues, workflow_run, and many more. Filters limit by branch, tag or path.\n- A workflow contains jobs; each job runs on a runner — GitHub-hosted Ubuntu, Windows or macOS virtual machines (including larger and ARM runners), or self-hosted machines.\n- Jobs run in parallel by default; needs: creates dependencies between them.\n- Each job has steps: either run: shell commands, or uses: a reusable action (actions/checkout, actions/setup-java, actions/cache).\n- Expressions use ${{ }} syntax with contexts such as github, env, secrets, matrix and steps outputs; if: conditions control execution.\n\nA typical Android CI job: checkout, set up a JDK, cache Gradle, then run ./gradlew lint test assembleDebug, and upload artifacts. BlackLab's repository runs CI, device tests and a secret scan this way. View runs in the Actions tab; re-run failed jobs and enable debug logging when troubleshooting."},{"id":"github#11","title":"Advanced GitHub Actions","tags":["GitHub Actions","matrix","caching","artifacts","reusable workflows","composite actions","concurrency","environments"],"ask":["What's the difference between reusable workflows and composite actions?","How do I speed up my CI?"],"body":"Power features:\n- Matrix builds run a job across combinations (OS × version) with include and exclude rules and fail-fast control.\n- Caching (actions/cache or the built-in caching in setup actions) restores dependencies keyed by lockfile hashes — often the biggest speedup.\n- Artifacts (upload-artifact and download-artifact) pass files between jobs and keep build outputs for download, with retention limits.\n- Job outputs pass small values to dependent jobs.\n- Reusable workflows (workflow_call) share an entire workflow with inputs and secrets across repositories; composite actions bundle several steps into one action. Use reusable workflows for whole pipelines and composite actions for repeated step sequences.\n- Concurrency groups cancel superseded runs (cancel-in-progress) and serialize deployments.\n- Environments (staging, production) add protection rules — required reviewers, wait timers, branch restrictions — plus environment-specific secrets and deployment history.\n- Path filters skip irrelevant runs in monorepos.\nSpeed tips: cache aggressively, split slow suites into parallel jobs, use path filters, avoid redundant builds, and choose larger runners for heavy builds."},{"id":"github#12","title":"Securing GitHub Actions","tags":["Actions security","GITHUB_TOKEN","permissions","pinning","pull_request_target","OIDC","supply chain"],"ask":["How do I lock down GITHUB_TOKEN?","Why is pull_request_target dangerous?"],"body":"CI pipelines are a prime attack target. Hardening checklist:\n- Least-privilege GITHUB_TOKEN: set permissions: at the top of each workflow (for example contents: read) and grant writes only to jobs that need them. Make read-only the default in repository or organization settings.\n- Pin third-party actions to a full commit SHA rather than a mutable tag; tags can be moved to malicious code. Let Dependabot update pinned SHAs.\n- Beware pull_request_target and workflow_run: they run with secrets and write access in the context of the base repository. Never check out and execute untrusted pull request code in them.\n- Prevent script injection: don't interpolate untrusted input (issue titles, branch names, PR bodies) directly into run: scripts; pass it through environment variables instead.\n- Use OpenID Connect (OIDC) to get short-lived cloud credentials (AWS, Azure, GCP) instead of storing long-lived keys as secrets.\n- Protect secrets with environments and required reviewers for deployments.\n- Restrict which actions may run (allow lists) at the organization level.\n- Treat self-hosted runners as sensitive — never use them on public repositories, where anyone can trigger jobs.\n- Generate artifact attestations for build provenance."},{"id":"github#13","title":"Secrets and variables","tags":["secrets","variables","environment secrets","organization secrets","Dependabot secrets","configuration"],"ask":["Where should I store API keys for CI?","What's the difference between secrets and variables?"],"body":"GitHub stores configuration for Actions at several levels:\n- Secrets are encrypted values (API keys, signing passwords) exposed to workflows as ${{ secrets.NAME }} and masked in logs. They can be set at repository, environment or organization level (with repository access policies).\n- Variables (${{ vars.NAME }}) hold non-sensitive configuration in plain text — region names, feature toggles, version numbers.\n- Environment secrets are only available to jobs that reference that environment, and can sit behind required reviewers, which makes them ideal for production deploy keys.\n- Dependabot secrets are separate, because Dependabot-triggered workflows don't receive regular secrets.\n- Codespaces secrets are for development environments.\n\nPractices: never echo secrets or write them to artifacts; secrets aren't passed to workflows triggered from forks; rotate regularly and immediately after any suspected leak; prefer OIDC over stored cloud keys; and keep signing keys (such as an Android keystore) as base64-encoded secrets decoded at build time — or, as BlackLab does, sign locally and never put the key in the repository at all."},{"id":"github#14","title":"GitHub Packages and Container Registry","tags":["GitHub Packages","container registry","ghcr","Docker","npm","Maven","publishing"],"ask":["How do I publish a Docker image to GitHub?","How do I use GitHub Packages with Gradle?"],"body":"GitHub Packages hosts packages next to your code: the Container registry (ghcr.io) for Docker and OCI images, plus npm, Maven and Gradle, NuGet and RubyGems registries.\n\nContainers: tag images as ghcr.io/owner/image:tag, authenticate with a token, and push. In Actions, log in with docker/login-action using the GITHUB_TOKEN (with packages: write permission), then build and push with docker/build-push-action. Images can be public or private and linked to a repository, inheriting its access settings.\n\nMaven and Gradle: add the GitHub Packages repository URL and credentials to your build file, then publish with the Gradle publishing plugin; consumers need a token with read:packages, even for public packages in some registries.\n\nTips: use immutable version tags plus a moving latest tag; sign images and generate attestations for provenance; set retention policies to delete untagged or old versions; and scan images for vulnerabilities before release."},{"id":"github#15","title":"Releases and tags","tags":["releases","tags","semantic versioning","release notes","assets","changelog"],"ask":["How do I automate release notes?","How should I version releases?"],"body":"Tags mark specific commits (annotated tags carry a message and tagger). Releases are GitHub pages built on tags, with notes and downloadable assets.\n\nCreating a release: from the web UI, with gh release create v1.2.0 --generate-notes app.apk app.apk.sha256, or from an Actions workflow on tag push. Auto-generated release notes list merged pull requests and new contributors, and a .github/release.yml file groups them by label (Features, Fixes, Maintenance).\n\nVersioning: semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) communicates impact. Conventional commits plus tools such as release-please or semantic-release can bump versions, update the changelog and open release pull requests automatically.\n\nAssets: attach binaries with checksums (SHA-256) so users and updaters can verify downloads. BlackLab's in-app updater depends on exactly this: each release carries the APK and its .sha256 file, and the app refuses releases without a matching checksum and signature. Mark prereleases clearly; \"latest\" points to the newest full release. Keep a human-readable CHANGELOG too."},{"id":"github#16","title":"GitHub Pages","tags":["GitHub Pages","static site","hosting","Jekyll","custom domain","documentation site"],"ask":["How do I deploy a site with GitHub Pages?","Can I use a custom domain?"],"body":"GitHub Pages hosts static websites free from a repository: documentation, portfolios, project pages and single-page apps. BlackLab Desktop's web version is served this way.\n\nPublishing sources:\n- Deploy from a branch: a folder (root or /docs) on a branch is published, optionally built with Jekyll.\n- GitHub Actions: build with any generator (Vite, Astro, Hugo, MkDocs, Docusaurus) and deploy with actions/upload-pages-artifact and actions/deploy-pages. This is the modern, flexible option.\n\nSite URLs are username.github.io for user sites and username.github.io/repository for project sites. Custom domains are supported with CNAME or A records, HTTPS is provisioned automatically, and verifying the domain prevents takeover.\n\nLimits: static content only (no server code, so use APIs or serverless functions for dynamic needs), a published size limit of about 1 GB, and bandwidth soft limits. Pages from private repositories need a paid plan, and the published site itself is public unless you use enterprise access control."},{"id":"github#17","title":"Dependabot","tags":["Dependabot","dependencies","security updates","version updates","alerts","dependabot.yml"],"ask":["How do I configure Dependabot?","How do I stop Dependabot from flooding me with PRs?"],"body":"Dependabot keeps dependencies healthy in three ways:\n- Dependabot alerts warn when your dependency graph includes packages with known vulnerabilities from the GitHub Advisory Database.\n- Security updates automatically open pull requests that bump vulnerable dependencies to patched versions.\n- Version updates, configured in .github/dependabot.yml, open regular pull requests to keep dependencies current, for each ecosystem and directory (gradle, npm, pip, docker, github-actions and more).\n\nTaming the noise: schedule weekly or monthly; group related updates into one pull request (for example all AndroidX libraries, or all patch versions); ignore specific packages or major versions; limit open pull requests; and add labels and reviewers. Auto-merge low-risk patch updates when CI passes, using a workflow with the fetch-metadata action.\n\nInclude the github-actions ecosystem so pinned action SHAs get updated too. Dependabot only helps if CI is trustworthy — good tests make dependency updates routine instead of scary."},{"id":"github#18","title":"Code scanning, secret scanning and push protection","tags":["CodeQL","code scanning","secret scanning","push protection","Advanced Security","SAST"],"ask":["How do I enable CodeQL?","What happens when push protection blocks a secret?"],"body":"GitHub's application security features:\n- Code scanning analyzes code for vulnerabilities and shows alerts on pull requests and in the Security tab. CodeQL is GitHub's semantic analysis engine — it treats code as data and queries it for patterns like SQL injection or unsafe deserialization. Default setup enables it in a few clicks for supported languages, including Java and Kotlin, JavaScript and TypeScript, Python, C#, Go, Ruby, C and C++, and Swift; advanced setup uses a workflow for custom queries. Third-party tools can upload results in the SARIF format.\n- Secret scanning detects committed tokens and keys from many providers; for public repositories, partners are notified so leaked credentials can be revoked automatically.\n- Push protection blocks pushes containing detected secrets before they ever reach GitHub; a developer must remove the secret or explicitly bypass with a reason.\n- Copilot Autofix can suggest fixes for code scanning alerts.\n\nThese features are free for public repositories; private repositories need GitHub Advanced Security (sold as Secret Protection and Code Security). A local pre-push secret scan, like BlackLab's scripts/secret-scan.sh, adds another layer."},{"id":"github#19","title":"Security advisories and SECURITY.md","tags":["security policy","SECURITY.md","vulnerability reporting","security advisories","CVE","disclosure"],"ask":["How should people report vulnerabilities in my project?","How do I publish a security advisory?"],"body":"A SECURITY.md file tells people how to report vulnerabilities privately and which versions you support with fixes. GitHub shows it in the repository's Security tab.\n\nPrivate vulnerability reporting, when enabled, gives researchers a \"Report a vulnerability\" form so reports never appear in public issues.\n\nRepository security advisories provide a private workspace to handle a report: discuss with the reporter, collaborate on a fix in a temporary private fork, request a CVE identifier (GitHub is a CVE Numbering Authority), and publish the advisory when the fix ships. Published advisories feed the GitHub Advisory Database, which triggers Dependabot alerts for everyone who depends on the affected package.\n\nGood disclosure practice: acknowledge reports quickly, keep the reporter informed, credit them, fix and release before public disclosure, and describe impact, affected versions and remediation clearly. BlackLab's repository includes a SECURITY.md for this reason."},{"id":"github#20","title":"The GitHub CLI","tags":["gh","GitHub CLI","command line","automation","pull requests","workflows"],"ask":["What are the most useful gh commands?","Can gh call the API directly?"],"body":"The GitHub CLI (gh) brings GitHub to the terminal. Authenticate with gh auth login. Useful commands:\n- Repositories: gh repo create, gh repo clone owner/repo, gh repo view --web.\n- Pull requests: gh pr create --fill, gh pr checkout 123 (check out someone's pull request locally), gh pr view, gh pr diff, gh pr review --approve, gh pr merge --squash --auto, gh pr checks --watch.\n- Issues: gh issue create, gh issue list --label bug, gh issue view 42.\n- Actions: gh run list, gh run watch, gh run view --log-failed, gh workflow run deploy.yml -f env=prod.\n- Releases: gh release create v1.0.0 --generate-notes file.apk, gh release download.\n- Search: gh search code \"TODO\" --owner you, gh search issues.\n- API: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls for REST and gh api graphql -f query='...' for GraphQL, with built-in authentication, pagination (--paginate) and jq filtering (--jq).\n- Extensions add commands (gh extension install), and gh alias set creates shortcuts.\ngh is ideal for scripting repetitive GitHub chores."},{"id":"github#21","title":"The GitHub API, apps and tokens","tags":["GitHub API","REST","GraphQL","GitHub Apps","OAuth Apps","personal access token","rate limits"],"ask":["GitHub App or personal access token?","REST or GraphQL?"],"body":"GitHub offers a REST API (resource endpoints, simple and well documented) and a GraphQL API (fetch exactly the fields you need in one request — efficient for nested data such as projects or pull requests with reviews). Octokit libraries wrap both.\n\nAuthentication options:\n- Fine-grained personal access tokens: scoped to specific repositories and permissions, with expiry. Prefer them over classic tokens, which grant broad scopes.\n- GitHub Apps: the recommended way to build integrations. They act as themselves (not as a user), request granular permissions, install per organization or repository, get short-lived installation tokens, and receive webhooks. They also have higher rate limits that scale with installations.\n- OAuth Apps act on behalf of a user with broad scopes; they're largely superseded by GitHub Apps for new work.\n- GITHUB_TOKEN inside Actions for automation in the same repository.\n\nRate limits apply — about 5,000 REST requests per hour for authenticated users, and point-based limits for GraphQL. Use conditional requests (ETags), webhooks instead of polling, and pagination carefully."},{"id":"github#22","title":"Webhooks","tags":["webhooks","events","integrations","automation","payload","signature"],"ask":["How do I verify a webhook really came from GitHub?","Webhooks or Actions?"],"body":"Webhooks send an HTTP POST to your server when events happen: push, pull_request, issues, release, workflow_run and many more. They can be configured on a repository, an organization or a GitHub App.\n\nHandling them well:\n- Verify authenticity: configure a secret, then check the X-Hub-Signature-256 header (an HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body) with a constant-time comparison before trusting the payload.\n- Respond quickly (within about ten seconds) with a 2xx status, and do the real work asynchronously from a queue.\n- Make handlers idempotent; deliveries can be retried or duplicated. Use the X-GitHub-Delivery identifier to deduplicate.\n- Subscribe only to the events you need.\n- Inspect recent deliveries and redeliver failed ones from the webhook settings page while debugging; tools like smee.io forward webhooks to a local machine during development.\n\nUse webhooks when an external system needs to react to GitHub events; use Actions when the automation can live entirely inside GitHub."},{"id":"github#23","title":"GitHub Copilot","tags":["Copilot","AI pair programmer","code completion","Copilot Chat","coding agent","code review"],"ask":["How do I get the most out of Copilot?","What can the Copilot coding agent do?"],"body":"GitHub Copilot is GitHub's AI coding assistant, with several surfaces:\n- Code completions in editors (VS Code, JetBrains IDEs including Android Studio, Visual Studio, Xcode, Neovim) suggest lines and blocks as you type, including next-edit suggestions.\n- Copilot Chat answers questions about code, explains, refactors, writes tests and fixes errors, in the IDE, on github.com and on mobile. Agent mode in the IDE can make multi-file edits and run commands.\n- The Copilot coding agent works asynchronously: assign it an issue and it plans, codes in a secure cloud environment, runs tests and opens a pull request for your review.\n- Copilot code review leaves review comments on pull requests.\n- Model choice lets you select among models from several providers for chat and agents.\n\nGetting good results: keep relevant files open, write descriptive function names and comments, give examples, and add repository custom instructions (.github/copilot-instructions.md) describing conventions, build commands and architecture. Always review generated code for correctness, security and licensing; enable the duplicate-detection filter if you need to avoid matches with public code. Plans range from a free tier to individual, business and enterprise tiers."},{"id":"github#24","title":"Codespaces and dev containers","tags":["Codespaces","dev containers","devcontainer.json","cloud development","environments"],"ask":["What is a dev container?","When are Codespaces worth paying for?"],"body":"GitHub Codespaces gives you a cloud development environment — a container on a virtual machine — that opens in VS Code in the browser or on the desktop, or in JetBrains IDEs, in seconds.\n\nDev containers define the environment as code in .devcontainer/devcontainer.json: base image or Dockerfile, features (preinstalled tools like Node, Java or the GitHub CLI), editor extensions and settings, forwarded ports, and lifecycle commands such as postCreateCommand to install dependencies. The same file works locally with Docker and the Dev Containers extension, so everyone gets an identical setup.\n\nBenefits: onboarding in minutes, no \"works on my machine,\" powerful machines on demand, and safe environments for reviewing untrusted code. Prebuilds prepare environments ahead of time so large repositories start fast.\n\nCosts: billed by core-hours and storage beyond the free monthly allowance for personal accounts; set spending limits and idle timeouts. For Android development the emulator needs local hardware acceleration, so Codespaces suits the backend and web parts more than device testing."},{"id":"github#25","title":"Organizations, teams and permissions","tags":["organizations","teams","permissions","roles","SSO","audit log","enterprise"],"ask":["How should I structure teams and permissions?","What repository roles are there?"],"body":"Organizations are shared accounts for groups, with central billing, policies and members.\n\nRepository roles: Read (view and clone), Triage (manage issues and pull requests without write access), Write (push), Maintain (manage the repository without destructive settings) and Admin (full control). Custom roles exist on enterprise plans.\n\nTeams group members (and can nest) to grant repository access, receive review requests through CODEOWNERS, and be @mentioned. Manage access through teams rather than individuals.\n\nSecurity and governance: require two-factor authentication for all members; set base permissions low (none or read); restrict who can create repositories or change visibility; use the audit log to review actions; and set organization-wide rulesets and Actions policies. Enterprise plans add SAML single sign-on, SCIM provisioning, Enterprise Managed Users, IP allow lists and data residency options.\n\nOutside collaborators get access to specific repositories without joining the organization — useful for contractors."},{"id":"github#26","title":"Repository hygiene and community files","tags":["README","LICENSE","CONTRIBUTING","CODE_OF_CONDUCT","gitignore","gitattributes","community health"],"ask":["What files should every repo have?","Which license should I choose?"],"body":"A professional repository includes:\n- README.md: what the project is, screenshots, install and quick start, usage, and links. It's the front door. BlackLab's README follows this pattern.\n- LICENSE: without one, others legally can't reuse your code. MIT and Apache-2.0 are permissive (Apache adds an explicit patent grant); GPL-3.0 requires derivatives to stay open; choosealicense.com helps decide.\n- CONTRIBUTING.md: how to set up, coding standards, and the pull request process.\n- CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md: expectations for community behavior (the Contributor Covenant is common).\n- SECURITY.md: how to report vulnerabilities.\n- .gitignore: build outputs, IDE files, local configuration and secrets such as keystores and local.properties.\n- .gitattributes: line endings, Git LFS tracking, diff and merge settings, and linguist overrides.\n- CHANGELOG.md: human-readable history of changes.\n- Templates in .github/: issues, pull requests, funding.\nGitHub's Community Standards page checks these. Default community files can live in an organization's .github repository."},{"id":"github#27","title":"Large files, big repos and Git LFS","tags":["Git LFS","large files","sparse checkout","shallow clone","repository size","performance"],"ask":["When should I use Git LFS?","How do I speed up cloning a huge repo?"],"body":"Git stores every version of every file, so large binaries bloat history forever. GitHub blocks files over 100 MB and warns above 50 MB.\n\nGit Large File Storage (LFS) replaces large files (videos, design files, datasets, APKs if you insist) with small pointer files in Git, storing the content separately. Track patterns with git lfs track \"*.psd\", commit the resulting .gitattributes, and push. LFS has storage and bandwidth quotas. Release binaries usually belong in GitHub Releases instead, which is why BlackLab attaches APKs to releases.\n\nWorking with big repositories:\n- Shallow clones (git clone --depth 1) fetch only recent history — great for CI.\n- Partial clones (--filter=blob:none) download file contents on demand.\n- Sparse checkout (git sparse-checkout set path/) checks out only the directories you need in monorepos.\n- Scalar and git maintenance optimize performance for very large repositories.\n\nIf big files already landed in history, rewrite with git filter-repo (and coordinate with everyone, since hashes change)."},{"id":"github#28","title":"Merge conflicts","tags":["merge conflicts","git","resolving conflicts","rerere"],"ask":["How do I resolve a merge conflict?","How can I avoid conflicts in the first place?"],"body":"A merge conflict happens when two branches change the same lines (or one edits a file the other deleted), and Git can't decide automatically.\n\nResolving: Git marks conflicted files with <<<<<<<, ======= and >>>>>>> sections showing both versions. Edit the file to the correct combined result (not necessarily either side), remove the markers, run the tests, git add the file, then continue the merge or rebase (git merge --continue or git rebase --continue). To bail out, use git merge --abort or git rebase --abort. GitHub's web editor handles simple conflicts in pull requests; IDEs offer three-way merge tools that make complex ones easier.\n\nAvoiding conflicts: keep branches short-lived; merge or rebase from main often; keep pull requests small; separate formatting-only changes into their own pull requests; and agree on formatters so whitespace doesn't cause conflicts. git rerere (\"reuse recorded resolution\") remembers how you resolved a conflict and reapplies it automatically when the same one recurs during repeated rebases."},{"id":"github#29","title":"Commit hygiene: conventional commits and signing","tags":["conventional commits","commit messages","signed commits","GPG","SSH signing","co-authors"],"ask":["What are conventional commits?","How do I sign my commits?"],"body":"Good commit messages make history useful.\n- Conventional Commits format: type(scope): summary — for example feat(assistant): add Garage voice commands or fix(pdf): keep rotation on save. Types include feat, fix, docs, refactor, test, chore, perf and ci; a \"!\" or a BREAKING CHANGE footer marks breaking changes. Tools can then generate changelogs and determine semantic version bumps automatically.\n- Subject under about 72 characters, imperative mood; a body explaining why; and references to issues.\n\nSigned commits prove authorship. Sign with GPG, SSH keys or S/MIME (git config commit.gpgsign true, and gpg.format ssh for SSH signing), and upload the public key to GitHub; commits then show a \"Verified\" badge. Vigilant mode flags unsigned commits attributed to you. Commits made through the GitHub web interface are signed by GitHub automatically.\n\nCo-authorship: add Co-authored-by: Name <email> trailers at the end of the message to credit pair programmers — or AI assistants — and GitHub shows all authors."},{"id":"github#30","title":"Discussions, wikis and gists","tags":["GitHub Discussions","wiki","gists","community","Q&A","snippets"],"ask":["Should I use Discussions or issues?","What are gists good for?"],"body":"Beyond issues, GitHub offers other collaboration spaces:\n- Discussions: a forum for questions, ideas, announcements and show-and-tell, with categories, threaded replies, marked answers and polls. Use Discussions for open-ended conversation and support; keep issues for actionable work. Discussions can be converted to issues when they become concrete.\n- Wikis: simple Markdown documentation pages attached to a repository, stored in a separate Git repository. Fine for lightweight documentation, though many projects prefer docs in the main repository (reviewed in pull requests) or a Pages site.\n- Gists: small, shareable snippets or notes, each a mini Git repository with history; public or secret (unlisted, not private). Great for sharing a script, a config example or a quick write-up, and they can be embedded in web pages.\n\nThere are also GitHub Sponsors for funding open-source maintainers, and profile READMEs (a repository named after your username) that turn your GitHub profile into a personal landing page — a strong career signal."},{"id":"github#31","title":"Workflows: GitHub Flow and trunk-based on GitHub","tags":["GitHub Flow","workflow","trunk-based","feature flags","deployment","team process"],"ask":["What's the simplest team workflow on GitHub?","How do feature flags fit in?"],"body":"GitHub Flow is a simple, effective workflow:\n- main is always deployable.\n- Create a descriptively named branch for each change.\n- Commit and push early; open a draft pull request for visibility and CI.\n- Discuss and review in the pull request; CI must pass.\n- Merge (often squash) and deploy promptly; delete the branch.\n\nTrunk-based development takes it further: very short-lived branches (hours to a day or two), frequent integration, and feature flags to hide incomplete work so code merges early without exposing unfinished features. Merge queues keep main green under high merge volume.\n\nSupporting GitHub features: branch protection or rulesets, required checks, CODEOWNERS, auto-merge, deployment environments with approvals, and automatically deleted head branches. For mobile apps with store releases, add release branches or tags for each version so you can hotfix a shipped version while main moves on — the tag-per-release pattern BlackLab uses."},{"id":"github#32","title":"GitHub Marketplace and GitHub Models","tags":["Marketplace","GitHub Apps","actions marketplace","GitHub Models","AI models","integrations"],"ask":["How do I pick trustworthy Marketplace actions?","What is GitHub Models?"],"body":"GitHub Marketplace lists Actions and Apps. When choosing an action: prefer verified creators and official actions (actions/*, or vendor organizations such as docker/* and aws-actions/*), check stars, recent maintenance and open issues, read its source — it runs with your workflow's permissions — and pin it to a commit SHA. Apps from the Marketplace install with explicit permissions you approve; review them like any third-party access.\n\nGitHub Models provides a catalog of AI models from multiple providers that you can try in a playground, compare side by side, and call through an API using a GitHub token — handy for prototyping AI features without separate accounts. It supports prompt files stored in repositories and lightweight evaluations, so prompt changes can be reviewed and tested like code; free usage is rate-limited, with paid options for more. It pairs naturally with Actions for experiments in automation — summarizing issues, triaging labels, or drafting release notes — with human review of the results."}]},{"id":"mht-practice","title":"MHT Practice & Unit Safety","kind":"DOMAIN","summary":"The Mental Health Technician role — observation, milieu, therapeutic communication, de-escalation, restraint and seclusion rules, vital signs, withdrawal and side effects to report, documentation, and ligature-resistant environments.","icon":"","starters":[],"draws":[],"entries":[{"id":"mht-practice#1","title":"The MHT role and scope","tags":["MHT","mental health technician","role","scope","behavioral health technician","psych tech"],"ask":["What's inside an MHT's scope?","What should I always hand to the nurse?"],"body":"A Mental Health Technician (also called behavioral health technician or psych tech) provides direct support and safety on a behavioral health unit under the direction of nurses and the treatment team. Typical duties: observation rounds and 1:1s, milieu management, helping with activities of daily living, running or co-leading groups, delegated vital signs and fingersticks, meal and fluid monitoring, belongings and room searches, admissions and discharge support, de-escalation, and documentation.\n\nMHTs don't diagnose, assess risk clinically, give or adjust medications, or make treatment decisions. Report to the nurse promptly: any safety concern, a new or worsening symptom, abnormal vital signs, possible medication side effects, a fall or injury, a refusal of care, withdrawal signs, and anything a patient says about suicide, self-harm or harming others. When unsure, report it — it's always within scope to tell the nurse."},{"id":"mht-practice#2","title":"Observation levels and rounds","tags":["observation","rounds","Q15","Q30","safety checks","rounding","observation levels"],"ask":["What am I actually checking on a Q15 round?","How do I check someone who's sleeping?"],"body":"Observation levels set how often staff see each patient. Routine rounds may be every 15, 30 or 60 minutes; higher levels include line of sight and one-to-one. Facilities often stagger Q15 checks so they aren't predictable. Each check means actually seeing the person — not their door or a shape under a blanket — and noting location and behavior on the observation record at the time of the check, never in advance or from memory.\n\nFor someone sleeping, confirm breathing: watch the chest rise or listen; turn on a low light if needed. Look for anything that doesn't fit — hands under the blanket, items in the room, a person in the bathroom too long, the door blocked. If you can't find someone, act immediately per policy (tell the charge nurse, search, call the code). Missed or late checks should be reported, not back-filled."},{"id":"mht-practice#3","title":"Line of sight and one-to-one observation","tags":["one to one","1:1","line of sight","constant observation","arm's length","sitter"],"ask":["How close do I stay on a 1:1?","Can I hand off a 1:1 for a break?"],"body":"Line of sight means the patient is continuously visible to a staff member. One-to-one (1:1) means one staff member assigned only to that patient, within eyesight or within arm's length as ordered — arm's length is used for the highest risk, such as imminent self-harm. The assignment continues in the bathroom, shower and while the patient sleeps, with privacy managed by policy (often the door ajar and same-gender staff when possible).\n\nA 1:1 staff member doesn't take other assignments, leave the patient, or get absorbed in a phone. Hand off only face-to-face to another staff member who acknowledges the assignment. Keep the environment clear of hazards, document at the required interval, and use the time therapeutically — quiet conversation, presence and support. Report any change in behavior or mood right away."},{"id":"mht-practice#4","title":"Milieu management","tags":["milieu","unit culture","milieu therapy","community","unit management","structure"],"ask":["How do I tell when the milieu is heating up?","What helps settle a tense unit?"],"body":"The milieu is the whole unit environment — people, routines, physical space and emotional tone — and it's a treatment tool in itself. A safe milieu is predictable, structured and respectful, with clear expectations, enough activity and visible, engaged staff.\n\nWatch the unit's temperature: rising noise, clustering, a new admission stirring others, conflicts over the TV or phone, someone isolating, or staff tied up at the station. Interventions include moving staff presence into common areas, offering activities, reducing stimulation, separating peers in conflict early, giving individual attention to someone escalating, and keeping the schedule on time. Shift change, meals, medication times and visiting hours are common flashpoints, so plan coverage for them."},{"id":"mht-practice#5","title":"Therapeutic communication","tags":["therapeutic communication","communication","listening","rapport","validation","questions"],"ask":["What do I say when a patient asks if I think they're crazy?","How do I avoid giving advice?"],"body":"Therapeutic communication helps patients feel heard and supports their treatment. Useful techniques: active listening; open-ended questions (\"What's on your mind?\"); reflecting (\"You sound frustrated\"); clarifying (\"Help me understand what you mean\"); validating feelings without agreeing to unsafe behavior; offering self (\"I'll sit with you for a while\"); giving information simply; and silence, which gives time to think.\n\nAvoid false reassurance (\"Everything will be fine\"), giving advice or opinions about treatment, \"why\" questions that feel accusatory, minimizing feelings, arguing, joking at a patient's expense and changing the subject when it gets uncomfortable. Use the person's preferred name, sit at eye level when safe, and keep your tone calm. If you can't answer something, say so and connect them with someone who can."},{"id":"mht-practice#6","title":"Verbal de-escalation fundamentals","tags":["de-escalation","verbal de-escalation","agitation","aggression","calming","safety"],"ask":["Where should I stand when someone's escalating?","What's the first thing I say?"],"body":"Verbal de-escalation aims to help an agitated person regain control without force. Start with yourself: take a breath, lower your voice, slow down. Respect personal space — a leg's length or more, standing at an angle, hands visible and relaxed, and never between the person and the exit or with yourself cornered.\n\nUse one primary speaker. Introduce yourself, use short sentences, listen for the need under the anger, and reflect it (\"It sounds like you're worried you won't get your phone call\"). Agree with what you can, offer real choices, and set clear, respectful limits with consequences you can enforce. Remove the audience if peers are watching. Call for support early — a show of calm staff presence can help — and know when to tag out to a colleague the person trusts."},{"id":"mht-practice#7","title":"The escalation cycle","tags":["crisis cycle","escalation","phases","assault cycle","de-escalation stages","recovery phase"],"ask":["Why does someone re-escalate after they calm down?","What should I do in each phase?"],"body":"Escalation tends to follow a cycle. A trigger or rising anxiety is followed by escalation (raised voice, pacing, challenging), then crisis (loss of control, possible aggression toward self, others or property), then recovery (energy drops, the person may be tearful or exhausted), and finally a return toward baseline. During recovery the person is still sensitive and can re-escalate quickly if pushed.\n\nMatch your response to the phase: support and problem-solve early, give clear direction and space as it rises, prioritize safety and team response during crisis, then give time, comfort and quiet in recovery before any discussion. Debrief when the person is back to baseline. Training programs such as CPI, Mandt and Safety-Care teach their own versions; follow the one your facility uses."},{"id":"mht-practice#8","title":"Restraint and seclusion rules","tags":["restraint","seclusion","CMS","face-to-face","monitoring","least restrictive","physical hold"],"ask":["How long can a restraint order last?","What has to happen within one hour?"],"body":"Restraint and seclusion are emergency safety interventions used only when there is imminent risk of physical harm and less restrictive measures have failed or aren't possible — never for punishment, convenience or staff coercion. In hospitals that participate in Medicare, federal rules require an order from a physician or other authorized practitioner; for violent or self-destructive behavior, orders are time-limited (up to four hours for adults 18 and older, two hours for ages 9 to 17 and one hour for children under 9) and must be renewed in person or by order within those limits.\n\nA face-to-face evaluation by a physician, licensed practitioner or trained RN or PA is required within one hour of initiation. The patient must be continuously monitored, released as soon as it's safe, and every episode documented. Staff must be trained and competent. Your facility's policy and state rules add details — know them before you need them."},{"id":"mht-practice#9","title":"Restraint safety and warning signs","tags":["restraint safety","positional asphyxia","prone restraint","breathing","medical emergency"],"ask":["What signs during a hold mean we need to stop?","Why is prone restraint so dangerous?"],"body":"Physical holds and restraints can cause serious injury or death, most often from restricted breathing (positional or restraint asphyxia), cardiac events, aspiration or trauma. Risk rises with prone (face-down) positioning, pressure on the chest, back or neck, obesity, intoxication, recent exertion and certain medications.\n\nWarning signs: the person says they can't breathe, gasping or noisy breathing, change in skin color (gray, blue or very pale), sudden quiet or limpness, vomiting, or loss of responsiveness. Release pressure and reposition immediately, call for medical help, and follow your emergency procedure. Never put weight on someone's torso or neck. One staff member should be assigned to watch the head, airway and breathing throughout. Only use techniques you've been trained in."},{"id":"mht-practice#10","title":"Suicide risk: warning signs and reporting","tags":["suicide","suicide precautions","warning signs","risk","reporting","observation"],"ask":["What should I report right away?","What do I say if a patient tells me they want to die?"],"body":"Report immediately to the nurse: any talk of suicide, death or being a burden; asking about ways to die; giving away belongings or saying goodbyes; hoarding medications or objects; sudden calm after severe depression; new agitation, insomnia or hopelessness; and attempts to get into restricted areas. Bad news, anniversaries, discharge planning and the first days after admission are higher-risk times.\n\nIf a patient tells you they're thinking about suicide, stay with them, stay calm, listen and thank them for telling you. You can ask directly whether they're thinking of hurting themselves — it doesn't increase risk. Don't leave them alone or promise to keep it secret. Tell the nurse right away; the clinical team assesses risk and sets precautions and observation level. Keep the environment safe and document what was said in their words."},{"id":"mht-practice#11","title":"Responding to self-harm","tags":["self-harm","self-injury","cutting","first aid","response","non-suicidal self-injury"],"ask":["How should I react when I find someone has self-harmed?","What do I document?"],"body":"When you find someone has self-harmed, stay calm and non-judgmental. Call for help, ensure safety (remove the object if it's safe to do so), and provide or get first aid per your training. Notify the nurse immediately for assessment. Avoid shock, anger, lecturing or showing disgust — shame can increase distress.\n\nSelf-harm can be a way of coping with overwhelming emotions and isn't always a suicide attempt, but every instance needs clinical evaluation. Afterward, search for other items per policy, check the environment for what was used and how it was obtained, and report environmental factors so they can be fixed. Document objectively: the time, what you observed, what the person said, the care given and who was notified. Debrief with your team; these moments affect staff too."},{"id":"mht-practice#12","title":"Elopement prevention","tags":["elopement","AWOL","exit seeking","absconding","missing patient","flight risk"],"ask":["What are the warning signs someone might run?","What do I do if a patient is missing?"],"body":"Elopement risk rises with involuntary status, recent admission, wanting to leave against advice, withdrawal or cravings, family or legal stress, court dates, confusion or dementia, and past elopement. Warning signs: hovering near doors, watching staff codes or keys, asking about schedules and exits, wearing shoes and coats indoors, packing belongings and sudden interest in outdoor time.\n\nPrevention: know who's at risk, report exit-seeking, keep doors and sally ports controlled, never prop doors, check doors behind you, and be aware during transports, visits and outdoor time. If someone is missing, act immediately: notify the charge nurse, call the facility's code, search the unit systematically, and give a description (clothing, last seen location and time). Every minute matters."},{"id":"mht-practice#13","title":"Contraband and searches","tags":["contraband","searches","room search","belongings","safety","skin check","prohibited items"],"ask":["What counts as contraband?","How do I search respectfully?"],"body":"Contraband includes items that could be used for harm or that undermine treatment: sharps, glass, cords, belts, shoelaces, plastic bags, lighters, medications or substances, aerosol cans, weapons and items your unit restricts. Common entry points are admissions, visitors, mail, outdoor time and items returned from passes.\n\nSearch per policy — usually with two staff, same-gender for personal searches, and the patient present for room searches when possible. Explain what you're doing and why, be respectful and thorough, and return or store belongings with an inventory. Report findings to the nurse and document what was found and where. Use contraband findings to improve safety: note how items got in and pass that on so procedures or the environment can be changed."},{"id":"mht-practice#14","title":"Vital signs and when to report","tags":["vital signs","blood pressure","pulse","temperature","respiration","oxygen saturation","report"],"ask":["What vital sign values should I report?","How do I get an accurate blood pressure?"],"body":"Typical adult resting ranges: blood pressure around 90–120 systolic and 60–80 diastolic; pulse 60–100 beats per minute; respirations 12–20 breaths per minute; oral temperature about 97–99°F (36.1–37.2°C); oxygen saturation 95–100% on room air. Your facility's policy sets the exact values that must be reported — follow it.\n\nReport promptly any value outside the parameters, any sudden change from the person's baseline, and symptoms such as chest pain, shortness of breath, dizziness, confusion, fever, or a fall. Get accurate readings: the right cuff size, the arm supported at heart level, the person seated and rested, legs uncrossed. Re-check unexpected values, and if a patient looks unwell, tell the nurse even if the numbers look normal."},{"id":"mht-practice#15","title":"Medication side effects to watch for and report","tags":["side effects","EPS","akathisia","NMS","lithium toxicity","serotonin syndrome","clozapine"],"ask":["What does akathisia look like?","Which side effects are emergencies?"],"body":"MHTs don't manage medications, but they're often first to see side effects. Report to the nurse: stiffness, tremor, shuffling gait or drooling (extrapyramidal symptoms); inner restlessness and constant pacing (akathisia); sudden muscle spasms of the neck, eyes or jaw (acute dystonia — urgent); repetitive mouth or tongue movements; dizziness on standing; heavy sedation; constipation; and rapid weight change.\n\nSome reactions are emergencies — tell the nurse immediately: high fever with rigid muscles and confusion (possible neuroleptic malignant syndrome); agitation, sweating, fever, tremor and diarrhea (possible serotonin syndrome); vomiting, diarrhea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness or confusion in someone taking lithium (possible toxicity); and fever, sore throat or signs of infection in someone taking clozapine. Also report rash, swelling or trouble breathing."},{"id":"mht-practice#16","title":"Alcohol, benzodiazepine and opioid withdrawal","tags":["withdrawal","alcohol withdrawal","benzodiazepine","opioid withdrawal","detox","seizures","DTs"],"ask":["Which withdrawal signs are dangerous?","What do opioid withdrawal symptoms look like?"],"body":"Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal can be life-threatening. Signs include tremor, sweating, anxiety, agitation, rapid heart rate, high blood pressure, nausea, insomnia, and hallucinations; severe withdrawal can cause seizures and delirium tremens (confusion, fever, severe agitation), often 48 to 96 hours after the last drink. Nurses use scales such as CIWA-Ar; MHTs report changes, take delegated vitals on schedule, and maintain seizure and fall precautions.\n\nOpioid withdrawal is extremely uncomfortable though rarely life-threatening on its own: yawning, runny nose and eyes, goosebumps, sweating, dilated pupils, muscle aches, abdominal cramps, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anxiety and cravings. Report symptoms so the nurse can treat them. Lost tolerance after withdrawal means a much higher overdose risk if someone uses again after leaving."},{"id":"mht-practice#17","title":"Recognizing overdose and using naloxone","tags":["overdose","naloxone","Narcan","opioid overdose","emergency","rescue breathing"],"ask":["What are the signs of an opioid overdose?","How does naloxone work?"],"body":"Signs of opioid overdose: unresponsive or can't be woken, slow, shallow or stopped breathing, gurgling or snoring sounds, pinpoint pupils, and blue or gray lips and fingertips. Call for help and activate your emergency response (a medical code or 911), give naloxone, support breathing and stay with the person.\n\nNaloxone (for example the nasal spray) reverses opioid effects for 30 to 90 minutes. It's safe and does nothing harmful if opioids aren't present. A second dose may be needed after two to three minutes if there's no response, especially with fentanyl. Because naloxone can wear off before the opioid, the person needs medical evaluation even after waking. Withdrawal symptoms can appear suddenly when they wake up — stay calm and reassuring. Know where naloxone is kept on your unit."},{"id":"mht-practice#18","title":"Common conditions in plain language","tags":["diagnoses","depression","bipolar","schizophrenia","PTSD","anxiety","personality disorder"],"ask":["What does mania look like on the unit?","How should I think about a diagnosis as a tech?"],"body":"Knowing common conditions helps you recognize changes to report, not diagnose. Major depression: persistent low mood, loss of interest, sleep and appetite changes, hopelessness. Bipolar disorder: episodes of depression and mania — high energy, little sleep, rapid speech, grandiosity and impulsivity. Schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders: hallucinations, delusions, disorganized thinking and withdrawal. PTSD: intrusive memories, nightmares, hypervigilance, avoidance and startle. Anxiety disorders: excessive worry, panic attacks. Borderline personality disorder: intense emotions, fear of abandonment, impulsivity and self-harm. Substance use disorders often co-occur.\n\nA diagnosis describes symptoms, not the person. Behavior on the unit usually makes sense as a response to symptoms, fear or unmet needs. Observe and report what you see and hear, in objective terms."},{"id":"mht-practice#19","title":"Communicating with someone experiencing psychosis","tags":["psychosis","hallucinations","delusions","responding to internal stimuli","paranoia"],"ask":["Should I argue with a delusion?","What do I say when someone hears voices?"],"body":"When someone is experiencing psychosis, keep communication simple, calm and concrete. Use short sentences, one idea at a time, and a quiet environment. Don't argue with delusions or pretend to agree with them; acknowledge the feeling instead (\"That sounds frightening\") and focus on what's real and needed now (\"I'm here, and you're safe on this unit\").\n\nIf someone appears to be responding to voices, you can ask gently what they're hearing and whether the voices are telling them to do anything — and report command hallucinations to the nurse right away. Paranoid patients may fear touch, food or medications; explain what you're doing before you do it and give space. Note the content and timing of what you observe for the team."},{"id":"mht-practice#20","title":"Communicating with someone in mania","tags":["mania","bipolar","hypomania","overstimulation","impulsivity","sleep"],"ask":["How do I redirect someone who won't stop talking?","Why does someone in mania get irritable?"],"body":"People in mania may be energetic, talkative, distractible, irritable or grandiose, and may sleep very little. Overstimulation makes it worse. Lower the stimulation — a quieter space, fewer people, dim lighting — and keep your own tone calm and even. Use brief, clear statements and set gentle, consistent limits on intrusive behavior.\n\nRedirect toward simple, contained activities, and support rest, food and fluids, since people in mania may forget to eat or drink. Avoid arguing with grandiose ideas or getting drawn into debates. Irritability can escalate quickly when someone feels blocked, so offer choices and give them time. Watch boundaries with peers, including impulsive sexual behavior and giving away belongings, and report sleep and behavior patterns to the nurse."},{"id":"mht-practice#21","title":"Working with adolescents","tags":["adolescents","youth","teens","child and adolescent","developmentally appropriate","boundaries"],"ask":["What's different about working on an adolescent unit?","How do I set limits with teens without power struggles?"],"body":"Adolescent units need structure, consistency and genuine relationships. Teens test limits and are highly sensitive to fairness, respect and being heard. Explain the reasons for rules, enforce them consistently across staff, and avoid public power struggles — address issues privately when you can. Offer choices within limits and praise specific positive behavior.\n\nExpect peer dynamics: alliances, conflict, romantic interest and contagion of behaviors. Supervise closely during transitions and unstructured time. Keep professional boundaries — you're a supportive adult, not a friend; avoid personal disclosure and never share social media. Families or guardians are usually involved, and reporting requirements for abuse or neglect apply. Watch for bullying, self-harm and boundary violations, and report them."},{"id":"mht-practice#22","title":"Older adults, dementia and delirium","tags":["geriatric","older adults","dementia","delirium","falls","sundowning","geropsych"],"ask":["How can I tell delirium from dementia?","What helps with sundowning?"],"body":"On geriatric units, safety and dignity go together. Dementia is a gradual, progressive decline in memory and thinking. Delirium is a sudden change in attention and awareness, often fluctuating over the day, and is a medical emergency often caused by infection, medications, dehydration or pain. Report any sudden change in confusion, alertness or behavior right away.\n\nHelp with orientation (clocks, calendars, natural light), hearing aids and glasses, and a predictable routine. For late-day agitation (sundowning), reduce noise, keep lighting steady, offer calm activities and check for unmet needs like toileting, hunger, pain or fatigue. Fall prevention is critical: non-slip footwear, call lights within reach, clear paths and assistance with walking. Monitor intake and output, skin integrity and swallowing safety."},{"id":"mht-practice#23","title":"Trauma-informed care on the unit","tags":["trauma-informed care","trauma","unit practice","triggers","re-traumatization","choice"],"ask":["What are common trauma triggers on an inpatient unit?","How do I make searches and holds less traumatizing?"],"body":"Many patients have trauma histories, and hospital routines — locked doors, searches, being watched, physical holds, loss of control — can be re-traumatizing. Trauma-informed care asks staff to prioritize safety, trustworthiness, choice, collaboration and empowerment in everyday interactions.\n\nPractical steps: explain what you're doing before you do it; knock and announce yourself; offer choices wherever possible; respect privacy and modesty; ask about triggers and calming preferences at admission and share them with the team; use comfort rooms and sensory tools; and avoid restrictive interventions whenever possible. After any incident, help the person regain a sense of control. Staff trauma matters too — debriefing and support after incidents are part of a trauma-informed unit."},{"id":"mht-practice#24","title":"Documentation principles for MHTs","tags":["documentation","charting","objective","late entry","observation record","legal record"],"ask":["What makes a note objective?","How do I correct a mistake in the chart?"],"body":"The medical record is a legal document. Write what you saw and heard, not opinions or labels: \"Patient paced the hallway for 20 minutes, said 'I need to get out of here,' and accepted an offer to walk to the courtyard with staff\" rather than \"Patient was agitated and manipulative.\" Quote patients' own words. Include times, interventions offered and the response.\n\nDocument promptly and only for care you provided or observed. Never document in advance, back-fill rounds you didn't do, or alter entries. Use your facility's process for corrections and late entries. Avoid abbreviations your facility doesn't allow. Keep personal notes about patients off personal devices unless your facility permits it — BlackLab drafts use initials only and are meant to be copied into the official record."},{"id":"mht-practice#25","title":"SBAR and shift report","tags":["SBAR","handoff","shift report","communication","change of shift"],"ask":["What belongs in a good handoff?","How do I report a concern to the nurse with SBAR?"],"body":"SBAR structures communication: Situation (what's happening now), Background (relevant context), Assessment (what you observed and think is going on — for MHTs, observations rather than clinical judgments), and Recommendation (what you need or suggest). It works for shift report and for calling a nurse about a concern.\n\nAt shift change, cover each patient's observation level and precautions, notable behaviors, sleep, intake, groups, incidents, family or legal events and anything pending. Include unit-level items: census, admissions and discharges, room changes, maintenance issues, sharps counts and environmental sweeps. Use a consistent order and give the oncoming staff time to ask questions. BlackLab's SBAR handoff pulls in your census and last checks automatically."},{"id":"mht-practice#26","title":"Environmental safety and ligature risks","tags":["ligature","ligature risk","environment of care","Joint Commission","NPSG 15.01.01","anti-ligature"],"ask":["What is a ligature point?","What does the Joint Commission expect for psychiatric units?"],"body":"A ligature point is anything a cord, sheet or other material could be tied to or looped over to hang or strangle — door hinges, handles, closers, hooks, shower heads, faucets, grab bars, pipes, light fixtures, sprinkler heads, vents, bed frames, and furniture. Hanging is a leading method of inpatient suicide.\n\nThe Joint Commission's National Patient Safety Goal on suicide prevention (NPSG 15.01.01) requires an environmental risk assessment to identify features that could be used for suicide, and expects psychiatric units in hospitals to be ligature-resistant, with mitigation for any remaining risks. Typical controls: continuous hinges, ligature-resistant hardware and fixtures, tamper-resistant fasteners, secured ceilings, and staff rounds to find damaged or missing items. Every staff member should report hazards immediately — BlackLab's Safety Rounds turn findings into work orders."},{"id":"mht-practice#27","title":"Unit safety sweeps and room checks","tags":["safety sweep","environment check","room check","unit sweep","hazards","maintenance"],"ask":["What should I look for on an environment sweep?","What do I do if I find a broken fixture?"],"body":"Environment sweeps at the start and end of each shift — and after incidents — catch hazards before they're used. Check patient rooms and bathrooms for damaged fixtures, loose screws or ceiling tiles, holes in walls, exposed wires, broken furniture, missing vent covers, cords, plastic bags, glass, sharps and hoarded medications. Check common areas for movable or throwable items, unsecured art supplies, unlocked doors to staff areas, and unattended carts.\n\nIf you find a hazard: make it safe now (remove the item, restrict access to the area, increase observation), report it to the charge nurse, and get a work order in right away with the exact location and problem. Follow up until it's fixed. Patterns — the same fixture failing repeatedly — should go to facilities and the safety committee so the root cause gets addressed."},{"id":"mht-practice#28","title":"Emergency codes and roles","tags":["codes","emergency response","code team","behavioral emergency","medical emergency","fire"],"ask":["What's my job when a behavioral code is called?","What should I do first in a medical emergency?"],"body":"Facilities use codes for medical emergencies, behavioral emergencies, missing patients, fire and security threats; names and colors vary, so learn yours. Know your role before an emergency: who leads, who responds, who stays with the rest of the patients, and where emergency equipment (AED, oxygen, suction, naloxone, first-aid kits) is kept.\n\nIn a behavioral emergency, respond calmly, follow the team leader, and avoid crowding — sometimes the best help is managing the milieu and moving other patients away. In a medical emergency, call for help, start basic life support if trained and needed, and bring equipment. For fire, follow RACE (rescue, alarm, contain, extinguish or evacuate) and PASS for extinguishers (pull, aim, squeeze, sweep). Debrief after every code."},{"id":"mht-practice#29","title":"Patient rights","tags":["patient rights","rights","grievance","privacy","dignity","informed consent","least restrictive"],"ask":["What rights do patients keep on a locked unit?","What do I do when a patient wants to file a grievance?"],"body":"Patients keep their rights when they are admitted, including on locked or involuntary units. These generally include being treated with dignity and respect, privacy and confidentiality, freedom from abuse, neglect and harassment, freedom from restraint or seclusion used as coercion, discipline, convenience or retaliation, participation in treatment planning, information about their care, communication with others subject to documented limits, and the right to voice complaints and file grievances without retaliation.\n\nKnow how the grievance process works on your unit and help patients use it — that's part of good care, not a threat. Any restriction of rights must follow policy and be documented by the team. If you see something that looks like abuse or neglect, report it through the required channel immediately."},{"id":"mht-practice#30","title":"HIPAA and personal devices","tags":["HIPAA","privacy","PHI","personal devices","phones","social media","confidentiality"],"ask":["Can I keep notes about patients on my phone?","What counts as protected health information?"],"body":"HIPAA protects identifiable health information — names, dates, room numbers combined with identifying details, photos, record numbers and anything else that could identify a patient along with information about their care. Share it only with people who need it for treatment, payment or operations, in private settings.\n\nNever photograph patients or post about work on social media, even without names. Don't discuss patients in elevators, cafeterias or at home. Most facilities restrict personal devices on units; follow your policy. If you use any personal tool, keep it free of identifiers: BlackLab's Behavioral Health workspace uses initials or room numbers only, encrypts everything on the phone, erases census and rounds automatically and never syncs or backs them up. The facility's EHR remains the official record."},{"id":"mht-practice#31","title":"Staff safety and recovery after incidents","tags":["staff safety","injury","workplace violence","debrief","staff wellbeing","post-incident"],"ask":["What should I do if I get hurt at work?","Why do staff debriefs matter?"],"body":"Behavioral health workers face higher rates of workplace violence than most occupations. Safety comes from training, teamwork, enough staff, good environments and a culture where staff call for help early. Know your duress or panic alarm, stay aware of exits, and never go into a room with an escalating patient alone if policy calls for two.\n\nIf you're hurt, get care, report the injury and complete the required documentation promptly — even minor injuries. After an incident, a debrief helps the team learn and helps people process what happened. Check on each other. Reactions like replaying the event, jumpiness, trouble sleeping or dread before a shift are common; if they persist, use your EAP or talk to someone. Taking care of yourself is part of taking care of patients."},{"id":"mht-practice#32","title":"Infection prevention basics","tags":["infection prevention","hand hygiene","standard precautions","PPE","cleaning","outbreaks"],"ask":["When do I need to wash my hands?","What PPE do I need for a blood spill?"],"body":"Hand hygiene is the single most important infection-prevention step: before and after patient contact, after touching blood or body fluids, after removing gloves, after touching the patient environment, and before eating. Use soap and water when hands are visibly soiled or after caring for someone with diarrhea; alcohol rub works for most other situations. On behavioral units, alcohol-based rub dispensers may be restricted or placed out of reach because of ingestion risk.\n\nStandard precautions treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious: gloves for contact, plus gowns, masks and eye protection when splashing is possible. Clean spills with approved disinfectants per policy and dispose of sharps and contaminated items properly. Shared spaces and items — group supplies, phones, recreation equipment — need regular cleaning, especially during outbreaks."}]},{"id":"peer-support","title":"Peer Support Practice","kind":"DOMAIN","summary":"Peer support values, recovery, purposeful sharing, boundaries, ethics, motivational interviewing, wellness planning, documentation and self-care for Peer Support Specialists.","icon":"","starters":[],"draws":[],"entries":[{"id":"peer-support#1","title":"What peer support is — and isn't","tags":["peer support","role","peer specialist","lived experience","definition","scope"],"ask":["How is a peer different from a counselor?","What should I say when someone asks me for clinical advice?"],"body":"Peer support is a relationship between people who share lived experience of mental health challenges, substance use, or both. A peer specialist uses that experience on purpose to build hope, model recovery and help people find their own path. The relationship is mutual and non-hierarchical: you walk alongside someone rather than directing them.\n\nPeer support is not therapy, case management, sponsorship or friendship. Peers do not diagnose, assess risk clinically, give medication advice or tell people what to do. When a question belongs to a clinician — a medication side effect, a diagnosis, a legal question — the peer response is to help the person put the question into words and connect them with the right professional, often by going with them. Being clear about the role protects both people and keeps the team working together."},{"id":"peer-support#2","title":"Core values of peer work","tags":["values","mutuality","hope","self-determination","peer values","principles"],"ask":["What does mutuality look like day to day?","How do I keep self-determination when I'm worried about someone?"],"body":"Peer work rests on a few shared values. Hope: recovery is possible for everyone, and your presence is evidence of it. Mutuality: both people learn from each other; power is shared. Self-determination: the person chooses their goals, pathways and pace — even when you would choose differently. Strengths-based: you look first at what is working. Authenticity: you are real, honest and transparent about your role. Respect and dignity: people are experts on their own lives.\n\nThese values show up in small things — asking before offering advice, using the person's words, celebrating small wins, being reliable about follow-up, and telling people plainly what you will and won't share with the team. When you feel pulled to take over, return to self-determination: offer options, explore pros and cons, and let the person decide."},{"id":"peer-support#3","title":"SAMHSA core competencies for peer workers","tags":["core competencies","SAMHSA","competencies","skills","peer certification","training"],"ask":["Which competency should I strengthen first?","How do the competencies show up on an inpatient unit?"],"body":"SAMHSA describes core competencies for peer workers in behavioral health, grouped into twelve areas: engaging peers in collaborative relationships; providing support; sharing lived experience; personalizing peer support; supporting recovery planning; linking to resources, services and supports; providing information about skills related to health, wellness and recovery; helping peers manage crises; valuing communication; supporting collaboration and teamwork; promoting leadership and advocacy; and promoting growth and development.\n\nMost state peer certifications and the national credential build training and exams around these areas. A useful habit is to tag your session notes with the competencies you used — over a few weeks you will see your strengths and the areas you rarely practice, which makes great material for supervision and your continuing-education plan."},{"id":"peer-support#4","title":"SAMHSA's working definition of recovery","tags":["recovery","definition","SAMHSA","health home purpose community","guiding principles"],"ask":["What are the four dimensions of recovery?","How do I explain recovery to someone who feels hopeless?"],"body":"SAMHSA defines recovery as a process of change through which individuals improve their health and wellness, live self-directed lives and strive to reach their full potential. It names four dimensions that support a life in recovery: health (managing conditions and making healthy choices), home (a stable and safe place to live), purpose (meaningful daily activities such as work, school, volunteering or caregiving), and community (relationships and social networks that provide support, friendship, love and hope).\n\nSAMHSA also lists guiding principles: recovery emerges from hope; is person-driven; occurs via many pathways; is holistic; is supported by peers and allies; is supported through relationship and social networks; is culturally based and influenced; is supported by addressing trauma; involves individual, family and community strengths and responsibility; and is based on respect. These principles make a good checklist when you plan a session or review a goal."},{"id":"peer-support#5","title":"Recovery capital","tags":["recovery capital","strengths","resources","assessment","recovery domains"],"ask":["How do I rate recovery capital with someone?","What do I do with a low score in one area?"],"body":"Recovery capital is the total of internal and external resources a person can draw on to start and sustain recovery. It is often described in four groups: personal capital (health, coping skills, hope, self-esteem), social capital (supportive relationships, recovery peers), community capital (access to services, housing, transportation, recovery-friendly spaces) and cultural capital (values, traditions and identity that support wellness).\n\nRating recovery capital together — even with a simple zero-to-five scale across life areas — turns a vague sense of \"things are hard\" into specific places to focus. High areas are strengths to build on; low areas become goals or referrals. Re-rate every few weeks so the person can see change. BlackLab's participant page has a recovery capital check you can do side by side; keep it collaborative and let the person give the numbers."},{"id":"peer-support#6","title":"Purposeful sharing of lived experience","tags":["self-disclosure","sharing your story","lived experience","purposeful sharing","storytelling"],"ask":["How much of my story should I share?","When should I not share?"],"body":"Sharing your experience is the peer specialist's most distinctive tool, and it works best when it is purposeful: brief, relevant and for the other person's benefit. Before sharing, ask yourself why — to instill hope, normalize an experience, build trust, or model a coping skill. Share the \"how\" of recovery more than the details of the hardest moments; graphic details of use, trauma or self-harm can trigger people and shift attention to you.\n\nA simple shape is then–turning point–now: what it was like, what helped, what life is like today. Keep it short, then turn the focus back: \"That's what helped me — what's been helpful for you?\" Don't share when you're feeling raw, when it would make the person take care of you, or when it would compare struggles. Talk through your disclosure boundaries in supervision before you need them."},{"id":"peer-support#7","title":"Boundaries and dual relationships","tags":["boundaries","dual relationships","ethics","friendship","gifts","social media"],"ask":["Can I be friends with someone I support?","What if I know a participant from my own recovery community?"],"body":"Boundaries keep peer relationships safe and helpful. Common ones: no romantic or sexual relationships; no lending or borrowing money; no gifts beyond what policy allows; no personal social-media connections while you're in a peer role; no sharing your personal phone unless your program allows it; and no doing for someone what they can do for themselves.\n\nDual relationships are common in peer work — you may meet participants at mutual-aid meetings, in your neighborhood or at work in another role. They aren't always avoidable, but they must be managed: be transparent, keep confidentiality, avoid exploiting the relationship, and bring it to supervision. If you hold another job at the same facility (for example in facilities or as a technician), be clear about which role you're in and never use one role's access to benefit the other."},{"id":"peer-support#8","title":"Confidentiality and its limits","tags":["confidentiality","HIPAA","42 CFR Part 2","privacy","release of information","duty to warn"],"ask":["What can I tell the team?","What if someone asks me to keep a secret?"],"body":"Peer specialists working in health settings are part of the treatment team and follow HIPAA. Substance use disorder treatment records may also be protected by the federal rule known as 42 CFR Part 2, which has stricter consent requirements for sharing. Never discuss participants outside work, in public places or on personal devices or social media, and share only what's needed within the team.\n\nBe honest about limits from the first meeting: \"Most of what you tell me stays between us and your care team. If I'm worried about your safety or someone else's, or about abuse of a child or vulnerable adult, I have to share that.\" Never promise to keep a secret before you know what it is. If someone tells you something you must report, tell them what you're going to do and stay with them through it when you can."},{"id":"peer-support#9","title":"Mandated reporting basics","tags":["mandated reporter","abuse","neglect","reporting","child abuse","elder abuse"],"ask":["Am I a mandated reporter?","What do I do if a participant discloses abuse?"],"body":"Many states make peer specialists and behavioral health staff mandated reporters of suspected abuse or neglect of children, and often of elders and adults with disabilities. Your employer's policy and your state law define exactly who must report, what must be reported and how quickly. Facility policy usually also requires you to notify your supervisor or the charge nurse.\n\nIf a disclosure happens: stay calm, listen, don't interrogate or promise secrecy, thank the person for telling you, and explain what happens next. Document what was said in the person's own words and report promptly through the required channel. You don't need proof — reasonable suspicion is enough. Afterward, take care of yourself and debrief with your supervisor; these conversations are heavy."},{"id":"peer-support#10","title":"Motivational interviewing spirit and OARS","tags":["motivational interviewing","OARS","open questions","affirmations","reflections","summaries","MI spirit"],"ask":["What's a good open question to start with?","How do I make a reflection instead of asking another question?"],"body":"Motivational interviewing (MI) is a collaborative conversation style for strengthening a person's own motivation for change. Its spirit is partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation — drawing out the person's own reasons rather than installing yours.\n\nThe core skills are OARS. Open questions invite more than a yes or no (\"What would you like to be different?\"). Affirmations recognize real strengths and efforts (\"You came back even after a hard week — that takes commitment.\"). Reflections repeat or rephrase what you heard, often with a guess at the meaning (\"You're worried going home means going back to the same people.\"). Summaries pull several points together and check accuracy. A good target is two or more reflections for every question. MI fits peer work closely because it respects self-determination."},{"id":"peer-support#11","title":"Stages of change","tags":["stages of change","transtheoretical model","readiness","precontemplation","contemplation","relapse"],"ask":["How do I support someone who isn't ready to change?","What stage is someone in if they keep saying \"I know, but…\"?"],"body":"The stages-of-change model describes readiness as a process: precontemplation (not yet considering change), contemplation (ambivalent — seeing pros and cons), preparation (planning and small steps), action (actively changing), and maintenance (sustaining change). Returning to old patterns can happen at any point and is part of many recovery stories, not a failure.\n\nMatch your support to the stage. In precontemplation, build the relationship, offer information without pressure and explore what the person values. In contemplation, explore ambivalence with MI. In preparation, help make a concrete plan. In action, problem-solve and celebrate. In maintenance, focus on wellness, relapse prevention and building a full life. Note your impression in your documentation as a peer observation, not a clinical assessment."},{"id":"peer-support#12","title":"Change talk, sustain talk and discord","tags":["change talk","sustain talk","ambivalence","resistance","discord","rolling with resistance"],"ask":["How do I respond when someone pushes back?","What does change talk sound like?"],"body":"In MI, change talk is anything the person says in favor of change — desire, ability, reasons, need, commitment (\"I want to get my kids back,\" \"I could call my sister\"). Sustain talk argues for staying the same (\"Drinking is the only thing that helps me sleep\"). Both are normal parts of ambivalence. Your job is to notice change talk and invite more of it with questions and reflections, without arguing against sustain talk.\n\nDiscord is friction in the relationship — the person feels pushed, judged or misunderstood. Don't push harder. Try a reflection (\"It sounds like you're tired of people telling you what to do\"), emphasize their choice (\"It's completely up to you\"), apologize if you got ahead of them, or shift focus. Arguing increases resistance; understanding lowers it."},{"id":"peer-support#13","title":"Wellness and crisis planning (WRAP-style)","tags":["WRAP","wellness recovery action plan","wellness plan","crisis plan","triggers","early warning signs"],"ask":["How do I introduce a wellness plan?","What goes in the crisis section?"],"body":"A Wellness Recovery Action Plan (WRAP) is a self-designed plan developed by Mary Ellen Copeland and widely used in peer support; formal WRAP facilitation requires training through the Copeland Center. Similar personal wellness plans usually include a wellness toolbox (things that help), daily maintenance, triggers and responses, early warning signs, signs things are breaking down, a crisis plan (who to contact, what helps and doesn't, preferences for treatment), and a post-crisis plan.\n\nThe person writes the plan in their own words; you ask questions and offer examples, including from your own plan if appropriate. It's never finished — revisit it as things change. Encourage sharing it with chosen supporters and the treatment team, and keep a copy accessible. BlackLab includes a wellness and crisis plan form you can draft together."},{"id":"peer-support#14","title":"Strengths-based practice","tags":["strengths","strengths-based","resilience","asset-based","affirmations"],"ask":["How do I find strengths when someone only talks about problems?","How do I document strengths?"],"body":"A strengths-based approach starts with what people have rather than what they lack: skills, interests, relationships, values, past successes, faith, humor and resilience. People who feel their strengths are seen are more likely to engage and to believe change is possible.\n\nAsk about times things went better (\"When was the last time you felt okay? What was different?\"), about how they've survived hard times, and about what others appreciate in them. Reflect strengths you notice in the moment. In notes, write strengths as specifically as problems: \"Participant identified their daughter and their faith community as reasons to stay sober and described walking daily as a coping skill.\" Goals built on strengths stick better than goals built on deficits."},{"id":"peer-support#15","title":"Trauma-informed peer support","tags":["trauma-informed","trauma","ACEs","safety","choice","re-traumatization"],"ask":["How do I avoid re-traumatizing someone?","What do I do if someone starts talking about trauma?"],"body":"Most people in behavioral health settings have experienced trauma. Trauma-informed peer support follows SAMHSA's principles: safety, trustworthiness and transparency, peer support, collaboration and mutuality, empowerment, voice and choice, and attention to cultural, historical and gender issues. In practice that means asking \"what happened to you?\" rather than \"what's wrong with you?\"\n\nOffer choices wherever you can (where to sit, whether to close the door, what to talk about). Explain what you're doing and why. Notice signs of distress — dissociation, agitation, shutting down — and slow down or shift to grounding. You don't need to hear trauma details to be helpful; supportive listening and connection to trauma-focused clinicians is the peer role. Watch your own reactions too; secondary trauma is real."},{"id":"peer-support#16","title":"Recovery-oriented, person-first language","tags":["language","person-first","stigma","recovery language","word choice"],"ask":["What words should I avoid in notes?","How do I talk about substance use without stigma?"],"body":"Words shape how people see themselves and how others treat them. Person-first language puts the person before the condition: \"a person living with schizophrenia,\" \"a person who uses drugs,\" \"a person in recovery.\" Avoid labels such as addict, junkie, abuser, schizophrenic, crazy, manipulative, non-compliant or \"frequent flyer.\"\n\nRecovery-oriented alternatives: \"chose not to take the medication\" or \"declined\" instead of \"non-compliant\"; \"substance use\" instead of \"abuse\"; \"positive or negative test\" instead of \"clean or dirty\"; \"return to use\" or \"recurrence\" alongside \"relapse\"; \"seeking support\" instead of \"attention-seeking.\" Ask people which words they prefer for themselves. Modeling this language in team meetings and documentation is one way peers change systems."},{"id":"peer-support#17","title":"Shared decision-making and advocacy","tags":["advocacy","shared decision making","self-advocacy","rights","treatment team"],"ask":["How do I advocate without overstepping?","How do I help someone speak up in a treatment team meeting?"],"body":"Peers support people to make informed choices and to have their voices heard. Shared decision-making means the person and their providers look at options together, with the person's values and preferences guiding the choice. A peer can help someone prepare questions, practice what they want to say, and attend meetings for support.\n\nSelf-advocacy comes first: help the person speak for themselves wherever possible. When you advocate directly, do it with their permission, on the issue they chose, through the right channels (the treatment team, a patient advocate, the grievance process). Be respectful with staff — you're part of the team and the relationship matters for next time. Know the rights process at your facility, and document advocacy as a peer intervention."},{"id":"peer-support#18","title":"Supporting someone in crisis as a peer","tags":["crisis","peer crisis support","escalation","safety","staying present","crisis response"],"ask":["What's my job when someone is in crisis?","What should I never do in a crisis as a peer?"],"body":"In a crisis your job is to stay calm, stay present and connect the person to the help they need. Listen without judgment, speak slowly, validate feelings, and help the person feel less alone. Use grounding skills if they are open to them. Offer choices and avoid power struggles. If there's any risk to safety, bring in the clinical team right away — that's part of peer support, not a betrayal of it.\n\nDon't promise outcomes you can't control, physically intervene unless you are trained and it's your role, or leave a person at risk alone. Afterward, help the person reflect on what led up to the crisis and what helped, and update their wellness or crisis plan. Then debrief with your supervisor; crisis moments affect helpers too."},{"id":"peer-support#19","title":"Suicide: warning signs and what a peer does","tags":["suicide","suicide prevention","warning signs","ask directly","988","gatekeeper"],"ask":["Is it okay to ask someone directly about suicide?","What do I do right after someone tells me they're thinking about suicide?"],"body":"Warning signs include talking about wanting to die or being a burden, looking for ways to end one's life, giving away possessions, saying goodbye, withdrawal, sudden calm after deep depression, increased substance use, rage or reckless behavior, and expressions of hopelessness or feeling trapped.\n\nAsking directly — \"Are you thinking about suicide?\" — does not put the idea in someone's head; research shows it can bring relief. If the answer is yes, listen, stay with them, thank them for telling you, and connect them to clinical help immediately: in a facility, tell the nurse or clinician right away; in the community, involve your supervisor and use 988 or emergency services when danger is immediate. Risk assessment belongs to clinicians. Gatekeeper trainings such as QPR teach these steps in a short course."},{"id":"peer-support#20","title":"Harm reduction and many pathways","tags":["harm reduction","pathways","naloxone","abstinence","multiple pathways","MAT"],"ask":["How does harm reduction fit with recovery?","What if someone's pathway isn't one I used?"],"body":"Recovery happens through many pathways: abstinence-based mutual aid, medication for addiction treatment, clinical treatment, faith-based programs, harm reduction, cultural healing or natural recovery without formal help. People often combine them. A peer supports the pathway the person chooses, even if it differs from their own.\n\nHarm reduction meets people where they are and reduces risk: naloxone access, not using alone, safer-use supplies, fentanyl test strips where legal, and any positive change. It keeps people alive and connected, which makes future change possible. Know your program's policy, and be ready to share overdose-response basics: recognize the signs, call 911, give naloxone, provide rescue breathing if trained and place the person in the recovery position."},{"id":"peer-support#21","title":"Medication for addiction treatment and peers","tags":["MAT","MOUD","buprenorphine","methadone","naltrexone","medication","stigma"],"ask":["Is someone on buprenorphine \"really\" in recovery?","What do I say when someone asks if they should stop their medication?"],"body":"Medications for opioid use disorder — methadone, buprenorphine and naltrexone — are evidence-based treatments that reduce overdose deaths and help people stay in recovery. Medications for alcohol use disorder include naltrexone, acamprosate and disulfiram. People using these medications are in recovery; questioning that is stigma.\n\nPeers don't advise on starting, stopping or changing any medication. If someone has questions or concerns, help them write the questions down and talk with their prescriber, and offer to go with them. You can share information about local programs, insurance and pharmacies, and support people who meet stigma in mutual-aid groups by pointing them to medication-friendly meetings and communities."},{"id":"peer-support#22","title":"Mutual-aid and recovery communities","tags":["mutual aid","AA","NA","SMART Recovery","Recovery Dharma","Al-Anon","meetings","sponsor"],"ask":["How do I help someone find a meeting that fits?","What's the difference between a sponsor and a peer specialist?"],"body":"Mutual-aid groups offer free, ongoing community support. Twelve-step fellowships such as Alcoholics Anonymous and Narcotics Anonymous use steps, sponsorship and meetings. SMART Recovery uses cognitive-behavioral tools and self-empowerment. Recovery Dharma draws on Buddhist practice; Celebrate Recovery is Christ-centered; Women for Sobriety, LifeRing and others exist too. Al-Anon, Alateen and Nar-Anon support families. Mental-health peer groups and clubhouses serve people with mental-health conditions.\n\nHelp people try several and find a fit — format, size, online or in-person, cultural identity, medication friendliness. A sponsor is a volunteer guide within a fellowship; a peer specialist is a trained, paid member of a care team with professional boundaries and documentation duties. Both can coexist in someone's life."},{"id":"peer-support#23","title":"Documentation for peer specialists","tags":["documentation","notes","DAP","SOAP","BIRP","GIRP","peer notes","charting"],"ask":["Which note format should I use?","How do I write about a session without sounding clinical?"],"body":"Peer notes record what happened, what support you gave, how the person responded and what's next. Common formats: DAP (data, assessment, plan), SOAP (subjective, objective, assessment, plan), BIRP (behavior, intervention, response, plan) and GIRP (goal, intervention, response, plan). Use whatever your program requires.\n\nWrite promptly, objectively and respectfully. Use the person's own words in quotes for their goals and feelings. Describe peer interventions concretely (purposeful sharing, goal setting, resource linkage). Note strengths, not only problems. Avoid diagnoses and clinical judgments — your \"assessment\" section is your peer observations. Never include anything you wouldn't want the person to read; many people can request their records. Document safety concerns and who you reported them to, with the time."},{"id":"peer-support#24","title":"Working on a clinical team","tags":["team","role clarity","integration","clinical team","collaboration","peer role drift"],"ask":["What do I do when staff ask me to do tasks outside my role?","How do I share a peer perspective in treatment team?"],"body":"Peers bring a perspective that clinical staff can't: lived experience and a relationship built on mutuality. Teams work best when everyone understands the peer role. Role drift — being pulled into tasks like enforcing rules, monitoring, transporting or clinical duties that undermine trust — is a common challenge. Clarify expectations with your supervisor and use your job description.\n\nIn treatment team meetings, offer the person's perspective (with their permission), their stated goals and strengths, and any barriers you see. Ask questions that center recovery (\"What does the person want?\"). Build relationships with nurses, techs and clinicians; small acts of reliability earn credibility. If you also work on the same unit in another role, be explicit with participants about which hat you're wearing."},{"id":"peer-support#25","title":"Self-care, compassion fatigue and vicarious trauma","tags":["self-care","burnout","compassion fatigue","vicarious trauma","secondary trauma","wellness"],"ask":["How do I know if I'm burning out?","How does a peer protect their own recovery at work?"],"body":"Helping work brings compassion satisfaction — the good feeling of making a difference — and costs: burnout (exhaustion and frustration often tied to workload and systems), and secondary or vicarious trauma (absorbing others' trauma, with intrusive images, avoidance or changed worldview). Peers face an added risk: work can stir up their own history.\n\nProtect your recovery deliberately: keep your own supports and practices, use supervision, notice triggers, take real breaks and time off, and have a transition ritual at the end of a shift. Signs to act on include dreading work, irritability, numbness, sleep changes, increased use of substances and pulling away from people. Talk to your supervisor or EAP early. BlackLab's after-shift check-in tracks these over time."},{"id":"peer-support#26","title":"Cultural humility","tags":["cultural humility","culture","diversity","equity","identity","inclusion"],"ask":["How is cultural humility different from cultural competence?","How do I ask about someone's culture respectfully?"],"body":"Cultural humility is a lifelong practice of self-reflection about your own culture and biases, learning from the people you support about theirs, and addressing power imbalances. It goes beyond \"competence\" — you can't fully know another person's culture, but you can stay curious and respectful.\n\nAsk open questions: \"What's important for me to know about you and your background?\" \"How does your family or community think about mental health or recovery?\" Respect language preferences, names and pronouns, faith practices and traditional healing. Recognize historical and systemic trauma, including racism and discrimination in health systems. Notice when your own experience doesn't match someone else's, and avoid assuming your path generalizes. Connect people to culturally specific resources when they want them."},{"id":"peer-support#27","title":"First meetings and building rapport","tags":["engagement","rapport","first session","introduction","trust","outreach"],"ask":["What do I say when I first meet someone?","How do I engage someone who doesn't want to talk?"],"body":"Start by introducing your role clearly: \"I'm a peer support specialist. That means I have my own experience with recovery, and I'm here to support you — not to evaluate you.\" Explain confidentiality and its limits. Ask what they'd find helpful and what they'd like to be called.\n\nLet the person set the pace. Some days engagement is a short, friendly check-in, sitting together, sharing a coffee or simply being reliable — showing up when you said you would. Notice interests and strengths. With someone guarded, low-pressure contact and consistency build trust over time. Avoid starting with forms or problems. A good first meeting ends with something small and concrete: a next time to meet, one question they want answered, or one thing they want to try."},{"id":"peer-support#28","title":"Transitions and post-discharge support","tags":["discharge","transition","post-discharge","follow-up","aftercare","bridge"],"ask":["Why is the time after discharge so risky?","What should a discharge plan include from a peer's view?"],"body":"The days and weeks after leaving inpatient or residential care are a period of higher risk for crisis, suicide, return to use and overdose — tolerance can drop quickly after time without substances. Peers can bridge this gap by building the relationship before discharge and following up soon after.\n\nHelpful pieces of a transition: a confirmed first appointment (ideally within days), a plan for medications and how to get them, a safe place to stay, transportation, crisis contacts including 988, a list of supports and meetings, naloxone if there's opioid risk, and a plan for the first difficult evening. A warm handoff — introducing the person to the next provider or community peer — works better than a phone number on paper. Follow up as agreed and document each contact."},{"id":"peer-support#29","title":"Goal setting that sticks","tags":["goals","SMART goals","action planning","recovery planning","steps"],"ask":["How do I turn a big goal into a first step?","What if the goal seems unrealistic to me?"],"body":"Recovery goals belong to the person and should be written in their words. Big goals (\"get my life back\") are valuable as direction; break them into small, specific steps that can be done soon. SMART is a handy check: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound — \"call the housing office Tuesday morning\" rather than \"work on housing.\"\n\nAsk what might get in the way and plan for it. Identify who can help. Start with something likely to succeed to build momentum. If a goal seems unrealistic, explore it with curiosity rather than discouraging it; often there's a meaningful first step. Review goals regularly, celebrate progress, and treat setbacks as information. BlackLab's participant page tracks goals by life area with checkable steps and target dates."},{"id":"peer-support#30","title":"Supervision for peer workers","tags":["supervision","peer supervision","supervisor","reflective practice","ethics consultation"],"ask":["What should I bring to supervision?","What's the difference between administrative and peer supervision?"],"body":"Supervision supports your growth, ethics and wellbeing. Administrative supervision covers schedules, policy and productivity; clinical or practice supervision covers how you work with people; peer supervision — ideally from someone who understands the peer role — focuses on staying true to peer values. Many certifications require a set number of supervised hours.\n\nBring specific situations: a boundary question, a disclosure you're unsure about, a hard conversation, a team conflict, a reaction you had. Bring wins too. Ask for feedback on documentation. Keep a simple log of date, length, supervisor and topics — BlackLab's supervision log totals hours by type and flags which count toward your credential. Good supervision is collaborative and safe; if it isn't, it's okay to ask for what you need."},{"id":"peer-support#31","title":"Peer certification and continuing education","tags":["certification","credential","CPSS","CPRS","NCPS","CEUs","renewal","ethics hours"],"ask":["How do I keep my certification current?","What counts as continuing education?"],"body":"Peer certification is usually granted by a state certification board or a state behavioral health agency, and requirements vary: a training curriculum, supervised work or volunteer hours, an exam, a code of ethics and a renewal cycle with continuing education (often including ethics hours). National options include NAADAC's National Certified Peer Recovery Support Specialist credential, and some states use IC&RC's peer recovery exam.\n\nKeep copies of certificates and training records, and track CEUs as you earn them rather than at renewal time. BlackLab's Education section tracks credentials with renewal reminders at 90, 30 and 7 days and keeps a CEU log. Good CEU topics for peers include ethics, trauma-informed care, motivational interviewing, suicide prevention, cultural humility, harm reduction and WRAP facilitation."}]},{"id":"software-development","title":"Software Development","kind":"DOMAIN","summary":"The craft day to day — clean code, testing, debugging, Kotlin and Android, tools, delivery and growing as a developer.","icon":"","starters":[],"draws":[],"entries":[{"id":"software-development#1","title":"Clean code essentials","tags":["clean code","naming","functions","readability","comments"],"ask":["How long should a function be?","When are comments worth writing?"],"body":"Code is read far more than it's written, so optimize for the reader.\n- Names reveal intent: overdueWorkOrders beats list2; avoid abbreviations and noise words (data, info, manager). Booleans read as questions (isOverdue, hasPermission).\n- Functions do one thing at one level of abstraction. Short is a side effect of focus, not a goal; a 30-line function that reads top to bottom is fine.\n- Few parameters; group related ones into an object. Avoid boolean flag arguments that switch behavior — split into two functions.\n- Prefer early returns over deep nesting.\n- Comments explain why, not what: business rules, surprising constraints, links to tickets or specs. If a comment explains what the code does, try renaming instead.\n- Consistency beats personal preference: follow the codebase's conventions and let a formatter (ktlint, Prettier, Black) settle style debates.\nReadable code is also the cheapest defense against bugs."},{"id":"software-development#2","title":"Refactoring","tags":["refactoring","code smells","technical debt","safe changes"],"ask":["What are the most common code smells?","How do you refactor without breaking things?"],"body":"Refactoring changes the structure of code without changing its behavior, in small, verifiable steps. Martin Fowler's catalog names the moves: Extract Function, Inline, Rename, Move, Extract Class, Replace Conditional with Polymorphism, Introduce Parameter Object, and more.\n\nCode smells that suggest refactoring: long functions, large classes, duplicated logic, long parameter lists, feature envy (a method more interested in another class's data), primitive obsession (strings for everything), shotgun surgery, divergent change, and comments covering for unclear code.\n\nSafety rules: have tests first (write characterization tests for legacy code); make one change at a time and run tests after each; commit often so you can roll back; use the IDE's automated refactorings; and never mix refactoring with behavior changes in the same commit — reviewers can't tell which is which. The \"make the change easy, then make the easy change\" approach (Kent Beck) is the everyday version."},{"id":"software-development#3","title":"Testing strategy: unit, integration and end-to-end","tags":["testing","unit tests","integration tests","end-to-end","test pyramid","mocks"],"ask":["How many tests are enough?","When should I use mocks?"],"body":"The test pyramid suggests many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests and a handful of end-to-end tests. The \"testing trophy\" variant emphasizes integration tests because they catch more real bugs per test. Either way the goal is confidence per minute of test time.\n\n- Unit tests check one piece of logic in isolation, run in milliseconds and pinpoint failures.\n- Integration tests check real collaborators together — your code with a real database (Testcontainers, an in-memory Room database) or HTTP layer.\n- End-to-end tests drive the whole system like a user. They're slow and flaky-prone, so cover only critical journeys.\n\nTest doubles: stubs return canned answers, fakes are working lightweight implementations (an in-memory repository), mocks verify interactions. Prefer fakes and real objects; over-mocking couples tests to implementation and makes refactoring painful. Good tests are deterministic, independent, readable (Arrange-Act-Assert) and test behavior, not private details. Coverage is a flashlight, not a target."},{"id":"software-development#4","title":"Test-driven development and BDD","tags":["TDD","BDD","red green refactor","Gherkin","test first"],"ask":["Is TDD worth it?","What does a good first test look like?"],"body":"Test-driven development cycles red, green, refactor: write a small failing test, write the simplest code that passes it, then clean up while tests stay green. Benefits: designs that are naturally testable and decoupled, a fast feedback loop, and a regression suite as a by-product. It shines for business logic, parsers, calculations and bug fixes (write the test that reproduces the bug first). It's less useful for exploratory UI work or spikes where you don't know the shape yet.\n\nTips: start with the simplest case, take small steps, and use the tests to discover the API you wish you had.\n\nBehavior-driven development (BDD) frames tests as examples of behavior in shared language — Given a work order due yesterday, When the daily check runs, Then it's marked overdue. Tools like Cucumber turn Gherkin scenarios into executable tests, but the bigger win is the conversation with stakeholders that produces clear examples."},{"id":"software-development#5","title":"Debugging methodically","tags":["debugging","bugs","git bisect","root cause","troubleshooting"],"ask":["What do you do when you can't reproduce a bug?","How does git bisect work?"],"body":"Debugging is the scientific method applied to code.\n- Reproduce reliably, ideally with an automated test. Capture exact inputs, versions and environment.\n- Read the actual error and stack trace slowly; the answer is often there.\n- Form a hypothesis, predict what you'll see, then test it with a debugger, logs or assertions. Change one thing at a time.\n- Narrow the search: binary search through code paths, inputs or history. git bisect finds the commit that introduced a bug by testing halfway points between a good and a bad commit — automate it with git bisect run.\n- Question assumptions: is that code even running? Is the config what you think? Is the cache stale?\n- Explain the problem out loud (rubber-duck debugging); it surfaces hidden assumptions.\n- When fixed, find the root cause, not just the symptom (Five Whys), add a regression test, and look for the same bug pattern elsewhere.\nFor intermittent bugs suspect concurrency, time, ordering, external services or resource limits, and add logging around the suspicious boundary."},{"id":"software-development#6","title":"Branching strategies","tags":["branching","trunk-based development","GitFlow","feature branches","version control"],"ask":["Trunk-based or GitFlow?","How long should a branch live?"],"body":"Trunk-based development: everyone integrates small changes into main at least daily, using short-lived branches and pull requests, with feature flags hiding unfinished work. It minimizes merge pain and is what DORA research associates with high performance. It needs good automated tests and CI.\n\nGitHub Flow is a lightweight version: branch from main, open a pull request, review, merge, deploy. Main is always deployable.\n\nGitFlow uses long-lived develop and main branches plus feature, release and hotfix branches. It suits software with scheduled, versioned releases and multiple supported versions (installed products, some mobile apps), but adds overhead and merge conflicts for continuously deployed services.\n\nWhatever the model: keep branches short-lived (days, not weeks), rebase or merge from main often, keep pull requests small, and protect main with required checks."},{"id":"software-development#7","title":"Code review that helps","tags":["code review","pull requests","feedback","collaboration"],"ask":["What should reviewers focus on?","How small should a pull request be?"],"body":"Code review exists to share knowledge, catch defects and keep the codebase coherent — not to prove who's smarter.\n\nFor authors: keep pull requests small (a few hundred lines at most), single-purpose, and well described — what, why, how it was tested, screenshots for UI. Review your own diff first. Separate refactoring from behavior changes.\n\nFor reviewers: prioritize correctness, security, design and readability over style (leave style to linters). Ask questions instead of issuing orders (\"What happens if this list is empty?\"). Label comment weight: blocking, suggestion, nit. Praise good work specifically. Review promptly — waiting days for a review kills flow. Approve when the change improves the codebase even if it isn't how you'd have written it.\n\nGoogle's guideline is a good north star: approve once the change definitely improves overall code health, even if it isn't perfect."},{"id":"software-development#8","title":"Continuous integration and delivery","tags":["CI","CD","continuous delivery","deployment","feature flags","canary","blue-green"],"ask":["What belongs in a CI pipeline?","Canary or blue-green deployments?"],"body":"Continuous integration means merging to main frequently with an automated build and test on every change; a broken build is fixed immediately. Continuous delivery keeps main always releasable, and continuous deployment releases every passing change automatically.\n\nA solid pipeline: build once, then promote the same artifact through environments; run fast checks first (lint, unit tests), then slower ones (integration, security scans); keep it under about ten minutes; cache dependencies; and make failures easy to read.\n\nRelease safely: feature flags decouple deploy from release; blue-green deployments switch traffic between two identical environments for instant rollback; canary releases send a small percentage of traffic to the new version and watch metrics before expanding; staged rollouts do the same for mobile apps. Database changes use the expand-and-contract pattern: add new columns, migrate, switch code, then remove old ones in a later release, so every step is backward compatible."},{"id":"software-development#9","title":"Dependencies and supply chain","tags":["dependencies","semantic versioning","lockfiles","SBOM","supply chain","security"],"ask":["How do you keep dependencies up to date safely?","What is an SBOM?"],"body":"Every dependency is code you didn't write but are responsible for. Before adding one, check maintenance activity, license, security history, size and whether a few lines of your own code would do.\n\nSemantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) signals breaking changes, features and fixes — but trust it with care. Commit lockfiles (package-lock.json, poetry.lock, Gradle lockfiles or version catalogs) for reproducible builds. Update regularly in small batches with automation (Dependabot, Renovate) and good tests; big-bang upgrades after two years are painful.\n\nSupply-chain risks include typosquatted packages, compromised maintainers, malicious install scripts and dependency confusion. Defenses: pin versions, verify checksums and signatures, use private registries or proxies, scan for known vulnerabilities, review new dependencies, and generate a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM, in SPDX or CycloneDX format) so you know what you ship. Frameworks such as SLSA describe levels of build integrity."},{"id":"software-development#10","title":"Kotlin essentials","tags":["Kotlin","null safety","data classes","sealed classes","extension functions","Android"],"ask":["What makes Kotlin better than Java?","When should I use a sealed class?"],"body":"Kotlin is concise, null-safe and fully interoperable with Java.\n- Null safety: types are non-null by default; String? may be null. Use ?. (safe call), ?: (Elvis) for defaults, and avoid !! except where you can prove non-null.\n- val is read-only, var is mutable — prefer val.\n- Data classes generate equals, hashCode, toString and copy; ideal for immutable state.\n- Sealed classes and interfaces model closed hierarchies (Loading, Success, Error); when expressions over them are exhaustive, so the compiler catches missing cases.\n- Extension functions add behavior to existing types without inheritance.\n- Scope functions: let (transform nullable values), apply (configure an object), also (side effects), run and with (compute in a context).\n- Collections: map, filter, groupBy, associateBy, fold, and sequences for lazy large pipelines.\n- Default and named arguments replace telescoping constructors.\n- Smart casts after is-checks, string templates, and expression-bodied functions keep code short.\nBlackLab itself is written in Kotlin with Jetpack Compose."},{"id":"software-development#11","title":"Kotlin coroutines and Flow","tags":["coroutines","Flow","StateFlow","structured concurrency","dispatchers","Kotlin"],"ask":["StateFlow or SharedFlow?","How does cancellation work in coroutines?"],"body":"Coroutines are lightweight, suspendable computations. suspend functions can pause without blocking a thread.\n\nStructured concurrency: coroutines launch inside a CoroutineScope (viewModelScope, lifecycleScope), and when the scope is cancelled, its children are cancelled too — no leaks. launch starts fire-and-forget work; async returns a Deferred for a result. coroutineScope waits for children and fails if one fails; supervisorScope lets siblings fail independently.\n\nDispatchers: Main for UI, IO for blocking I/O, Default for CPU-heavy work. Use withContext to switch. Cancellation is cooperative: suspending calls check it, and long loops should call ensureActive() or yield(). Don't swallow CancellationException.\n\nFlow is a cold asynchronous stream with operators (map, filter, combine, debounce, flatMapLatest). StateFlow holds a current value and is ideal for UI state; SharedFlow broadcasts events to multiple collectors without a current value. In Compose, collect with collectAsStateWithLifecycle so collection stops in the background."},{"id":"software-development#12","title":"Android app architecture","tags":["Android","architecture","ViewModel","Room","WorkManager","lifecycle","MVVM"],"ask":["How should an Android app be layered?","When do I use WorkManager?"],"body":"Google's recommended architecture has a UI layer (Compose screens plus ViewModels holding UI state), an optional domain layer (use cases for reusable business logic), and a data layer (repositories that own data sources such as Room databases, DataStore and network APIs).\n\nPrinciples: unidirectional data flow (state flows down, events flow up); a single source of truth for each piece of data; the UI observes state as StateFlow; and the ViewModel survives configuration changes.\n\nKey Jetpack pieces: Room for SQLite with compile-time checked queries and migrations; DataStore for preferences; WorkManager for deferrable, guaranteed background work that survives restarts; Navigation for screens; Hilt or manual dependency injection.\n\nRespect the lifecycle: don't hold Activity references in long-lived objects, stop collecting when not visible, and use foreground services only for user-visible ongoing work (recording, playback, navigation) with the right service type. Android also enforces background limits, runtime permissions and scoped storage; design around them early."},{"id":"software-development#13","title":"Jetpack Compose","tags":["Compose","Jetpack Compose","recomposition","state","side effects","Android UI"],"ask":["Why is my composable recomposing so much?","How do side effects work in Compose?"],"body":"Compose is Android's declarative UI toolkit: UI is a function of state, and when state changes, affected composables recompose.\n\nState: remember keeps values across recompositions; rememberSaveable survives configuration changes and process death; mutableStateOf makes reads observable. Hoist state to the lowest common parent and pass values down with event callbacks up, so composables are stateless and reusable.\n\nSide effects: LaunchedEffect runs suspend work tied to keys; DisposableEffect registers and cleans up listeners; rememberCoroutineScope launches from callbacks; SideEffect publishes to non-Compose code; derivedStateOf avoids recomposing on every change of a fast-moving input.\n\nPerformance: keep composables skippable by passing stable, immutable parameters; use keys in LazyColumn items; defer state reads (lambda-based modifiers like offset { }) to the layout or draw phase; avoid heavy work in composition; and check with the Layout Inspector's recomposition counts. Baseline profiles improve startup and scrolling smoothness."},{"id":"software-development#14","title":"Python for engineers","tags":["Python","virtual environments","typing","packaging","uv","idioms"],"ask":["How should I set up a Python project?","What are Python's must-know idioms?"],"body":"Python is the lingua franca of scripting, data and AI.\n- Isolate every project in a virtual environment. Modern tooling: uv (very fast installs, lockfiles and Python version management), or venv plus pip, or Poetry. Declare dependencies in pyproject.toml.\n- Use type hints and check them with mypy or pyright; add dataclasses or Pydantic models for structured data.\n- Idioms: list, dict and set comprehensions; enumerate and zip; context managers (with open(...)) for cleanup; f-strings; pathlib for paths; generators for streaming large data; unpacking.\n- Know the traps: mutable default arguments (def f(x=[])), late-binding closures in loops, and integer versus float division.\n- Concurrency: threads for I/O-bound work (the GIL limits CPU parallelism, though free-threaded builds are arriving), multiprocessing for CPU-bound work, asyncio for many concurrent network calls.\n- Quality tools: Ruff for linting and formatting, pytest for tests.\nFor facilities work, Python plus pandas is excellent for crunching meter readings, work-order exports and budgets."},{"id":"software-development#15","title":"JavaScript and TypeScript essentials","tags":["JavaScript","TypeScript","event loop","promises","async","Node.js"],"ask":["How does the JavaScript event loop work?","Why use TypeScript?"],"body":"JavaScript runs on a single thread with an event loop: synchronous code runs to completion; then microtasks (promise callbacks) run, then macrotasks (timers, I/O events). Long synchronous work freezes the UI, so break it up or move it to a Web Worker.\n\nAsync: promises represent future values; async/await makes them read like synchronous code. Run independent work concurrently with Promise.all (or Promise.allSettled when failures shouldn't cancel the rest) instead of awaiting in sequence. Always handle rejections.\n\nGotchas: == coerces types (use ===); this depends on how a function is called (arrow functions capture it lexically); floating-point math (0.1 + 0.2); and mutation through shared object references.\n\nTypeScript adds static types: interfaces and type aliases, union types and narrowing, generics, and discriminated unions for state machines. Enable strict mode. Types catch whole classes of bugs and make refactoring safe, and editors use them for autocompletion. Node.js runs JavaScript on servers; npm and pnpm manage packages."},{"id":"software-development#16","title":"SQL fluency","tags":["SQL","joins","window functions","CTE","query optimization","N+1"],"ask":["How do window functions work?","What is the N+1 query problem?"],"body":"SQL is worth mastering; it outlives most frameworks.\n- Joins: INNER keeps matches, LEFT keeps all rows from the left side, and FULL OUTER keeps both. Watch for row multiplication when joining one-to-many twice.\n- Aggregation: GROUP BY with COUNT, SUM, AVG; HAVING filters groups.\n- Common table expressions (WITH) make complex queries readable; recursive CTEs walk hierarchies such as building › floor › room › asset.\n- Window functions compute across related rows without collapsing them: ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY asset ORDER BY date DESC) finds the latest inspection per asset; LAG compares with the previous reading; running totals use SUM() OVER (ORDER BY ...).\n- Performance: read the plan with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE); add indexes for WHERE, JOIN and ORDER BY columns; avoid functions on indexed columns in filters; select only needed columns.\n- The N+1 problem: one query for a list, then one query per item. Fix it with a join, an IN query, or eager loading in the ORM.\nAlways use parameterized queries — never build SQL by concatenating user input."},{"id":"software-development#17","title":"Data structures and complexity","tags":["data structures","Big-O","complexity","hash map","tree","heap","graph"],"ask":["When should I use a heap?","Explain Big-O simply"],"body":"Big-O describes how cost grows with input size: O(1) constant, O(log n) logarithmic (binary search), O(n) linear, O(n log n) (good sorting), O(n²) quadratic (nested loops — fine at 100 items, painful at a million).\n\nCore structures:\n- Array or list: O(1) index access, O(n) search, appends amortized O(1).\n- Hash map and set: O(1) average lookup, insert and delete; the workhorse for de-duplication, counting and indexing.\n- Balanced tree (sorted map): O(log n) operations with ordering and range queries.\n- Heap (priority queue): O(1) peek at the min or max, O(log n) insert and remove; great for \"top K\", scheduling and Dijkstra.\n- Stack (LIFO) and queue or deque (FIFO): undo, parsing, BFS.\n- Graph (adjacency list): networks, dependencies, routes.\n- Trie: prefix search and autocomplete.\nPick the structure by the operations you need most often. Space complexity matters too, especially on phones."},{"id":"software-development#18","title":"Algorithm patterns","tags":["algorithms","two pointers","sliding window","BFS","DFS","dynamic programming","binary search"],"ask":["How do I recognize a dynamic programming problem?","Teach me the sliding window pattern"],"body":"Most problems are variations on a handful of patterns:\n- Two pointers: sorted arrays, pair sums, de-duplicating in place.\n- Sliding window: longest or shortest subarray or substring meeting a condition; expand right, shrink left.\n- Binary search: on sorted data, or on the answer itself (\"smallest capacity that works\").\n- BFS: shortest path in unweighted graphs, level-by-level processing. DFS: exploring all paths, connected components, cycle detection, topological sort (dependency ordering).\n- Dijkstra for weighted shortest paths; union-find for connectivity.\n- Dynamic programming: overlapping subproblems plus optimal substructure. Define the state, the recurrence and base cases; memoize top-down or fill a table bottom-up. Classic examples: knapsack, edit distance, longest increasing subsequence.\n- Greedy: make the locally best choice when you can prove it's globally optimal (interval scheduling).\n- Backtracking: permutations, combinations, constraint puzzles.\nPractice by naming the pattern before coding."},{"id":"software-development#19","title":"Error handling","tags":["error handling","exceptions","result types","fail fast","resilience"],"ask":["Exceptions or result types?","How do you write a good error message?"],"body":"Errors are part of the design, not an afterthought.\n- Fail fast on programmer errors (invalid state, broken invariants) with assertions or exceptions — hiding them spreads corruption.\n- Handle expected failures (network down, file missing, validation errors) explicitly. Kotlin's Result, sealed result types, or Either make the failure path visible in the type system; exceptions are fine when failure is truly exceptional.\n- Catch at the right level: where you can do something useful (retry, fall back, show a message). Don't catch and ignore. Don't catch broad Exception deep in libraries.\n- Preserve context: wrap low-level errors with what you were trying to do (\"Couldn't save work order 42: disk full\"), and keep the cause.\n- Error messages for users say what happened and what to do next, without stack traces or blame. Messages for developers include identifiers and values.\n- Clean up with finally, use, or try-with-resources so failures don't leak resources.\n- In coroutines, never swallow CancellationException."},{"id":"software-development#20","title":"Logging well","tags":["logging","structured logs","log levels","correlation ID","privacy"],"ask":["What log levels should I use?","What should never go in logs?"],"body":"Logs are for the person debugging at 2 a.m., often you.\n- Use structured logging (key-value or JSON) so logs are searchable: event=work_order_saved id=42 duration_ms=18.\n- Levels: ERROR for failures needing attention, WARN for recoverable oddities, INFO for significant business events, DEBUG for details off by default. Don't log errors you've handled as ERROR.\n- Include context: request or correlation IDs, user or tenant IDs (pseudonymous where possible), versions.\n- Never log secrets, passwords, tokens, full card numbers or sensitive personal data. Mask or omit them. Logs leak — they get copied to tools and tickets.\n- Log once, at the boundary where you handle the error, not at every layer.\n- Watch volume and cost: sample noisy debug logs, set retention policies.\n- On mobile, keep release builds quiet; logcat is readable by anyone with debugging access to the device."},{"id":"software-development#21","title":"Secure coding","tags":["secure coding","injection","XSS","secrets","cryptography","input validation"],"ask":["How do I store secrets safely?","What crypto mistakes are most common?"],"body":"- Validate input at trust boundaries: allow-lists over block-lists, with type, length and range checks.\n- Prevent injection: parameterized SQL, safe APIs for shell commands (no string concatenation), and context-aware output encoding against XSS. Use framework defaults that escape automatically.\n- Authorization on every server request; don't trust client-side checks or hidden fields.\n- Secrets never go in source code or repos. Use a secret manager or environment injection in CI; on Android, the Keystore for keys. Rotate secrets and scan repositories for leaks — BlackLab runs a secret scan before every push.\n- Cryptography: don't invent your own. Use high-level libraries (libsodium, Tink), AES-GCM for encryption, random IVs, a CSPRNG for tokens, Argon2 or bcrypt for passwords, and TLS everywhere. Never use MD5 or SHA-1 for security, or ECB mode.\n- Deserialize untrusted data carefully; prefer simple formats such as JSON with schemas.\n- Keep dependencies patched and run static analysis (CodeQL, Semgrep) in CI."},{"id":"software-development#22","title":"Performance in everyday code","tags":["performance","profiling","memory","allocation","optimization"],"ask":["What are easy performance wins?","When is premature optimization OK?"],"body":"Knuth's line is about small inefficiencies: premature optimization is the root of much evil, but choosing the right data structure and avoiding needless work isn't premature.\n\nEveryday wins:\n- Avoid repeated work: hoist invariant calculations out of loops, memoize expensive pure functions, cache parsed results.\n- Use the right structure: a set for membership tests instead of a list scan.\n- Batch I/O: one query with an IN clause instead of a hundred; bulk inserts inside one transaction.\n- Stream large data instead of loading it all into memory.\n- Reduce allocations in hot paths (on Android, avoid allocating inside onDraw or tight animation loops).\n- Do slow work off the main thread; keep the UI thread for UI.\n- Measure with real profilers: Android Studio profilers and Perfetto, async-profiler for the JVM, py-spy for Python, Chrome DevTools for JavaScript.\nWrite the clear version first, measure, then optimize the hot spot — usually a small fraction of the code."},{"id":"software-development#23","title":"Containers and Docker","tags":["Docker","containers","images","Dockerfile","multi-stage","compose"],"ask":["How do multi-stage builds work?","Containers versus virtual machines?"],"body":"Containers package an app with its dependencies into an image that runs the same everywhere. Unlike virtual machines, they share the host kernel, so they start in seconds and use little overhead.\n\nDockerfile tips:\n- Use small, trusted, pinned base images (official slim or distroless images).\n- Order layers from least to most frequently changing — copy dependency manifests and install before copying source — to maximize cache hits.\n- Multi-stage builds compile in one stage and copy only the artifacts into a minimal runtime stage.\n- Run as a non-root user; add a .dockerignore; don't bake secrets into images.\n- One main process per container; log to stdout and stderr.\n\nDocker Compose defines multi-container setups (app, database, cache) for local development with one command. Images are stored in registries such as Docker Hub or GitHub Container Registry. Scan images for vulnerabilities and rebuild regularly to pick up base-image patches."},{"id":"software-development#24","title":"Kubernetes basics","tags":["Kubernetes","k8s","pods","deployments","services","orchestration"],"ask":["Do I actually need Kubernetes?","What's the difference between a pod and a deployment?"],"body":"Kubernetes orchestrates containers across a cluster. Core objects:\n- Pod: one or more containers that share a network namespace; the smallest unit, and ephemeral.\n- Deployment: declares the desired number of pod replicas and handles rolling updates and rollbacks.\n- Service: a stable network address that load-balances across matching pods.\n- Ingress or Gateway: routes external HTTP traffic to services.\n- ConfigMap and Secret: configuration and credentials injected into pods.\n- StatefulSet for databases and other stateful apps; Job and CronJob for batch work.\n\nHealth checks matter: liveness probes restart stuck containers; readiness probes keep traffic away until a pod is ready. Set resource requests and limits so the scheduler can place pods well. You describe desired state in YAML (often templated with Helm or Kustomize) and controllers reconcile reality toward it.\n\nKubernetes is powerful but complex. For a small team or a single app, a managed platform (Cloud Run, App Service, Fly.io, a simple VM) is often the better choice."},{"id":"software-development#25","title":"Cloud fundamentals and infrastructure as code","tags":["cloud","AWS","Azure","GCP","serverless","Terraform","infrastructure as code"],"ask":["When does serverless make sense?","Why use Terraform?"],"body":"Cloud service models: IaaS gives you virtual machines and networks; PaaS runs your code with managed scaling (App Service, Cloud Run, Elastic Beanstalk); serverless functions (Lambda, Cloud Functions) run small handlers on demand and scale to zero; SaaS is finished software.\n\nServerless fits spiky, event-driven workloads and small teams; watch for cold starts, execution time limits, and costs at steady high load. Managed databases, queues and storage remove a great deal of operational toil.\n\nInfrastructure as code (Terraform or OpenTofu, Pulumi, AWS CDK, Bicep) declares infrastructure in versioned files. You get reviewable changes, reproducible environments, drift detection and disaster recovery. Keep state files secure and remote, use modules for reuse, and run plan in CI before apply.\n\nCost hygiene: tag resources by owner and project, set budgets and alerts, shut down idle environments, and right-size instances. The cloud bill is an architecture metric."},{"id":"software-development#26","title":"Linux and shell productivity","tags":["Linux","shell","bash","command line","grep","ssh","permissions"],"ask":["What shell commands should every developer know?","How do Linux file permissions work?"],"body":"The command line multiplies productivity.\n- Navigation and files: cd, ls -la, find, tree, du -sh, df -h.\n- Text: grep -rn (or ripgrep, rg), sed for substitutions, awk for columns, sort | uniq -c | sort -nr for counting, head and tail -f for live logs, jq for JSON.\n- Pipes and redirection: chain small tools with |, write with > and append with >>, send errors with 2>&1.\n- Processes: ps aux, top or htop, kill, background jobs, nohup or tmux for long-running sessions.\n- Networking: curl -v, ssh with keys (never passwords), scp or rsync, ss -tulpn to see listening ports.\n- Permissions: rwx for owner, group and others; chmod 600 for private keys, chmod +x for scripts; avoid chmod 777.\n- Scripting: start bash scripts with set -euo pipefail, quote variables (\"$file\"), and prefer Python once logic gets complex.\nHistory search (Ctrl+R), aliases and a good prompt pay off every day."},{"id":"software-development#27","title":"Developer productivity and flow","tags":["productivity","flow","IDE","automation","focus","tooling"],"ask":["How do I protect deep-work time?","What tools give the biggest productivity boost?"],"body":"Productivity comes from flow — long stretches of focused work — more than from typing speed.\n- Protect focus: block calendar time, batch meetings, silence notifications, and keep a scratch list for interrupting thoughts.\n- Master your IDE: navigation (go to definition, find usages), automated refactorings, multi-cursor editing, the debugger, and keyboard shortcuts. Ten minutes learning shortcuts saves hours.\n- Automate repetitive work: scripts, Makefile or task runners, snippets, code generation, pre-commit hooks.\n- Keep feedback loops fast: incremental builds, focused test runs, hot reload.\n- Use AI assistants well: for boilerplate, exploring unfamiliar APIs, writing tests and explaining code — but review everything, and keep your own understanding sharp.\n- Write things down: a work log, decision notes and command snippets turn into a personal knowledge base.\n- Energy matters: sleep, breaks and exercise are performance tools, not luxuries."},{"id":"software-development#28","title":"Writing documentation","tags":["documentation","README","Diátaxis","docs","technical writing"],"ask":["What makes a great README?","How do you keep docs from going stale?"],"body":"Documentation is a product for future readers, including you.\n\nThe Diátaxis framework sorts docs by need: tutorials (learning by doing), how-to guides (solving a specific task), reference (accurate, complete facts such as APIs and configuration), and explanation (background and why). Mixing them confuses readers.\n\nA strong README answers: what is this, why does it exist, how do I install and run it, how do I use it, how do I contribute, and where do I get help. Include a quick-start that works when copied and pasted.\n\nKeep docs alive: store them next to the code (docs as code), review them in pull requests, generate reference docs from code where possible, add runnable examples that CI tests, and delete obsolete pages — wrong docs are worse than none. Write plainly: short sentences, active voice, concrete examples, and headings people can scan."},{"id":"software-development#29","title":"Working with legacy code","tags":["legacy code","characterization tests","seams","maintenance","Michael Feathers"],"ask":["How do you get legacy code under test?","Rewrite or refactor?"],"body":"Michael Feathers defines legacy code as code without tests: you can't change it with confidence.\n\nStrategy:\n- Identify change points: where must you change, and what could break?\n- Find seams: places where you can alter behavior without editing the code there (constructor parameters, interfaces, overridable methods) so you can substitute test doubles.\n- Write characterization tests that capture what the code currently does, even if it's wrong, before changing anything.\n- Use sprout method or sprout class (write new, tested code and call it from the old code) and wrap method (wrap old behavior with new) to add features safely.\n- Break dependencies with small, mechanical, low-risk refactorings.\n\nRewriting from scratch is tempting but risky: the old code encodes years of bug fixes and edge cases. Prefer incremental replacement (strangler fig). Respect the code — it shipped and paid the bills — and leave each area a little better each visit."},{"id":"software-development#30","title":"Design patterns quick reference","tags":["design patterns","Gang of Four","strategy","observer","factory","adapter","decorator","builder"],"ask":["Which design patterns matter most today?","Strategy versus state pattern?"],"body":"Patterns are named solutions to recurring problems. The most useful today:\n- Strategy: swap algorithms behind an interface (pricing rules, export formats). In Kotlin often just a function parameter.\n- Observer: notify subscribers of changes — the root of listeners, Flow and reactive UI.\n- Factory and factory method: centralize object creation when the concrete type varies.\n- Builder: construct complex objects step by step; Kotlin's named and default arguments often replace it.\n- Adapter: make an existing interface fit the one you need (wrapping a vendor SDK).\n- Decorator: add behavior by wrapping (logging, caching or retry around a repository).\n- Facade: a simple interface over a complex subsystem.\n- Command: represent actions as objects (undo/redo, queued jobs).\n- State: an object's behavior changes with its state; sealed classes and state machines model it cleanly.\n- Repository: abstract data access behind a collection-like interface.\nPatterns are vocabulary, not goals. Reach for one when the problem appears, not in advance."},{"id":"software-development#31","title":"Functional programming ideas","tags":["functional programming","immutability","pure functions","higher-order functions"],"ask":["Why does immutability reduce bugs?","What's a pure function?"],"body":"Functional programming concepts improve code in any language.\n- Pure functions return the same output for the same input and have no side effects. They're trivial to test, cache and parallelize.\n- Immutability: values don't change after creation; you create new ones (Kotlin's copy on data classes). It eliminates whole classes of bugs from shared mutable state, especially with concurrency.\n- Higher-order functions take or return functions: map, filter, reduce, fold, flatMap. They replace manual loops with intent-revealing pipelines.\n- Composition: build behavior by combining small functions.\n- Algebraic data types: sealed classes (sum types) and data classes (product types) model domains precisely, and pattern matching with when handles every case.\n- Make illegal states unrepresentable: design types so invalid combinations can't be constructed.\n- Push side effects to the edges: a functional core with an imperative shell keeps business logic pure and I/O at the boundaries.\nYou don't need a purely functional language; Kotlin, TypeScript and Python support these styles well."},{"id":"software-development#32","title":"Reading an unfamiliar codebase","tags":["onboarding","code reading","new codebase","learning","navigation"],"ask":["Where do you start in a big codebase?","How do you ramp up on a new team fast?"],"body":"Reading code is a skill you can practice.\n- Start from the outside: README, architecture docs, build files and dependency manifests tell you the stack and structure.\n- Run it locally, then use it like a user. Behavior gives the code meaning.\n- Follow one request or feature end to end: entry point (main activity, route, controller), through services, to storage. Use go to definition and find usages heavily.\n- Read tests; they're executable documentation of intended behavior.\n- Use version control history: git log on key files, git blame for why a line exists, and recent pull requests for current conventions.\n- Draw a quick map of modules and data flow; you'll refine it.\n- Make a small, real change early (fix a typo, a tiny bug); shipping something builds context and confidence.\n- Ask focused questions after you've tried: \"I traced X to Y but couldn't find where Z happens — where should I look?\"\nAI assistants help summarize files and answer \"where is X handled?\", but verify against the code."},{"id":"software-development#33","title":"Growing from junior to senior and beyond","tags":["career","senior engineer","staff engineer","growth","mentoring","learning"],"ask":["What separates a senior engineer from a mid-level one?","How do I become a staff engineer?"],"body":"Levels differ mostly in scope and judgment, not raw coding speed.\n- Junior: completes well-defined tasks with guidance; learning the tools and codebase.\n- Mid-level: owns features end to end; works independently; writes solid, tested code.\n- Senior: owns ambiguous problems and systems; makes sound trade-offs; anticipates operational issues; raises the team's level through reviews and mentoring; communicates clearly with non-engineers.\n- Staff and principal: work across teams and multi-quarter horizons; set technical direction; find the most important problems; lead through influence rather than authority; write the documents that align people.\n\nHabits that accelerate growth: ship and finish things, take on-call and learn from incidents, write design documents, seek feedback, teach what you learn, read other people's code, and understand the business problem behind every ticket. A background in operations leadership — managing budgets, vendors, safety and people — maps directly onto senior and staff-level expectations: prioritizing, communicating trade-offs and owning outcomes."}]},{"id":"software-engineering","title":"Software Engineering","kind":"DOMAIN","summary":"Architecture, distributed systems, reliability, security and the trade-offs behind systems that last.","icon":"","starters":[],"draws":[],"entries":[{"id":"software-engineering#1","title":"What software engineering actually is","tags":["fundamentals","trade-offs","engineering","programming"],"ask":["How is engineering different from just coding?","What trade-offs come up most often?"],"body":"Programming is getting a computer to do something; software engineering is programming integrated over time and over people. The questions change from \"does it work?\" to \"will it still work, and can others change it safely, in three years?\" Three forces dominate: time (code must survive changing requirements, dependencies and platforms), scale (more users, more data, more engineers), and trade-offs (every decision buys something and costs something).\n\nGood engineers make trade-offs explicit: consistency versus availability, speed of delivery versus robustness, flexibility versus simplicity. They write down why a choice was made, measure instead of guessing, and prefer decisions that are cheap to reverse. A useful habit is asking \"what would make this decision wrong?\" before committing to it."},{"id":"software-engineering#2","title":"Requirements and quality attributes","tags":["requirements","non-functional","quality attributes","user stories","acceptance criteria"],"ask":["How do you write good acceptance criteria?","Which quality attributes matter most?"],"body":"Functional requirements say what the system does; quality attributes (non-functional requirements) say how well: performance, availability, security, scalability, maintainability, usability, cost. Architecture is mostly driven by the quality attributes, so make them measurable: \"p95 search latency under 300 ms at 200 requests per second,\" not \"fast.\"\n\nUser stories (\"As a facility manager, I want overdue work orders flagged so that nothing slips\") capture intent; acceptance criteria make them testable, often in Given/When/Then form. Separate the problem from the proposed solution, rank requirements (MoSCoW: must, should, could, won't), and identify constraints early: regulations, budget, existing systems, team skills. Most project failures trace back to misunderstood requirements, not bad code."},{"id":"software-engineering#3","title":"Modularity, coupling and cohesion","tags":["modularity","coupling","cohesion","design","boundaries"],"ask":["How do you spot tight coupling?","What makes a good module boundary?"],"body":"Cohesion measures how strongly the parts of a module belong together; coupling measures how much modules depend on each other. Aim for high cohesion and low coupling: a change to one business rule should touch one module.\n\nSigns of trouble: shotgun surgery (one change touches many files), modules that share database tables directly, circular dependencies, and \"utility\" packages that everything imports. Good boundaries follow business capabilities (billing, scheduling, inventory) rather than technical layers alone. Depend on stable abstractions, communicate through narrow interfaces, and keep data ownership clear — exactly one module writes a given piece of data. Connascence is a sharper vocabulary for coupling: prefer weak forms (name, type) over strong ones (timing, execution order, shared meaning of magic values)."},{"id":"software-engineering#4","title":"SOLID principles","tags":["SOLID","object-oriented","design principles","single responsibility","dependency inversion"],"ask":["Which SOLID principle matters most in practice?","Can SOLID be overdone?"],"body":"SOLID is five object-oriented design guidelines:\n- Single Responsibility: a class should have one reason to change (one stakeholder or concern).\n- Open/Closed: open for extension, closed for modification — add behavior with new code (strategies, plugins) rather than editing stable code.\n- Liskov Substitution: a subtype must work anywhere its base type is expected, without surprising callers.\n- Interface Segregation: many small, client-specific interfaces beat one fat interface.\n- Dependency Inversion: high-level policy depends on abstractions, not on low-level details like databases or HTTP clients.\n\nIn practice, Single Responsibility and Dependency Inversion pay off the most: they make code testable and keep infrastructure swappable. SOLID can be overdone — interfaces with one implementation and layers of indirection add cost. Apply it where change actually happens."},{"id":"software-engineering#5","title":"Deep modules and information hiding","tags":["abstraction","information hiding","deep modules","API design","complexity"],"ask":["What's a shallow module?","How do you reduce complexity in a codebase?"],"body":"John Ousterhout's \"A Philosophy of Software Design\" frames complexity as anything that makes a system hard to understand or modify, showing up as change amplification, cognitive load and unknown unknowns. His key idea is the deep module: a simple interface hiding a lot of functionality (a file system read call hides caching, scheduling and disk layout). Shallow modules have interfaces almost as complex as their implementation and add little value.\n\nInformation hiding means each module encapsulates design decisions likely to change — formats, algorithms, storage — so those decisions don't leak into callers. Other practical takeaways: define errors out of existence where possible (make operations naturally safe), pull complexity downward into the module rather than pushing it to every caller, and design it twice — sketch two alternatives before choosing."},{"id":"software-engineering#6","title":"Layered, hexagonal and clean architecture","tags":["architecture","hexagonal","ports and adapters","clean architecture","layers"],"ask":["When is hexagonal architecture worth it?","How do layers go wrong?"],"body":"Layered architecture stacks presentation, application, domain and data layers, each depending only on the one below. It's simple but tends to make everything depend on the database.\n\nHexagonal architecture (ports and adapters) puts the domain at the center. The domain defines ports — interfaces such as \"WorkOrderRepository\" or \"Notifier\" — and adapters implement them for the outside world (a SQL database, an email service, a REST controller, a test fake). Clean Architecture and Onion Architecture are close cousins with the same rule: source-code dependencies point inward, toward business rules.\n\nBenefits: domain logic is testable without infrastructure, and you can swap a database or UI without rewriting rules. Costs: more types and mapping. For small CRUD apps a well-organized layered design is fine; for long-lived systems with real business rules, ports and adapters pays back."},{"id":"software-engineering#7","title":"Monoliths, microservices and the modular monolith","tags":["microservices","monolith","modular monolith","service boundaries","architecture"],"ask":["When should a team move to microservices?","What is a modular monolith?"],"body":"A monolith is one deployable unit. It's simple to build, test, deploy and debug, and transactions are easy. Microservices split the system into independently deployable services that own their data. They allow independent scaling and team autonomy but bring distributed-systems costs: network failures, eventual consistency, versioning, observability, and operational overhead.\n\nMicroservices solve an organizational problem — many teams stepping on each other — more than a technical one. A common, healthy path: start with a modular monolith (strict internal module boundaries, separate schemas or table ownership, communication through interfaces), then extract a service only when there's a clear reason: a different scaling profile, a different release cadence, or a team boundary. Red flags: services that must deploy together, chatty synchronous call chains, and a shared database — the \"distributed monolith.\""},{"id":"software-engineering#8","title":"Domain-driven design","tags":["DDD","domain-driven design","bounded context","aggregates","ubiquitous language"],"ask":["How do you find bounded contexts?","What is an aggregate?"],"body":"Domain-driven design (DDD) aligns software structure with the business domain. Strategic DDD: build a ubiquitous language with domain experts and use the same words in code; split the domain into bounded contexts — areas where a model and its terms have one consistent meaning (\"asset\" in maintenance versus \"asset\" in accounting). Context maps describe how contexts relate (customer/supplier, anti-corruption layer, shared kernel).\n\nTactical DDD: entities have identity; value objects are defined by their values and are immutable (Money, Address); aggregates are consistency boundaries with one root entity that guards invariants, saved and loaded as a unit; domain events record that something meaningful happened (\"WorkOrderCompleted\"); repositories retrieve aggregates.\n\nKeep aggregates small, reference other aggregates by ID, and accept eventual consistency between them. DDD is worth it where the domain is complex; for simple CRUD it's overhead."},{"id":"software-engineering#9","title":"Event-driven architecture, event sourcing and CQRS","tags":["events","event-driven","event sourcing","CQRS","pub/sub"],"ask":["Events versus commands — what's the difference?","When does event sourcing make sense?"],"body":"In event-driven architecture, components publish events (\"PaymentReceived\") and others react, without the publisher knowing who listens. Commands are requests to do something and have one handler; events are facts about the past and can have many subscribers. This decouples services and enables scalable, asynchronous workflows, at the cost of harder tracing and eventual consistency.\n\nEvent sourcing stores state as an append-only log of events and rebuilds current state by replaying them. You get a perfect audit trail, temporal queries and the ability to build new read models later; the costs are schema evolution of events, replay performance (use snapshots) and a steeper learning curve.\n\nCQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) separates the write model from read models optimized for queries. It pairs naturally with event sourcing but doesn't require it. Use these patterns for audit-heavy or high-scale domains, not by default."},{"id":"software-engineering#10","title":"API design","tags":["API","REST","gRPC","GraphQL","versioning","idempotency","pagination"],"ask":["How should APIs be versioned?","REST, gRPC or GraphQL — which one?"],"body":"Good APIs are consistent, predictable and hard to misuse. For REST: model resources as nouns (/work-orders/123), use HTTP methods by meaning (GET safe, PUT and DELETE idempotent, POST for creation and actions), return proper status codes (400 validation, 401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404, 409 conflict, 429 rate limited), and give errors a consistent body with a machine-readable code.\n\nDesign for change: additive changes are safe; removals and renames break clients, so version explicitly (/v2 or a header) and deprecate with timelines. Make writes idempotent with idempotency keys so clients can retry safely. Paginate with cursors rather than offsets for large or changing collections. Document with OpenAPI.\n\ngRPC suits internal service-to-service calls (fast, typed, streaming). GraphQL suits client-driven data fetching across many entities, but needs care with caching, authorization and query cost limits. REST remains the safest default for public APIs."},{"id":"software-engineering#11","title":"Distributed systems fundamentals","tags":["distributed systems","CAP","PACELC","consistency","fallacies","network"],"ask":["Explain CAP in plain English","What are the fallacies of distributed computing?"],"body":"The fallacies of distributed computing are assumptions that bite: the network is reliable, latency is zero, bandwidth is infinite, the network is secure, topology doesn't change, there is one administrator, transport cost is zero, the network is homogeneous.\n\nCAP says that during a network partition a system must choose between consistency (every read sees the latest write) and availability (every request gets a response). PACELC extends it: Else, when there's no partition, you trade latency against consistency. Real systems pick per operation — a bank balance wants strong consistency; a \"likes\" counter can be eventually consistent.\n\nConsistency models range from linearizable (behaves like one copy) through sequential and causal to eventual consistency. Practical rules: assume any remote call can fail, time out or be duplicated; design operations to be idempotent; use timeouts everywhere; and never rely on synchronized clocks for ordering — use logical clocks, version numbers or a single leader."},{"id":"software-engineering#12","title":"Replication and consensus","tags":["replication","consensus","Raft","Paxos","quorum","leader election"],"ask":["How does Raft work?","What does a quorum guarantee?"],"body":"Replication keeps copies of data on several machines for availability and read scale. Leader/follower replication sends all writes through one leader; it's simple but the leader is a bottleneck and failover is tricky. Multi-leader and leaderless (Dynamo-style) designs accept writes in several places and must resolve conflicts.\n\nQuorums: with N replicas, writing to W and reading from R where W + R > N guarantees a read overlaps the latest write. Typical: N=3, W=2, R=2.\n\nConsensus algorithms such as Raft and Paxos let a cluster agree on a value or log despite failures, as long as a majority is up. Raft elects a leader with randomized timeouts; the leader appends entries to followers and commits once a majority acknowledges. Consensus underpins etcd, Consul, ZooKeeper and the control planes of Kubernetes and many databases. Rule of thumb: use a proven implementation; never write your own consensus for production."},{"id":"software-engineering#13","title":"Scalability, partitioning and statelessness","tags":["scalability","sharding","partitioning","horizontal scaling","stateless","load balancing"],"ask":["How do you choose a shard key?","Vertical or horizontal scaling first?"],"body":"Vertical scaling (a bigger machine) is simple and often the right first step. Horizontal scaling (more machines) needs work: keep application servers stateless — sessions in a shared store or signed tokens — so a load balancer can send any request anywhere.\n\nData is the hard part. Read replicas scale reads. Partitioning (sharding) scales writes and storage by splitting data across nodes. Choose a shard key that spreads load evenly and keeps related data together (tenant ID for a SaaS app). Avoid keys that create hot spots, like timestamps or a single big customer. Consistent hashing reduces data movement when nodes join or leave. Cross-shard queries and transactions get expensive, so design access patterns first.\n\nAlso scale with asynchrony: queues absorb bursts, background workers smooth load, and caches and CDNs remove work entirely. Measure before scaling — most systems hit database limits first."},{"id":"software-engineering#14","title":"Caching strategies","tags":["caching","cache invalidation","CDN","cache-aside","TTL","Redis"],"ask":["How do you handle cache invalidation?","What's a cache stampede?"],"body":"Caching trades freshness for speed. Common patterns:\n- Cache-aside (lazy loading): the app checks the cache, falls back to the database, then fills the cache. Most common.\n- Read-through and write-through: the cache layer loads and writes to the database itself; write-through keeps the cache fresh at the cost of write latency.\n- Write-behind: writes go to the cache and are flushed later — fast but risky on failure.\n\nInvalidation strategies: time-to-live (simple, bounded staleness), explicit delete on write, or versioned keys. Watch for the cache stampede: when a hot key expires, thousands of requests hit the database at once. Fixes include request coalescing (single flight), early probabilistic refresh, and jittered TTLs. Cache at many levels — browser, CDN, application, database buffer pool — and always measure hit rate. A cache that hides a slow query can become a hidden dependency: if it empties, can the database survive?"},{"id":"software-engineering#15","title":"Databases, indexes and transactions","tags":["databases","SQL","NoSQL","indexes","transactions","isolation levels","ACID"],"ask":["When should I pick NoSQL over Postgres?","What do isolation levels actually prevent?"],"body":"Relational databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server) offer schemas, joins, constraints and ACID transactions; they're the right default for most business data. NoSQL families fit specific shapes: key-value (Redis, DynamoDB) for simple lookups at scale, document stores (MongoDB) for nested records, wide-column (Cassandra) for huge write volumes, graph databases for relationship-heavy queries, time-series databases for sensor and metrics data.\n\nIndexes speed reads but slow writes and use space. B-tree indexes support equality and range queries; put the most selective, equality-filtered columns first in composite indexes, and check plans with EXPLAIN.\n\nIsolation levels trade correctness for concurrency. Read Committed prevents dirty reads; Repeatable Read prevents non-repeatable reads; Serializable prevents anomalies like write skew. Many bugs come from read-modify-write races — use row locks (SELECT … FOR UPDATE), atomic updates or optimistic concurrency with a version column."},{"id":"software-engineering#16","title":"Queues, messaging and delivery guarantees","tags":["queues","messaging","Kafka","RabbitMQ","outbox","idempotency","dead letter"],"ask":["Is exactly-once delivery real?","What is the transactional outbox?"],"body":"Message queues (RabbitMQ, SQS) distribute work to consumers; logs (Kafka, Kinesis) keep an ordered, replayable stream that many consumers read at their own pace.\n\nDelivery guarantees: at-most-once can lose messages; at-least-once can duplicate them; \"exactly-once\" in practice means at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing (deduplicate by message ID, or make the effect naturally idempotent). Ordering usually holds only within a partition or key.\n\nThe dual-write problem — updating a database and publishing a message — can't be made atomic directly. The transactional outbox pattern writes the event into an outbox table in the same transaction as the data, and a relay publishes it afterward. Use dead-letter queues for messages that keep failing, with alerting and a replay path. Monitor consumer lag; a growing backlog is an early warning."},{"id":"software-engineering#17","title":"SLIs, SLOs and error budgets","tags":["reliability","SRE","SLO","SLI","error budget","availability"],"ask":["How do you pick a good SLO?","What happens when the error budget runs out?"],"body":"Site Reliability Engineering defines reliability from the user's point of view. A Service Level Indicator (SLI) is a measurement, such as the percentage of requests that succeed within 300 ms. A Service Level Objective (SLO) is the target for that indicator over a window — 99.9% over 28 days. An SLA is a contractual promise with penalties, and should be looser than the SLO.\n\nThe error budget is the allowed unreliability: 99.9% leaves 0.1%, about 40 minutes of downtime in 28 days. While budget remains, teams ship features; when it's spent, they prioritize reliability work. This turns arguments about \"stability versus velocity\" into data.\n\nChoose few SLIs that reflect user pain (availability, latency, freshness, correctness), measure as close to the user as possible, and avoid 100% targets — they're impossible and freeze change. Each extra nine costs roughly ten times more."},{"id":"software-engineering#18","title":"Resilience patterns","tags":["resilience","retries","backoff","circuit breaker","timeouts","bulkhead","rate limiting"],"ask":["How do retries make outages worse?","When do you use a circuit breaker?"],"body":"Failures are normal; resilience limits their blast radius.\n- Timeouts on every remote call, shorter than the caller's own timeout.\n- Retries only for transient errors and idempotent operations, with exponential backoff and jitter so clients don't retry in sync. Cap total attempts and use a retry budget — naive retries multiply load during an outage (retry storms).\n- Circuit breakers stop calling a failing dependency for a while and fail fast, then probe with a few requests (half-open) before closing again.\n- Bulkheads isolate resources (separate thread or connection pools) so one slow dependency can't exhaust everything.\n- Rate limiting and load shedding protect a service from overload; prefer serving some requests well over all requests badly.\n- Backpressure lets consumers signal producers to slow down.\n- Graceful degradation: serve cached or partial results when a dependency is down.\nTest these with fault injection and game days, not just in theory."},{"id":"software-engineering#19","title":"Observability: logs, metrics and traces","tags":["observability","monitoring","logging","metrics","tracing","OpenTelemetry","RED","USE"],"ask":["What should I alert on?","Logs, metrics or traces — where do I start?"],"body":"Monitoring tells you whether known things are wrong; observability lets you ask new questions about unknown problems. Three signals:\n- Metrics: cheap numeric time series for dashboards and alerts.\n- Logs: detailed, structured events (JSON with request IDs) for investigation.\n- Traces: the path of one request across services, with timing for each span.\n\nUseful frameworks: RED for services (Rate, Errors, Duration) and USE for resources (Utilization, Saturation, Errors). The four golden signals are latency, traffic, errors and saturation. OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral standard for emitting all three signals.\n\nAlert on symptoms users feel (SLO burn rate, error rate, latency), not on every cause (high CPU). Every alert should be actionable and link to a runbook. Watch cardinality — labels like user ID explode metrics cost. Propagate a correlation ID through every hop so logs and traces join up."},{"id":"software-engineering#20","title":"Security engineering and threat modeling","tags":["security","threat modeling","STRIDE","OWASP","least privilege","defense in depth"],"ask":["How do you run a quick threat model?","What's on the OWASP Top 10?"],"body":"Threat modeling asks four questions: what are we building, what can go wrong, what will we do about it, and did we do a good job? Draw a data-flow diagram with trust boundaries, then walk STRIDE: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege.\n\nCore principles: least privilege (minimal permissions for people, services and tokens), defense in depth (several independent controls), secure defaults, fail closed, minimize attack surface, and never trust input crossing a trust boundary.\n\nThe OWASP Top 10 for web apps covers broken access control (the most common), cryptographic failures, injection, insecure design, security misconfiguration, vulnerable and outdated components, identification and authentication failures, software and data integrity failures, logging and monitoring failures, and server-side request forgery. Bake security into the pipeline: dependency scanning, secret scanning, static analysis, and code review checklists."},{"id":"software-engineering#21","title":"Authentication and authorization","tags":["authentication","authorization","OAuth","OIDC","JWT","RBAC","sessions"],"ask":["OAuth versus OpenID Connect?","Are JWTs a good idea for sessions?"],"body":"Authentication proves who you are; authorization decides what you may do. Don't build either from scratch when a vetted provider or library exists.\n\nOAuth 2.0 is a delegation protocol: it lets an app obtain an access token to call an API on a user's behalf. OpenID Connect adds identity on top — an ID token describing the user — and is what \"Sign in with Google\" uses. For browser and mobile apps, use the Authorization Code flow with PKCE; the implicit flow is deprecated.\n\nJWTs are signed, self-contained tokens. They're great for short-lived access tokens between services, but awkward as long-lived sessions because revocation is hard. Validate the signature, issuer, audience and expiry, and never accept \"alg: none.\"\n\nAuthorization models: role-based (RBAC) is simple; attribute-based (ABAC) and relationship-based (ReBAC, as in Google Zanzibar) handle finer rules. Enforce authorization on the server for every request — hidden buttons are not security."},{"id":"software-engineering#22","title":"Performance engineering","tags":["performance","latency","percentiles","profiling","Amdahl","load testing"],"ask":["Why do percentiles matter more than averages?","How do you find a bottleneck?"],"body":"Measure before optimizing. Averages hide pain; use percentiles — p50 for typical experience, p95 and p99 for the tail that users remember. In fan-out systems tail latency compounds: if one request touches 100 backends, a 1% slow rate per backend makes most requests slow.\n\nApproach: define the goal, build a reproducible benchmark or load test, profile (CPU, memory, I/O, locks), fix the biggest bottleneck, and re-measure. Amdahl's law limits speedup by the part you can't parallelize. Little's law (L = λ × W) relates concurrency, throughput and latency and is handy for sizing pools.\n\nCommon wins: fewer round trips (batching, avoiding N+1 queries), the right indexes, caching, streaming instead of buffering, connection pooling, and doing work asynchronously. Know rough latency numbers: memory ~100 ns, SSD read ~100 µs, same-datacenter round trip ~0.5 ms, cross-continent ~100+ ms."},{"id":"software-engineering#23","title":"Technical debt","tags":["technical debt","refactoring","maintenance","prioritization"],"ask":["How do you convince leadership to pay down debt?","Is all technical debt bad?"],"body":"Technical debt is the future cost of choosing an easier solution now. Like financial debt it isn't automatically bad; deliberate, prudent debt (\"ship now, refactor after launch\") can be smart. Martin Fowler's quadrant separates deliberate from inadvertent and prudent from reckless debt.\n\nInterest shows up as slower feature delivery, more bugs, onboarding pain and fear of change. To manage it: make it visible (a debt register with impact and effort), tie it to business outcomes (\"this module causes 40% of incidents\"), and pay it continuously — leave code better than you found it, reserve a steady slice of capacity, and refactor in the area you're about to change anyway.\n\nPrioritize debt by interest rate: code that changes often and causes pain matters; ugly code that nobody touches can wait. Hotspot analysis (change frequency times complexity from version history) finds where to start."},{"id":"software-engineering#24","title":"Documenting architecture: ADRs and the C4 model","tags":["documentation","ADR","architecture decision records","C4 model","diagrams"],"ask":["What goes in an ADR?","How detailed should architecture diagrams be?"],"body":"Architecture Decision Records capture significant decisions in short documents stored with the code: title, status, context (forces at play), decision, and consequences (good and bad). They answer the most common question new engineers ask — \"why is it like this?\" — and prevent re-litigating settled choices. Superseded ADRs stay as history.\n\nThe C4 model gives diagrams consistent zoom levels: System Context (the system, its users and external systems), Containers (deployable units such as apps, databases and queues), Components (major parts inside a container), and Code (optional, usually generated). Most teams only need the first two or three. Keep diagrams as code (Structurizr, Mermaid, PlantUML) so they're versioned and reviewed.\n\nGood documentation is minimal and maintained: README for getting started, ADRs for decisions, runbooks for operations, and diagrams for orientation."},{"id":"software-engineering#25","title":"Estimation and planning under uncertainty","tags":["estimation","planning","forecasting","story points","uncertainty"],"ask":["How do you forecast without story points?","Why are estimates always wrong?"],"body":"Software estimates are uncertain because much of the work is discovery. The cone of uncertainty shows estimates can be off by a factor of four early in a project, narrowing as unknowns are resolved.\n\nBetter practices: break work into small, similar-sized pieces; estimate in ranges with confidence (\"80% likely within 3–5 weeks\"); identify the riskiest unknowns and spike them first; and track actual throughput. Probabilistic forecasting with Monte Carlo simulation over historical throughput (items finished per week) often beats story points and needs less ceremony.\n\nCommunicate estimates as forecasts, not promises, and update them as you learn. Protect scope, not just dates: fixed date plus fixed scope plus fixed quality rarely works, so agree on what flexes. Hofstadter's law applies — it always takes longer than you expect, even when you account for Hofstadter's law."},{"id":"software-engineering#26","title":"Engineering metrics: DORA and SPACE","tags":["DORA","metrics","deployment frequency","lead time","SPACE","productivity"],"ask":["What are the DORA metrics?","How do you measure developer productivity without gaming it?"],"body":"The DORA research (Accelerate) found four key metrics that predict software delivery performance: deployment frequency, lead time for changes (commit to production), change failure rate, and time to restore service. Elite teams deploy on demand with lead times under a day, low failure rates and fast recovery — speed and stability go together. DORA later added reliability as a fifth area.\n\nThe SPACE framework reminds us productivity is multi-dimensional: Satisfaction and well-being, Performance (outcomes), Activity, Communication and collaboration, and Efficiency and flow. Use several metrics across dimensions, at team level, for learning rather than for ranking individuals.\n\nGoodhart's law warns that when a measure becomes a target it stops being a good measure: lines of code, commit counts and story points are easily gamed. Pair quantitative metrics with qualitative signals such as developer surveys and retrospectives."},{"id":"software-engineering#27","title":"Concurrency fundamentals","tags":["concurrency","threads","race condition","deadlock","async","locks","actors"],"ask":["How do you avoid deadlocks?","Async or threads — which is better?"],"body":"Concurrency is dealing with many things at once; parallelism is doing many things at once. Shared mutable state is the root of most concurrency bugs.\n- Race condition: the result depends on timing, typically a read-modify-write that isn't atomic.\n- Deadlock: threads wait on each other forever. Prevent it by always acquiring locks in a consistent global order, using timeouts, or avoiding nested locks.\n- Starvation and livelock: threads never make progress despite being active.\n\nTools: mutexes and read-write locks, atomic variables, thread-safe collections, immutable data, message passing (actors, channels), and confinement — keep state owned by one thread.\n\nAsync/await (Kotlin coroutines, JavaScript promises, Python asyncio) handles many I/O-bound tasks on few threads; it doesn't make CPU-bound work faster. Structured concurrency ties task lifetimes to scopes so nothing leaks. Rule: minimize shared state, and when sharing is unavoidable, make the synchronization obvious and testable."},{"id":"software-engineering#28","title":"Batch and stream data processing","tags":["data engineering","batch","streaming","pipelines","ETL","Kafka","idempotent"],"ask":["Batch or streaming — how do you choose?","How do you make pipelines rerunnable?"],"body":"Batch processing handles bounded data on a schedule (nightly ETL, monthly reports); it's simple, cheap and easy to rerun. Stream processing handles unbounded data continuously (sensor alerts, fraud detection) with low latency but more complexity: event time versus processing time, windows, late data and state management.\n\nClassic patterns: Lambda architecture runs batch and streaming paths side by side; Kappa architecture uses one streaming path with replay from a log such as Kafka. Many teams now use the medallion approach in a lakehouse: raw (bronze), cleaned (silver) and business-ready (gold) layers.\n\nMake pipelines idempotent and rerunnable: partition outputs by date, overwrite partitions instead of appending blindly, and track high-water marks. Validate data contracts at boundaries (schemas, null rates, ranges), because bad data silently propagates. For facility operations, building-sensor telemetry is a natural stream; monthly energy reporting is a natural batch."},{"id":"software-engineering#29","title":"Evolutionary architecture and the strangler fig","tags":["evolutionary architecture","strangler fig","migration","legacy","fitness functions"],"ask":["How do you replace a legacy system safely?","What is a fitness function?"],"body":"Evolutionary architecture accepts that requirements will change and designs for incremental, guided change. Fitness functions are automated checks that protect important architectural qualities — dependency rules, performance budgets, security scans — running in the build pipeline so the architecture can't silently decay.\n\nThe strangler fig pattern replaces a legacy system gradually. Put a facade or routing layer in front of it, build new functionality alongside, route one capability at a time to the new system, and retire the old pieces once traffic is gone. It avoids the big-bang rewrite, which often fails because the old system's hidden behavior is underestimated and the business can't freeze for a year.\n\nSupporting techniques: branch by abstraction (introduce an interface, build the new implementation behind it, switch), feature flags, parallel runs comparing old and new outputs, and an anti-corruption layer so the old model doesn't leak into the new one."},{"id":"software-engineering#30","title":"Offline-first and sync design","tags":["offline-first","sync","mobile","conflict resolution","CRDT","local-first"],"ask":["How do apps resolve sync conflicts?","What are CRDTs?"],"body":"Offline-first apps treat the local database as the source of truth for the user interface and sync in the background — instant interactions, works without a signal. BlackLab works this way: everything lives on the phone and syncs optionally.\n\nThe hard part is conflicts when two devices change the same data. Options:\n- Last-writer-wins by timestamp: simple, but loses data, and clocks drift.\n- Field-level merge: track changes per field so edits to different fields both survive.\n- Operational transformation: transform concurrent edits against each other (classic Google Docs).\n- CRDTs (conflict-free replicated data types): data structures designed so concurrent updates always merge to the same result (counters, sets, sequences for text). Libraries such as Automerge and Yjs make them practical.\n\nAlso design for sync hygiene: stable IDs (UUIDs) created on the client, tombstones for deletes, version vectors or per-record revision numbers, idempotent sync endpoints, and showing users pending or failed sync state honestly."},{"id":"software-engineering#31","title":"Approaching a system design problem","tags":["system design","interview","design process","capacity estimation","framework"],"ask":["Walk me through designing a URL shortener","How do you estimate capacity quickly?"],"body":"A repeatable approach for design discussions or interviews:\n- Clarify requirements: core features, users, scale, and the most important quality attributes. Write down what's out of scope.\n- Estimate: requests per second (daily volume ÷ 86,400, then times 2–10 for peak), storage per year, bandwidth. Round aggressively.\n- Define the API and the data model first; they constrain everything else.\n- Sketch a high-level design: clients, load balancer, stateless services, data stores, cache, queues.\n- Deep dive on the hardest parts: the hot path, the bottleneck, consistency needs, failure modes.\n- Discuss trade-offs and alternatives explicitly, then how you'd evolve the design as scale grows ten times.\n\nExample: a URL shortener needs a key-generation strategy (base62 of a counter or random IDs with collision checks), a read-heavy key-value store, aggressive caching and a CDN, plus analytics written asynchronously. Saying what you'd measure and monitor shows production thinking."}]},{"id":"conv-ai-builder","title":"AI Builder's Lab","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"Brainstorm and pressure-test AI projects — from idea to prototype to production, with honest talk about RAG, agents, evals, cost and hype.","icon":"","starters":["Pitch me AI projects for facility operations","My RAG answers are bad — help","Should I build an agent for this?","How do I start writing evals?","Give me a 90-day AI learning plan"],"draws":["ai-engineering","ai-development","software-engineering","software-development","github"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-ai-builder#1","title":"AI projects that pay off in facility operations","tags":["facilities","use cases","operations","ROI","ideas"],"ask":["Which of these would your team use first?","What data do you already have?"],"body":"Practical AI projects for facility operations, roughly from quickest win to biggest lift:\n- Work-order intake triage: classify incoming requests by trade, priority and location, and draft the work order from a free-text email or a photo. Measurable by reduced dispatcher time and fewer misroutes.\n- Technician note cleanup and summaries: turn voice notes into clean closeout notes, parts used and follow-ups — dictation plus summarization, exactly what BlackLab already does on the phone.\n- Document Q&A over O&M manuals, warranties and service contracts: \"what's the warranty on RTU-4's compressor?\" with citations.\n- Inspection and compliance assistant: checklists from photos, deficiency extraction, report drafting.\n- Energy anomaly detection on meter and BAS data: catch simultaneous heating and cooling, schedules overriding, or drifting baselines.\n- Predictive maintenance for critical assets using trends and work-order history.\n- Vendor and contract analysis: compare bids, flag unusual terms.\nStart with the one where you already have data, a clear owner and a measurable metric."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#2","title":"Idea to prototype in a weekend","tags":["prototype","MVP","rapid prototyping","LLM app","weekend project"],"ask":["What idea do you want to prototype?","What would success look like after the weekend?"],"body":"Here's a weekend plan for an LLM prototype:\n- Friday night: write the problem in one paragraph, collect 20 real examples of inputs and what a great output looks like. Those examples are your first eval set.\n- Saturday morning: one prompt, a strong model, run all 20 examples, read every output. Note failure patterns.\n- Saturday afternoon: fix the biggest failure with the cheapest lever — clearer instructions, a couple of examples, structured output, or retrieval if the model lacks knowledge.\n- Saturday night: wrap it in the simplest interface someone can use — a script, a small web page, a phone shortcut.\n- Sunday: put it in front of one real user, watch them, and add their failures to the eval set.\n\nResist building infrastructure — no vector database, agent framework or fine-tuning until the evals show you need them. If it isn't useful with a great prompt and good examples, more machinery rarely saves it."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#3","title":"RAG troubleshooting clinic","tags":["RAG","troubleshooting","retrieval","chunking","debugging"],"ask":["What does a bad answer look like — wrong, vague or made up?","Have you looked at what's being retrieved?"],"body":"When RAG answers are bad, first find out which half is failing. Take ten bad answers and look at the retrieved passages. If the right information isn't in them, it's a retrieval problem; if it is there and the answer is still wrong, it's a generation problem.\n\nRetrieval fixes: improve chunking so passages keep context (headings, section titles); add keyword search alongside embeddings for part numbers, codes and names; rewrite vague user queries into better search queries; filter by metadata (building, date, document type); add a reranker; and retrieve a few more candidates.\n\nGeneration fixes: tell the model to answer only from the context and say when it can't; ask it to quote the relevant lines first; put the question after the documents; reduce noise by passing fewer, better passages.\n\nAlso check the data itself — scanned PDFs with bad OCR, duplicated outdated versions of a document, and tables flattened into nonsense cause a huge share of RAG failures."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#4","title":"Should this be an agent?","tags":["agents","workflows","design decision","complexity"],"ask":["can I write down the steps? If the task follows a predictable sequence — extract, classify, look something up, draft — build a workflow: fixed steps with LLM calls where needed. It's cheaper, faster, easier to test and easier to trust."],"body":"Reach for an agent when the path genuinely varies: the number and order of steps depend on what's found along the way — debugging, research, multi-system troubleshooting. Even then, give it a tight toolset, a budget on steps and cost, checkpoints for human approval before consequential actions, and good logging.\n\nThen ask about the cost of mistakes. An agent drafting a summary that a human reviews can be fairly autonomous; an agent issuing purchase orders or changing building setpoints needs strict limits and approvals. A good middle ground is \"agent proposes, human disposes\": it prepares the work order, email or change, and a person approves with one tap."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#5","title":"Writing your first evals","tags":["evals","evaluation","testing","quality","metrics"],"ask":["What does a great output look like for your use case?","How many examples could you gather this week?"],"body":"Evals turn \"it seems better\" into numbers. Start tiny:\n- Gather 20–50 real inputs, including the hard and weird ones.\n- For each, write what a good output must contain or must not do. For extraction, that's the exact expected fields; for summaries, a short checklist (\"mentions the due date,\" \"no invented costs\").\n- Score automatically where you can — exact match, JSON schema checks, keyword or regex checks — and use an LLM judge with a specific rubric for fuzzier qualities like completeness and tone.\n- Calibrate the judge: grade 20 outputs yourself and confirm the judge mostly agrees.\n- Run the suite on every prompt or model change and keep a simple results table over time.\n\nMost importantly, read outputs. Patterns in failures — always missing dates, overly long answers, trouble with abbreviations — tell you exactly what to fix. Add every production failure to the set; your eval suite becomes the institutional memory of what \"good\" means."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#6","title":"Hype versus reality","tags":["hype","AI limits","expectations","realism","strategy"],"ask":["What AI claim have you heard that smelled off?","Where have you seen AI genuinely deliver?"],"body":"A sanity check for AI claims: what's the baseline, what's the eval, and what happens when it's wrong? Demos are cherry-picked; production is the long tail of weird inputs.\n\nWhat reliably works today: drafting and summarizing text, extracting structure from messy documents, classification and routing, answering questions over your documents with citations, code assistance, and speech-to-text. Agents handle well-scoped tasks with good tools, though reliability falls as tasks get longer and less defined.\n\nWhat still needs care: exact numbers and calculations (use tools), high-stakes decisions without review, long multi-step autonomy, and anything requiring knowledge the model doesn't have and you didn't provide.\n\nThe pattern that wins is boring: pick a narrow, high-volume task, measure the current cost, add AI with a human in the loop, measure again, and expand. Most value comes from many small, reliable improvements rather than one magical system."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#7","title":"Building on-device AI like BlackLab","tags":["on-device","Gemini Nano","mobile AI","privacy","offline","Android"],"ask":["What would you add to BlackLab's assistant next?","What runs well on a phone model?"],"body":"BlackLab is a good case study in on-device AI. The assistant runs a deterministic command router first — fast, offline and predictable for things like \"add a task\" or \"what's on my calendar.\" Unrecognized requests go to Gemini Nano through ML Kit's Prompt API, which either rewrites them into a command or answers directly. Ask-my-files uses on-device BM25 search to find passages, then Nano writes a cited answer. When Nano isn't available, it falls back to extractive answers, so nothing breaks.\n\nWhat runs well on a small model: rewriting, short summaries, classification, extraction from a page or two, and turning loose phrasing into structured commands. What doesn't: long documents in one go (so summaries use map-reduce chunking), deep reasoning, and broad world knowledge — which is why the knowledge bases ship inside the app as searchable notes.\n\nDesign lessons: keep prompts short and explicit, constrain output formats, always have a non-AI path, and respect privacy as a feature, not an afterthought."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#8","title":"The real cost of AI features","tags":["cost","budget","tokens","pricing","economics"],"ask":["How many requests a day do you expect?","Would a smaller model pass your evals?"],"body":"Costs sneak up through volume and context size. A quick estimate: requests per day times tokens per request times price per token. The surprise is usually input tokens — long system prompts, retrieved documents and chat history are sent with every call. Agents multiply it again with many calls per task.\n\nLevers, in order of impact: choose the smallest model that passes your evals; route easy requests to cheap models; use prompt caching for stable prefixes; retrieve fewer, better passages; summarize long histories; cap output length; batch offline work at discount rates; and cache repeated answers.\n\nOn-device models change the math entirely — zero per-call cost and no data leaving the phone — at the price of capability. Hybrid designs get the best of both: do the routine work locally, escalate hard cases to a cloud model with the user's consent.\n\nAlways tie cost to value: minutes saved per task times tasks per month times labor rate. AI that saves dispatchers an hour a day pays for a lot of tokens."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#9","title":"Prompt critique session","tags":["prompts","prompt engineering","critique","improvement"],"ask":["Want to read me a prompt you're using?","What's the output problem you're seeing?"],"body":"When I review a prompt, I check a few things:\n- Is the task stated plainly in the first lines? Models, like people, anchor on the opening.\n- Is there context — who the output is for, why, and what good looks like?\n- Are instructions positive and specific (\"Write three bullets under 15 words each\") rather than vague (\"be concise\")?\n- Are there examples, and are they varied enough not to be copied blindly?\n- Is the output format defined exactly, ideally as a schema or template?\n- Are documents and data clearly separated from instructions, with labels or tags?\n- Does it tell the model what to do when information is missing?\n- Is there contradictory or leftover instruction from older versions? Prompts accumulate cruft.\n\nThen test changes one at a time against your eval set. A surprising amount of improvement comes from deleting things — long prompts full of \"IMPORTANT\" and capital letters often perform worse than a calm, clear one."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#10","title":"Staying current without drowning","tags":["learning","news","staying current","information diet","AI field"],"ask":["Where do you get your AI news now?","How much time a week can you give it?"],"body":"The AI field moves weekly, and trying to follow everything is a recipe for burnout. A sustainable information diet:\n- A few primary sources: official model release notes and documentation from the major labs and platforms you actually use.\n- One or two curated newsletters or digests, read weekly rather than daily.\n- A handful of thoughtful practitioners rather than hype accounts.\n- Hands-on time: thirty minutes trying a new capability on your own problem teaches more than ten threads about it.\n\nFilter questions for any announcement: does this change what I can build, what it costs, or how reliable it is? If not, skip it. Keep a running \"try later\" list and revisit monthly.\n\nFoundations age slowly — evaluation, retrieval, prompting principles, software engineering, data quality. Invest there, and the weekly news becomes easier to place."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#11","title":"Ethics gut-checks for AI projects","tags":["ethics","responsible AI","privacy","fairness","safety"],"ask":["Who could be affected by your project that isn't in the room?","What's the worst realistic misuse?"],"body":"Quick gut-checks before shipping an AI feature:\n- Who's affected, including people who aren't users — employees being monitored, tenants, contractors?\n- What data does it touch, and did people expect it to be used this way? Minimize and protect it.\n- What happens when it's wrong, and who catches it? Higher stakes need human review and easy appeal.\n- Could it treat groups differently — by language, location, role? Test across them.\n- Is it clear to people that AI is involved and what it can't do?\n- What's the worst realistic misuse, and have you made it harder?\n- Can you turn it off quickly?\n\nIn facilities, watch occupancy analytics and camera-based features especially: aggregate data, avoid identifying individuals, involve HR and legal early, and be transparent with staff. Responsible AI isn't a separate project; it's these questions asked at design time and revisited after launch."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#12","title":"A 90-day AI builder plan","tags":["learning plan","roadmap","90 days","study","projects"],"ask":["How many hours a week can you commit?","What project would you want to finish by day 90?"],"body":"A 90-day plan for a busy professional, around five hours a week:\n- Days 1–30, foundations: how LLMs work (tokens, context, sampling), prompt engineering with structured outputs, and calling a model API from Python. Project: a script that turns work-order emails into structured JSON, with a 30-example eval set.\n- Days 31–60, knowledge and tools: embeddings, chunking, hybrid search and RAG with citations; tool calling. Project: Q&A over your O&M manuals and contracts, with retrieval evals (did the right passage come back?).\n- Days 61–90, systems: agents versus workflows, MCP, observability, cost control, and deployment. Project: a small assistant that drafts a work order from a photo plus a voice note, asks for confirmation, and logs everything. Publish it on GitHub with a README and an evals section.\n\nAlong the way, read one solid explainer a week and keep a learning log. By day 90 you'll have three working projects, real intuition about failure modes, and a portfolio that speaks for itself."},{"id":"conv-ai-builder#13","title":"Classic ML or LLM?","tags":["classic ML","LLM","model choice","tabular data","decision"],"ask":["Is your data mostly numbers or mostly text?","How many labeled examples do you have?"],"body":"Not every problem needs a large language model. Rules of thumb:\n- Mostly numeric, tabular data — sensor readings, costs, runtimes — with a clear target: classic ML (gradient boosting) is usually more accurate, cheaper, faster and more explainable. Energy forecasting and failure prediction live here.\n- Mostly text, documents, images or speech, with fuzzy categories and little labeled data: LLMs shine, because they bring general knowledge and need only a few examples.\n- Lots of labeled text and very high volume: a small fine-tuned classifier may beat an LLM on cost and speed — and an LLM can help label the training data.\n- Hybrid is common: an LLM extracts structured fields from messy text (failure mode, part, cause), then classic ML or simple analytics run on those fields.\n\nStart with the simplest approach that could work, set a baseline, and let your evals choose. An honest \"a spreadsheet rule handles 90% of this\" is a great outcome."}]},{"id":"conv-architecture","title":"Architecture Roundtable","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"Talk through system design like two senior engineers at a whiteboard — trade-offs, scaling stories, failure modes and design reviews.","icon":"","starters":["Design a work-order system for 300 buildings","When should I split a monolith?","How do I run a design review?","What are one-way and two-way door decisions?","Walk me through designing a chat app"],"draws":["software-engineering","software-development","ai-engineering","ai-development","github"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-architecture#1","title":"How to run a design conversation","tags":["design discussion","whiteboard","facilitation","requirements","trade-offs"],"ask":["Want to practice on a real system you own?","Should we start with requirements or the data model?"],"body":"The best design conversations follow a rhythm: clarify, constrain, sketch, stress, decide. Start by restating the problem in one sentence and naming who it's for. Then pin down the two or three quality attributes that really matter — is it latency, cost, correctness, or time to ship? Most bad designs optimize the wrong one.\n\nNext, put rough numbers on the board: users, requests per second at peak, data growth per year. Numbers kill hand-waving. Then sketch the simplest thing that could work, and only then attack it: what breaks at ten times the load, what happens when the database fails over, what's the worst thing a malicious user could do?\n\nClose by writing down the decision, the alternatives you rejected and why, and what would make you revisit it. That last part is gold six months later."},{"id":"conv-architecture#2","title":"Designing a work-order system for a portfolio of buildings","tags":["work orders","CMMS","facilities","system design","case study"],"ask":["Should technicians work offline?","How would you prioritize incoming requests automatically?"],"body":"Let's design a work-order platform for about 300 buildings. Requirements first: requesters submit issues with photos; dispatchers triage and assign; technicians receive jobs on phones, often in basements with no signal; preventive maintenance generates jobs on schedules; managers want SLAs and cost reporting.\n\nScale is modest — maybe 2,000 work orders a day — so correctness, offline support and integrations matter more than raw throughput. A modular monolith on a relational database fits: modules for requests, assets, scheduling, inventory and reporting. Technicians get an offline-first mobile app with a local database and sync, client-generated IDs, and per-field merge for conflicts.\n\nPreventive maintenance is a scheduler that materializes jobs a few weeks ahead from rules (calendar or runtime-based). Photos go to object storage with thumbnails. Integrations — building automation alarms, ERP for costs — come in through a queue so outages don't drop events. Reporting reads from replicas or a nightly warehouse. AI fits nicely at intake: classify trade and priority, and extract asset details from photos, with a human confirming."},{"id":"conv-architecture#3","title":"Monolith first, split with a reason","tags":["monolith","microservices","decomposition","team topology"],"ask":["What's the first service you'd extract?","How do you know boundaries are right?"],"body":"My default is a modular monolith until there's a reason to split, and \"microservices are modern\" isn't a reason. Good reasons: a component needs to scale very differently (image processing versus CRUD), deploy on its own cadence, use a different technology, or belong to a separate team that keeps colliding with yours.\n\nBefore splitting, make the boundary real inside the monolith: separate packages or modules, no reaching into another module's tables, communication through interfaces or events. If you can't keep a boundary clean inside one codebase, a network won't fix it — it'll just make the mess slower and harder to debug.\n\nWhen you do extract, pick the module with the cleanest seam and the clearest benefit, give it its own data, put a strangler-style router in front, and measure. One question I always ask: can each service be deployed without coordinating with others? If not, you've built a distributed monolith."},{"id":"conv-architecture#4","title":"One-way and two-way doors","tags":["decisions","reversibility","risk","speed","leadership"],"ask":["Which decisions in your current project are one-way doors?","How do you speed up two-way door decisions?"],"body":"Jeff Bezos popularized a simple lens: most decisions are two-way doors — if you're wrong, you walk back through. Make those fast, with the people closest to the work. A few are one-way doors — expensive or impossible to reverse — and deserve slow, careful thought.\n\nIn architecture, one-way doors include your core data model and identifiers, public API contracts other people build on, choice of primary database for a large dataset, data retention and privacy commitments, and vendor lock-in with heavy migration costs. Two-way doors include most library choices, internal service boundaries in a monolith, UI frameworks for internal tools, and caching strategies.\n\nThe skill is converting one-way doors into two-way ones: hide vendors behind interfaces, version APIs from day one, keep data exportable, use feature flags. Facility leaders know this instinctively — you don't pour a foundation the way you rearrange furniture."},{"id":"conv-architecture#5","title":"Running a design review that people like","tags":["design review","design doc","RFC","feedback","process"],"ask":["What should a design doc contain?","How do you handle a reviewer who wants to redesign everything?"],"body":"A good design review is a conversation about risk, not a performance. Send a short design doc two days ahead: context and goals, non-goals, the proposed design with a diagram, alternatives considered, risks and open questions, rollout and rollback plan, and how you'll know it worked.\n\nIn the meeting, spend the time on the open questions and the riskiest parts, not on reading the doc aloud. Reviewers should ask \"what happens when…\" questions: when traffic doubles, when this dependency is down, when data is corrupted, when a user does something weird. Keep a running list of decisions and action items.\n\nIf a reviewer wants to redesign everything, separate \"this won't work\" from \"I'd do it differently.\" The first needs addressing; the second needs a strong argument tied to requirements. Time-box debates, and let the owner decide after hearing input. Write down what was decided — a short ADR is enough."},{"id":"conv-architecture#6","title":"Failure-mode brainstorming","tags":["failure modes","resilience","FMEA","pre-mortem","reliability"],"ask":["Want to run a pre-mortem on something you're building?","What fails most often in practice?"],"body":"My favorite design exercise is the pre-mortem: imagine it's six months from now and the system failed badly. What happened? People find risks they'd never raise when asked \"any concerns?\"\n\nThen walk the system box by box and arrow by arrow, asking what happens if this is slow, down, wrong, duplicated or overloaded. Engineers borrowed failure mode and effects analysis from manufacturing for exactly this — rate each failure by severity, likelihood and detectability.\n\nIn practice, the usual suspects are boring: expired certificates, full disks, a config change, a dependency's rate limit, a retry storm, a slow query after data grew, a timezone or daylight-saving bug, and a cron job running twice. Design for detection as much as prevention: health checks, alerts on symptoms, and runbooks. Facility operations has the same instinct — you plan for the chiller failing on the hottest day, because that's when it fails."},{"id":"conv-architecture#7","title":"Designing a chat or messaging system","tags":["chat","messaging","real-time","WebSockets","system design"],"ask":["How would you handle read receipts?","How do you store messages at scale?"],"body":"For a chat app, start with requirements: one-to-one and group chats, real-time delivery, history, read receipts, push notifications for offline users, maybe attachments.\n\nReal-time delivery uses persistent connections — WebSockets — to a fleet of gateway servers. A user's connection lands on one gateway, so you need a presence and routing layer (which gateway holds which user), often in Redis. Sending a message: the client posts it with a client-generated ID (for idempotency), the service stores it, assigns a per-conversation sequence number for ordering, then fans it out to online members' gateways and queues push notifications for offline ones.\n\nStorage is write-heavy and append-only, partitioned by conversation ID, with messages sorted by sequence — a wide-column store or a sharded relational database works. Clients sync by asking \"everything after sequence N,\" which also handles reconnects. Large groups change the fan-out math: switch to fan-out on read. Attachments go to object storage with signed URLs. End-to-end encryption changes everything server-side, so decide early — it's a one-way door."},{"id":"conv-architecture#8","title":"Rate limiters and protecting services","tags":["rate limiting","token bucket","API protection","design"],"ask":["Token bucket or sliding window?","Where should rate limiting live?"],"body":"Rate limiting protects services from abuse and overload and keeps usage fair. Classic algorithms: the token bucket (tokens refill at a fixed rate, each request spends one, and bursts up to the bucket size are allowed — the most common choice), the leaky bucket (smooths output to a steady rate), fixed windows (simple but allow bursts at window edges), and sliding windows (more accurate, a bit more work).\n\nWhere it lives: at the edge (API gateway or load balancer) for coarse protection, and in the application for business rules (five password attempts per account per hour). In a fleet of servers, counters need to be shared — Redis with atomic increments or Lua scripts is the standard approach — or approximated per node if exactness doesn't matter.\n\nReturn 429 Too Many Requests with a Retry-After header so well-behaved clients back off. Rate-limit by the right key — user, API key, IP address, or tenant — and remember that limits are part of your API contract, so document them."},{"id":"conv-architecture#9","title":"Build versus buy","tags":["build vs buy","vendors","SaaS","total cost of ownership","strategy"],"ask":["How do you evaluate a vendor's lock-in?","When is building worth it?"],"body":"The rule I use: build what differentiates you, buy what doesn't. Authentication, payments, email delivery, monitoring and CMMS basics are solved problems — buying them is usually cheaper than the engineers you'd need to build and maintain them. Build when the capability is core to your advantage, when nothing on the market fits your workflow, or when vendor constraints (data residency, offline needs, integration limits) are deal-breakers.\n\nCompare total cost of ownership over three to five years: licenses versus salaries, maintenance, security patching, on-call, upgrades and opportunity cost. Engineers routinely underestimate maintenance — the build is maybe a third of the lifetime cost.\n\nFor vendors, evaluate exit costs up front: can you export all your data in a usable format? Are there APIs for everything? What happens if they're acquired or raise prices? A facility director runs this play for chillers and service contracts all the time; software is the same game with faster depreciation."},{"id":"conv-architecture#10","title":"Data model first","tags":["data modeling","schema","entities","database design"],"ask":["Want to model a domain together?","How do you model history and auditing?"],"body":"When I'm stuck on a design, I go back to the data model. Get the nouns, their relationships and their lifecycles right, and the services and screens mostly fall out. Get them wrong and every feature fights the schema.\n\nQuestions to ask: what are the core entities and their identifiers? What's one-to-many versus many-to-many? Which data is mutable, and do we need its history? Who owns each piece of data — which module is allowed to write it? What are the invariants that must always hold? What are the read patterns, and how big will each table get?\n\nFor history and auditing, consider an append-only event or audit table, or temporal tables with valid-from and valid-to columns. Use surrogate keys (UUIDs or sequences) rather than natural keys that change. Model money as integers in the smallest unit plus a currency, times in UTC with time zones stored separately when they matter, and states as explicit enums with allowed transitions."},{"id":"conv-architecture#11","title":"Architecture smells","tags":["architecture smells","anti-patterns","technical debt","warning signs"],"ask":["Which smell shows up most in your systems?","How do you fix a shared database between services?"],"body":"Some warning signs that an architecture is drifting:\n- Everything talks to the same database tables, so no one can change a column safely.\n- A \"common\" or \"utils\" library that every service depends on and changes weekly.\n- Synchronous call chains five services deep, where any one being slow makes everything slow.\n- Features that always require coordinated changes in several components.\n- Configuration explosions — dozens of flags whose combinations no one understands.\n- God services or god classes that own too much.\n- Cyclic dependencies between modules.\n- Dashboards nobody looks at and alerts everybody ignores.\n\nFixes are usually incremental: assign data ownership and put an API in front of shared tables; break up common libraries by purpose; replace synchronous chains with events or caches where consistency allows; and add fitness functions in CI that enforce dependency rules so the architecture can't silently drift back."},{"id":"conv-architecture#12","title":"Designing AI features into a system","tags":["AI features","LLM integration","architecture","product design"],"ask":["Where would AI help most in your operations?","How do you keep AI features from being flaky?"],"body":"When adding AI to a product, I treat the model as an unreliable but brilliant dependency. Wrap it in a service with a clear contract, timeouts, fallbacks and evals. Start with the user's job to be done — \"triage incoming requests,\" \"summarize a technician's notes\" — not with \"add a chatbot.\"\n\nArchitecturally: a gateway for model access, cost tracking and provider switching; retrieval over your own data with permission filtering; tools exposed with least privilege; and a human checkpoint for consequential actions. Log prompts, outputs and feedback for evaluation, with privacy controls.\n\nDesign the experience for uncertainty: show sources, let users edit outputs, and make undo easy. Measure the business outcome — time saved per work order, fewer misrouted requests — not just model accuracy. And keep a non-AI path working; when the model is down or wrong, the system should degrade, not break. BlackLab's own assistant falls back from Gemini Nano to rules for exactly this reason."},{"id":"conv-architecture#13","title":"Scaling a system ten times","tags":["scaling","growth","capacity planning","bottlenecks"],"ask":["What would break first in your system at ten times the load?","How do you capacity plan?"],"body":"A useful drill: take any system and ask what breaks at ten times the load, then a hundred times. Usually the order is predictable. First the database: slow queries, missing indexes, connection limits, and lock contention. Then shared caches and queues, then third-party rate limits, then the human processes — on-call, deploys, data fixes.\n\nCapacity planning means knowing your current headroom: peak utilization of CPU, memory, connections and storage, and how they grow with users. Load-test to find the knee of the curve rather than guessing.\n\nTypical scaling moves, roughly in order: optimize queries and add indexes; add caching; scale the stateless tier horizontally; add read replicas; move slow work to queues; partition data by tenant or key; and finally split services where scaling profiles differ. Each step adds complexity, so take it when the data says so — not because a conference talk said so."},{"id":"conv-architecture#14","title":"Offline-first mobile design","tags":["offline-first","mobile","sync","field technicians","conflict resolution"],"ask":["How would you sync technician updates from a basement with no signal?","What conflicts actually happen in practice?"],"body":"Field apps — technicians in mechanical rooms, inspectors on roofs — must work offline. The pattern: the phone's local database is the source of truth for the UI, every change is written locally first and queued, and a sync engine pushes and pulls in the background when a connection returns.\n\nKey choices: client-generated IDs so records can be created offline; a change log or per-record version numbers to sync incrementally; idempotent sync endpoints so retries are safe; tombstones for deletes; and a clear conflict policy. In practice, most \"conflicts\" are rare and benign — two people editing different fields. Field-level merging handles those. True same-field conflicts can use last-writer-wins for low-stakes fields and explicit resolution for critical ones like a work order's status.\n\nShow sync state honestly — pending, synced, failed — and never silently drop changes. Photos and large attachments sync separately with resumable uploads. BlackLab itself is offline-first: everything lives on the phone, and sync is optional."}]},{"id":"conv-code-craft","title":"Code Craft & Review","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"A pair-programming partner for the craft — reviewing code, debugging, refactoring, testing, naming, and getting better every week.","icon":"","starters":["How do I give code review feedback that lands?","Help me debug something weird","How do I get better at naming?","What's a good way to practice coding?","How do I review AI-generated code?"],"draws":["software-development","software-engineering","github","ai-engineering","ai-development"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-code-craft#1","title":"Giving review feedback that lands","tags":["code review","feedback","communication","teamwork"],"ask":["Want to practice rewording a comment?","How do you handle disagreement in a review?"],"body":"Feedback lands when it's about the code, explains why, and suggests a path. Compare \"this is wrong\" with \"If the list is empty, line 40 throws — could we return early here?\" The second names the risk, points at the spot, and offers a fix.\n\nHabits that help: lead with questions when you're not sure (\"Is there a reason we're not using the existing date helper?\"); label weight — blocking, suggestion, or nit — so the author knows what must change; batch small style things or, better, automate them with a linter; and say what's good, specifically. \"Nice call extracting this into its own function — the test got much simpler\" teaches as much as criticism does.\n\nWhen you disagree, move synchronous fast: a five-minute call beats a twenty-comment thread. And remember the bar: approve when the change makes the codebase better, not when it matches what you'd have written."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#2","title":"A debugging session, step by step","tags":["debugging","troubleshooting","bugs","method"],"ask":["What's the bug you're chasing?","Can you reproduce it reliably?"],"body":"Let's debug like scientists. First, can you reproduce it? If it's intermittent, note everything about when it happens — time, device, data, sequence. Then shrink it: the smallest input and the fewest steps that still show the bug.\n\nNext, read the evidence carefully: the full error, the stack trace, the logs right before. Then form one hypothesis at a time, predict what you'd see if it's true, and check that prediction cheaply — a breakpoint, a log line, an assertion. If the prediction fails, cross that hypothesis off; that's progress too.\n\nIf you're lost, binary-search: comment out half, check halfway through the data flow, or run git bisect between a good and a bad version. And question the \"impossible\": is the code you're reading actually what's running? Is there a cache, a stale build, a different config? When you find it, write the test that would've caught it before fixing it."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#3","title":"Getting better at naming","tags":["naming","readability","clean code","variables","functions"],"ask":["Have a name you're stuck on?","How do you name booleans well?"],"body":"Naming is design in miniature: if something's hard to name, it may be doing too much. Good names say what something means in the problem domain, not how it's implemented. technicianAssignments beats mapOfLists.\n\nPractical rules:\n- Functions are verbs that describe the effect: calculateDueDate, sendReminder. If you need \"and\" in the name, split the function.\n- Booleans read as yes-or-no questions: isOverdue, hasAttachments, canEdit.\n- Collections are plural; counts say so: workOrders, workOrderCount.\n- Avoid noise words like data, info, manager, helper, and vague verbs like process or handle unless the context makes them precise.\n- Use the business's language. If facility staff say \"PM,\" the code can say preventiveMaintenance.\n- Scope matters: a loop index can be i; a module-level constant should be descriptive.\nRename freely — modern IDEs make it safe — and when a teammate misreads a name, treat it as a bug in the name."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#4","title":"Refactoring a messy function together","tags":["refactoring","clean code","long function","safe changes"],"ask":["What does the function do in one sentence?","Does it have tests?"],"body":"Here's how I'd approach a 200-line function. First, characterization tests: run it with a few realistic inputs and pin down the outputs, even weird ones. Now we can change things without fear.\n\nThen small, safe moves, running tests after each. Rename variables to say what they mean. Extract the obvious chunks into well-named functions — validation, the calculation, formatting the output. Replace nested if-pyramids with guard clauses that return early. Pull magic numbers into named constants. If there's a big when or switch on type, consider whether each branch belongs with its type.\n\nCommit after each green step, so you can always back up. Don't change behavior and structure in the same commit. Often, once the structure is clear, the real bug or the needed feature becomes obvious — Kent Beck's line fits: make the change easy, then make the easy change."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#5","title":"How to practice coding deliberately","tags":["practice","learning","katas","skills","deliberate practice"],"ask":["What do you want to get better at specifically?","How much time can you practice each week?"],"body":"Deliberate practice beats just writing more code. Pick a specific skill, work at the edge of your ability, get fast feedback, and repeat.\n\nIdeas that work:\n- Code katas: small problems (bowling score, Roman numerals, a word wrapper) solved repeatedly with TDD, trying a different approach each time.\n- Rebuild something small you use: a to-do app, a URL shortener, a CLI tool for your work data.\n- Read great code: well-known open-source projects in your language; study how they structure things.\n- Review your own old code — what would you change now?\n- Teach: write a short explainer or walk a friend through something. Gaps show up immediately.\n- Timed problem sets for algorithms, a few a week, focusing on recognizing patterns.\n\nBuild around your real world: a script that cleans your work-order export, an app feature in BlackLab, a dashboard for energy data. Real stakes make lessons stick."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#6","title":"Reviewing AI-generated code","tags":["AI-generated code","Copilot","code review","LLM","verification"],"ask":["Where has AI-generated code burned you?","How do you prompt for better code?"],"body":"AI assistants write plausible code fast, which makes careful review more important, not less. Read it like a pull request from a confident new hire who's never seen your codebase.\n\nChecklist: Does it actually solve the stated problem, including edge cases like empty input, nulls, time zones and errors? Does it use APIs that exist in your versions, or did it invent a method? Is it consistent with the codebase's patterns, or did it introduce a new style or library? Security: input validation, injection, secrets, permissions. Tests: did it write meaningful ones, or tests that just restate the implementation?\n\nBetter prompts produce better code: give the context (files, conventions, versions), state constraints, ask for small steps, and ask it to explain its reasoning or list assumptions. Run the code and the tests; don't trust \"this should work.\" Use AI heavily for boilerplate, tests, unfamiliar APIs and explanations — and keep your own understanding sharp, because you own what ships."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#7","title":"Writing tests that actually help","tags":["testing","unit tests","test design","flaky tests","coverage"],"ask":["Which part of your code scares you most to change?","How do you deal with flaky tests?"],"body":"A good test fails for one reason, tells you exactly what broke, and survives refactoring. Test behavior through public interfaces rather than private details, and name tests like specifications: overdueTask_isFlaggedOnHome rather than test3.\n\nArrange-Act-Assert keeps them readable. Use real objects or simple fakes over deep mocks; when a test needs a dozen mocks, the code under test probably has too many dependencies.\n\nStart where fear lives: the calculation everyone's afraid to touch, the parser that breaks on weird input, the bug you fixed last week. Coverage numbers are a flashlight to find untested areas, not a goal.\n\nFlaky tests poison trust. Common causes: time (use an injectable clock), randomness (seed it), ordering assumptions, shared state between tests, real network calls, and race conditions in async code. Quarantine flaky tests quickly, fix the root cause, and never let \"just re-run it\" become normal."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#8","title":"Pull request etiquette for authors","tags":["pull requests","PR size","descriptions","collaboration"],"ask":["How big are your typical PRs?","What makes you procrastinate on reviews?"],"body":"Make your reviewer's job easy and you'll get faster, better reviews.\n- Small and focused: one purpose per pull request. Under a few hundred changed lines when possible. Split refactoring from behavior changes.\n- A real description: what changed, why, how you tested it, and what you'd like feedback on. Screenshots or recordings for UI changes.\n- Review your own diff first. You'll catch leftover debug logs, commented-out code and typos.\n- Stack large features as a series of small pull requests, or hide work in progress behind a feature flag.\n- Respond to every comment — fix it, explain, or agree to follow up — and push fixes as new commits during review so changes are easy to see.\n- Don't take it personally; the reviewer is on your team, reviewing the code, not you.\nIf reviews are slow on your team, raise it — review latency is one of the biggest hidden drags on delivery."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#9","title":"Reading code faster","tags":["code reading","comprehension","onboarding","navigation"],"ask":["Which codebase are you trying to understand?","Do you use the debugger to read code?"],"body":"Reading code is a skill you can train. Start with purpose: what's this module for, and what are its inputs and outputs? Then trace one concrete scenario through it end to end rather than reading files top to bottom.\n\nUse your tools: go to definition, find usages, call hierarchy, and the debugger — stepping through a real request shows what actually runs, which beats guessing. Tests are executable documentation of intended behavior. Version history explains the why: git log on a file, blame on a confusing line, and the pull request that introduced it.\n\nKeep notes or a quick sketch of the flow as you go; your understanding compounds. And ask good questions after you've tried: \"I followed the save path to the repository, but I can't find where validation happens — is it in the ViewModel?\" Specific questions get fast answers and show respect for people's time."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#10","title":"Kotlin and Compose habits worth building","tags":["Kotlin","Jetpack Compose","Android","habits","best practices"],"ask":["Are you fighting recomposition issues?","Want to talk coroutine scopes?"],"body":"For Kotlin and Compose work, a few habits pay off daily:\n- Prefer val and immutable data classes for state; expose StateFlow from ViewModels and keep mutable state private.\n- Model screen state as a single data class or a sealed interface (Loading, Content, Error) so impossible combinations can't happen.\n- Hoist state: composables take values and callbacks; they don't own business logic.\n- Use remember and derivedStateOf deliberately; pass stable parameters so Compose can skip work.\n- Launch coroutines in lifecycle-aware scopes (viewModelScope, lifecycleScope), switch to Dispatchers.IO for disk and network, and never block the main thread.\n- Use sealed results for operations that can fail in expected ways.\n- Keep Room queries returning Flow for reactive screens; do writes in the app-wide scope if they must survive navigation — the pattern BlackLab uses.\n- Run lint and keep warnings low; they catch real bugs.\nSmall, consistent patterns make a big codebase feel small."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#11","title":"Handling legacy code without despair","tags":["legacy code","maintenance","technical debt","morale"],"ask":["What's the scariest part of the codebase you maintain?","Rewrite or refactor — what's your instinct?"],"body":"Legacy code is just code that's been successful long enough to accumulate history. The despair comes from fear — no tests, unclear behavior, and changes that break things far away.\n\nThe way out is small and steady. Before changing anything, add characterization tests around the area you're touching. Find seams where you can isolate dependencies. Add new behavior in new, tested code and call it from the old (sprout method). Refactor only what you need for the change in front of you, and leave each area a bit better than you found it.\n\nResist the full rewrite unless the system truly can't evolve; rewrites usually take longer than planned and miss hidden behavior the old code handled. Celebrate progress — \"this module now has tests\" is a win. And respect the people who wrote it; they made reasonable choices under constraints you can't see."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#12","title":"Estimating your own work honestly","tags":["estimation","planning","communication","deadlines"],"ask":["What are you estimating right now?","How do you communicate a slipping date?"],"body":"Developer estimates go wrong for predictable reasons: we estimate the coding and forget the rest — understanding the problem, testing, review, deployment, fixing the surprises, meetings and interruptions.\n\nBetter habits: break the work into pieces you can picture finishing in a day or less; estimate each and add them; then add explicit buffers for integration and the unknown. Give ranges (\"two to four days\") and say what drives the uncertainty (\"depends on whether the API supports batch updates — I'll know by tomorrow\"). Spike the riskiest part first, and re-estimate as you learn.\n\nWhen a date slips, say so early with options: cut scope, move the date, or add help (which rarely speeds things up short-term). People forgive slipping estimates; they don't forgive surprises. Tracking your estimates against actuals for a month is humbling and quickly makes you better."},{"id":"conv-code-craft#13","title":"Staying sharp as a part-time developer","tags":["learning","career","part-time","side projects","motivation"],"ask":["How much time do you get to code each week?","What would you build if you had a free weekend?"],"body":"Lots of strong developers code alongside another demanding job. The trick is consistency over intensity: three focused hours a week, every week, beats an occasional all-nighter.\n\nMake it easy to start: keep a running \"next step\" note so you can pick up in two minutes; keep projects small enough to finish; and work on things that matter to you — automating a tedious report at work, an app feature you'll actually use, a dashboard for your team. Real use gives real feedback.\n\nRide the learning curve with intention: one new concept at a time, applied immediately. Read code and short articles in the gaps — commutes, lunch — and save the deep work for protected blocks. Share your work on GitHub; a steady commit history and a few finished projects with good READMEs are a portfolio. And don't compare yourself to full-time engineers' output; compare yourself to where you were three months ago."}]},{"id":"conv-downtime","title":"Downtime & Interests","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"The fun stuff — football and fantasy, grilling and barbecue, road trips, the outdoors, DIY, music, movies, games and good drinks.","icon":"","starters":["Help me win my fantasy football league","How do I smoke a brisket?","Plan a road trip in the Santa Fe","What should I listen to on a long drive?","Give me a project for the garage this weekend"],"draws":["conv-downtime"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-downtime#1","title":"Fantasy football strategy","tags":["fantasy football","draft strategy","NFL","waiver wire","lineup"],"ask":["What scoring format is your league — PPR or standard?","Where are you drafting from?"],"body":"Fantasy football rewards preparation and in-season hustle more than draft-day luck. Know your scoring first: in PPR leagues, pass-catching running backs and high-target receivers gain value; superflex leagues push quarterbacks way up.\n\nDraft approach: use tiers rather than strict rankings — when a tier is about to run out at a position, grab one. Prioritize volume and opportunity (targets, carries, snap share) over last year's touchdowns, which swing a lot. Don't reach for a kicker or defense early; stream them weekly based on matchups. Late rounds are for upside: backup running backs one injury away from a big role and young receivers in good offenses.\n\nIn season, the waiver wire wins leagues. Watch snap counts and target shares, act on injuries fast, and handcuff your star running back if it's cheap. Play matchups at the flex, check inactives before kickoff, and trade from depth to fix weaknesses. And always set your lineup before the Thursday game."},{"id":"conv-downtime#2","title":"Smoking a brisket","tags":["brisket","smoking","barbecue","BBQ","smoker","Texas style"],"ask":["What smoker are you working with?","Have you tried wrapping in butcher paper?"],"body":"Brisket is the barbecue final exam, but it's very doable with patience.\n- Pick a whole packer brisket with good marbling and a flexible flat. Trim the hard fat and leave about a quarter inch on the fat cap.\n- Season simply: Texas style is coarse black pepper and kosher salt, often with a little garlic. Season the night before or an hour ahead.\n- Smoke around 225–275°F with clean, thin blue smoke — post oak, hickory or oak are classics.\n- The stall hits somewhere in the 150s–160s°F internal as moisture evaporates; it can last hours. Wrap in butcher paper (keeps bark) or foil (faster, softer bark) to push through.\n- Done is about feel more than a number: usually around 200–205°F, when a probe slides in like butter.\n- Rest is crucial: at least an hour, and longer wrapped in towels in a dry cooler.\n- Slice the flat against the grain about pencil-thick; the point makes great burnt ends.\nPlan roughly 1 to 1.5 hours per pound, and start earlier than you think."},{"id":"conv-downtime#3","title":"Grilling a great steak","tags":["steak","grilling","reverse sear","ribeye","temperatures"],"ask":["Gas or charcoal?","How do you like your steak cooked?"],"body":"For thick steaks (an inch and a half or more), the reverse sear is foolproof: salt the steak ahead (an hour or overnight, uncovered in the fridge), cook it on the indirect side of the grill at around 225–275°F until it's about 10–15°F below your target, then sear it over screaming-hot direct heat for a minute or so per side to build the crust.\n\nFor thinner steaks, go hot and fast over direct heat the whole time, flipping every minute or so for an even cook.\n\nPull temperatures (it climbs a few degrees while resting): rare about 120°F, medium-rare about 125–130°F, medium about 135–140°F. An instant-read thermometer is the best grilling tool you can buy. Rest five to ten minutes, slice against the grain, and finish with flaky salt. Ribeye is forgiving thanks to its fat; strip and sirloin are great values; flank and skirt love a marinade and quick sear. Food-safety guidance for ground beef is 160°F, since grinding spreads surface bacteria through the meat."},{"id":"conv-downtime#4","title":"Road trip in the Santa Fe","tags":["road trip","travel","Santa Fe","packing","driving","car"],"ask":["Where are you thinking of heading?","Kids along, or a guys' trip?"],"body":"A good road trip is half planning, half leaving room for detours.\n\nBefore you go: check tire pressures cold against the door placard (not the sidewall), tread depth, wipers, fluids, and any service coming due — BlackLab's Garage shows what's next. Load the heaviest items low and forward in the cargo area, and stay within the vehicle's payload rating.\n\nPack a car kit: phone chargers and a USB cable for Android Auto, a tire inflator and plug kit, jumper pack, first-aid kit, flashlight, water, snacks, paper towels, wet wipes and a blanket. Download offline maps for dead zones, and line up podcasts, audiobooks and playlists — BlackLab's music and recordings show up right on the car's screen.\n\nOn the road: plan stops every two hours or so, swap drivers, avoid driving drowsy, and use voice for calls, navigation and notes. Use BlackLab's Garage to log fill-ups and see real-world mpg on the highway. Build in one unplanned stop a day — the roadside diner or overlook is often the best part."},{"id":"conv-downtime#5","title":"Fishing for beginners and returners","tags":["fishing","bass fishing","outdoors","tackle","lakes"],"ask":["Lake, river or saltwater near you?","Want a simple starter tackle list?"],"body":"Fishing is a great reset — quiet, outside, and a perfect thing to do with kids or friends.\n\nStarter setup: a medium-action spinning rod around 6.5 to 7 feet with a 2500-size reel and 8–10 lb line handles most freshwater fishing. Add hooks, split shot, bobbers, a few soft-plastic worms, a couple of inline spinners, and a pair of pliers. Live bait like nightcrawlers catches nearly everything.\n\nWhere and when: early morning and evening are prime, especially in summer. Look for structure — weed edges, fallen trees, docks, drop-offs and points — where fish hide and feed. Overcast days can keep the bite going.\n\nFor bass, a Texas-rigged soft-plastic worm worked slowly along the bottom is a classic. For panfish, a small hook under a bobber with a piece of worm is nonstop action for kids.\n\nGet a fishing license where required, learn local size and creel limits, and practice catch-and-release handling: wet hands, minimal time out of the water."},{"id":"conv-downtime#6","title":"Weekend garage and DIY projects","tags":["DIY","garage","projects","tools","workshop","home improvement"],"ask":["What tools do you already have?","Want a project you can finish in one afternoon?"],"body":"Satisfying projects you can knock out in a weekend:\n- Garage organization: wall-mounted rails or a French cleat system, overhead storage racks, and a pegboard tool wall. A clean garage makes every future project faster.\n- Build a sturdy workbench from 2x4s and plywood — a classic first build that pays off for decades.\n- Upgrade lighting: LED shop lights transform a dim garage.\n- Smart home basics: smart thermostat, video doorbell or leak sensors near the water heater and washer (you'll appreciate the facilities logic).\n- Exterior refresh: power-wash the deck and siding, re-stain a fence, or re-caulk windows.\n- Car detailing day for the Santa Fe: clay bar, polish and a ceramic spray sealant.\n\nTool advice: buy once, cry once on the tools you'll use constantly (drill/driver, impact driver, circular saw, a good tape measure, level and stud finder); rent or borrow specialty tools. Stick to one battery platform. Safety glasses and hearing protection always — and know when to call a licensed pro for electrical, gas or structural work."},{"id":"conv-downtime#7","title":"Music for the drive","tags":["music","playlists","classic rock","90s","road trip music","podcasts"],"ask":["What were you listening to in high school?","Want a playlist theme for your next drive?"],"body":"Turning 40 means your high school soundtrack was probably the late '90s and early 2000s — a great era for drive music. Some playlist themes to build:\n- Rock radio throwbacks: Foo Fighters, Linkin Park, Incubus, Matchbox Twenty, Third Eye Blind, Creed, Nickelback (no shame on a road trip), and Green Day.\n- Hip-hop that still hits: OutKast, Jay-Z, Eminem, Nas, Missy Elliott, Kanye's early albums.\n- Classic rock your dad played: Tom Petty, Bob Seger, CCR, Boston, Lynyrd Skynyrd, Eagles.\n- Country for the open road: Brooks & Dunn, George Strait, Alan Jackson, Chris Stapleton, Zach Bryan.\n- Focus drive: instrumental post-rock, film scores, or lo-fi for the commute.\n\nPodcasts and audiobooks are great for long hauls too — history, true stories, sports talk and business books. BlackLab's music player shows your songs, albums, playlists and recordings right on the Santa Fe's screen, and you can listen back to recorded meetings or lectures on the commute."},{"id":"conv-downtime#8","title":"Movie and TV night picks","tags":["movies","TV shows","streaming","recommendations","films"],"ask":["What's the last thing you watched that you loved?","Action, comedy, drama or documentary tonight?"],"body":"Without knowing what's new on streaming this week, here's a way to always find something good: start from what you loved and branch out.\n- If you love tense crime and heists: Heat, The Departed, Sicario, Prisoners, No Country for Old Men, Breaking Bad, Better Call Saul.\n- Big action done right: Mad Max: Fury Road, Top Gun: Maverick, John Wick, the Mission: Impossible films, Gladiator.\n- Smart sci-fi: Interstellar, Arrival, Blade Runner 2049, The Expanse, Edge of Tomorrow.\n- Comedy that still holds up: Office Space (painfully relevant to anyone in operations), Anchorman, Superbad, Step Brothers, Ted Lasso.\n- War and history: Band of Brothers, Saving Private Ryan, Dunkirk, Master and Commander.\n- Documentaries: Free Solo, The Last Dance, Planet Earth.\nAsk me with a mood and I'll narrow it down — and check your streaming services' search for availability, since catalogs change constantly."},{"id":"conv-downtime#9","title":"Video games, old and new","tags":["video games","gaming","retro games","consoles","co-op"],"ask":["What did you play growing up?","Looking for something to play with your kids?"],"body":"If you grew up in the '90s, your gaming roots might include the Super Nintendo, N64 GoldenEye and Mario Kart, PlayStation classics like Metal Gear Solid and Final Fantasy VII, or Halo LAN parties on the original Xbox.\n\nFor limited time as an adult, great picks are games you can play in short sessions: roguelikes (Hades), sports games, puzzle and indie gems, or a good racing game. Story-driven games are great for a weekend binge.\n\nPlaying with kids is a fantastic way to connect: Mario Kart, Minecraft, Rocket League, and co-op games where you work together rather than against each other. Let them teach you; they love being the expert.\n\nRetro gaming is easy now through official classic collections and subscription libraries on modern consoles. Set limits that work for your family, and keep it fun — a few rounds after the kids go to bed is a perfectly good way to unwind."},{"id":"conv-downtime#10","title":"Coffee done right at home","tags":["coffee","brewing","espresso","pour-over","beans"],"ask":["How do you brew now?","Do you take it black or with cream?"],"body":"Better coffee at home comes down to a few things:\n- Fresh beans: buy whole beans with a roast date, ideally within the last few weeks, and store them airtight away from light and heat.\n- Grind right before brewing with a burr grinder — the single biggest upgrade most people can make.\n- Ratio: start around 1 gram of coffee to 15–17 grams of water (about 2 tablespoons per 6 ounces) and adjust to taste.\n- Water just off the boil, roughly 195–205°F, and good-tasting filtered water.\n- Match grind to method: coarse for French press and cold brew, medium for drip, medium-fine for pour-over, fine for espresso.\n\nIf it's bitter, grind a little coarser or brew shorter; if it's sour or weak, grind finer or use more coffee. Cold brew (coarse grounds steeped 12–18 hours in the fridge) is smooth and great for hot days. And remember the earlier advice about caffeine timing — that 3 p.m. cup can cost you sleep."},{"id":"conv-downtime#11","title":"Craft beer and whiskey basics","tags":["beer","craft beer","whiskey","bourbon","tasting","drinks"],"ask":["Bourbon, scotch or beer guy?","Want a tasting lineup for a get-together?"],"body":"A little knowledge makes tasting more fun.\n\nBeer styles: lagers (crisp and clean — pilsners, Mexican-style lagers), pale ales and IPAs (hop-forward: West Coast is bitter and piney, hazy New England is juicy and soft), stouts and porters (roasty, coffee and chocolate notes), wheat beers (light, sometimes citrusy or banana-clove), and sours (tart). Good non-alcoholic beers are a solid option for weeknights.\n\nWhiskey: bourbon is American whiskey made from at least 51% corn and aged in new charred oak — sweeter, with vanilla and caramel notes. Rye is spicier. Scotch varies by region, from light and fruity to the smoky, peaty Islay malts. Irish whiskey tends to be smooth and approachable.\n\nTasting tips: look, smell, sip, and add a few drops of water to whiskey to open it up. A fun get-together is a flight of three bourbons at different price points, tasted blind. And of course — enjoy responsibly, and never drink and drive; rideshares exist for a reason."},{"id":"conv-downtime#12","title":"Hobbies worth picking up at 40","tags":["hobbies","new skills","leisure","interests","midlife"],"ask":["What did you love doing before life got busy?","Do you want something social or solo?"],"body":"Forty is a great age to start something new — you know yourself better and can afford decent gear. Ideas by type:\n- Hands-on: woodworking, leatherworking, restoring an old car or motorcycle, home brewing, knife sharpening, or 3D printing.\n- Outdoors: hiking, fly fishing, cycling, kayaking, golf, pickleball (social and easy to learn), or disc golf.\n- Mind: learning an instrument (guitar is classic; apps make starting easy), chess, history reading, photography — your phone camera in BlackLab has a Pro mode to learn on.\n- Tech: building a home lab, Raspberry Pi or Arduino projects, home automation, or coding side projects — which you're already doing.\n- Community: coaching your kid's team, volunteering, or joining a club.\n\nTips: start cheap or rent gear until you know you'll stick with it, schedule it like an appointment, and find a buddy or group — it's more fun and you'll actually go. Pick one; dabbling in five at once usually means none stick."},{"id":"conv-downtime#13","title":"Golf for the casual player","tags":["golf","golf tips","driving range","handicap","beginner"],"ask":["How often do you get out?","What's the part of your game that drives you crazy?"],"body":"Golf is a great social sport for the long haul, and you don't need to be good to enjoy it. A few tips that help casual players most:\n- Short game first: most strokes happen within 100 yards. Practicing chipping and putting lowers scores faster than chasing driver distance.\n- Grip and setup fix most problems: a neutral grip, good posture, the ball in the right spot in your stance, and aiming your body correctly.\n- Smooth tempo beats swinging hard. Try swinging at about 80% effort.\n- Play the right tees; it makes the game more fun and faster.\n- On the course, aim for the middle of the green and play to your strengths rather than hero shots.\n- A lesson or two with a pro early saves years of bad habits.\n\nRange sessions with a purpose (a target and a goal for every ball) beat banging a bucket of drivers. Simulators and short courses are great for busy schedules, and a casual scramble with friends is one of the best ways to keep friendships going."},{"id":"conv-downtime#14","title":"Sports talk without the scores","tags":["sports","NFL","college football","baseball","NBA","sports debates"],"ask":["Who's your team?","What's the best game you ever watched live?"],"body":"I don't have live scores, schedules or this week's news, so check a sports app for those — but sports debates are timeless:\n- Greatest of all time arguments: Brady versus Montana, Jordan versus LeBron, the best team that never won it all.\n- Rule debates: should overtime rules give both teams a possession, should the designated hitter have been universal (it is now in MLB), and is the college playoff format fair?\n- Strategy talk: when to go for it on fourth down (analytics says more often than coaches used to), the rise of the three-pointer, and pitch counts versus old-school complete games.\n- Best stadium experiences, tailgating setups, and road trips to see your team play away.\n- Fantasy and pick'em strategy, and how injuries and depth charts change everything.\nTell me your teams and I'll happily argue with you about them — that's half the fun."},{"id":"conv-downtime#15","title":"Camping and outdoor weekends","tags":["camping","hiking","outdoors","family camping","gear"],"ask":["Tent, cabin or car camping?","Who's coming along?"],"body":"Car camping is the easiest way into the outdoors, especially with an SUV like the Santa Fe to haul gear. A solid starter list:\n- Shelter and sleep: a tent rated for one or two more people than you have, sleeping pads (they matter more than the bag for comfort), sleeping bags rated for the night's low, and pillows from home.\n- Kitchen: a two-burner camp stove, fuel, a cooler with block ice (it lasts longer than cubes), a pot and pan, utensils, a wash basin, biodegradable soap and trash bags.\n- Light and comfort: headlamps, a lantern, camp chairs and layers including a rain jacket.\n- Safety: first-aid kit, water, a map, knowledge of fire restrictions, and food stored properly — bear boxes where required.\n\nPlan simple meals: foil-packet dinners, breakfast burritos and s'mores. Do a backyard test run with the kids first. Leave No Trace: pack out everything, camp on durable surfaces and leave campsites better than you found them. Reservations at popular parks fill up months ahead, so book early."},{"id":"conv-downtime#16","title":"Trivia and fun facts for the road","tags":["trivia","fun facts","games","road trip games","kids"],"ask":["Want me to quiz you?","Pick a category — history, science, sports or geography?"],"body":"A few road-trip-ready facts and games:\n- Honeybees communicate the location of flowers with a \"waggle dance.\"\n- Octopuses have three hearts and blue blood.\n- The Eiffel Tower can grow several inches taller in summer as the iron expands in the heat.\n- Bananas are technically berries, and strawberries technically aren't.\n- A day on Venus is longer than its year.\n- The shortest war in recorded history, between Britain and Zanzibar in 1896, lasted under an hour.\n- Wombat poop is cube-shaped.\n\nGames for the car: twenty questions; the alphabet game with signs and license plates; \"would you rather\"; name a song from its first few seconds; and a geography chain where each place must start with the last letter of the previous one. Ask me for a quick quiz in any category and I'll keep score."}]},{"id":"conv-health","title":"Health & Performance at 40","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"Training, energy, sleep, food, stress and checkups — straight talk about staying strong and sharp in your 40s.","icon":"","starters":["How should I train at 40?","Why am I tired all the time?","What checkups should I get at 40?","How much protein do I need?","How do I handle stress better?"],"draws":["conv-health"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-health#1","title":"Training after 40","tags":["strength training","exercise","workout","fitness","muscle","aging"],"ask":["How many days a week can you realistically train?","Any injuries or nagging joints?"],"body":"After 40, strength training becomes the most important exercise most guys aren't doing enough of. Muscle and strength decline gradually with age unless you train them, and keeping them protects joints, metabolism, balance and independence later on.\n\nA simple, effective plan: two to four strength sessions a week built on big movement patterns — squat or leg press, hinge (deadlift or hip thrust), push (press), pull (rows, pull-ups or pulldowns), and carries. Two to four sets of roughly six to twelve reps, stopping one to three reps short of failure, adding weight or reps over time. Warm up properly; your joints appreciate ten minutes of easy movement now more than they did at 25.\n\nThe general public-health guideline is at least 150 minutes a week of moderate cardio (or 75 vigorous) plus muscle strengthening on two or more days. Consistency beats intensity — a plan you'll follow for years beats a brutal one you quit in March. If you're starting from zero or have heart risk factors, a quick check with your doctor first is smart."},{"id":"conv-health#2","title":"Zone 2 cardio and VO2 max","tags":["cardio","zone 2","VO2 max","endurance","heart health"],"ask":["What cardio do you enjoy?","Want a simple weekly cardio plan?"],"body":"Two kinds of cardio give big returns. Zone 2 is steady, easy-to-moderate effort — you can talk in full sentences but you'd rather not sing. It builds your aerobic base, improves how your body uses fat and sugar, and is easy to recover from. Brisk walking uphill, cycling, rowing or an easy jog all work; two to four sessions of 30 to 60 minutes a week is a common target.\n\nThen a little high intensity: once a week, intervals like four rounds of four minutes hard with three minutes easy. That pushes VO2 max — your maximum oxygen use — which is one of the strongest predictors of long-term health and longevity in research.\n\nRoughly 80% easy and 20% hard is a good balance. Wearables estimate zones and VO2 max reasonably well, but your breathing is a fine guide. And walking counts: a daily walk, especially after meals, helps blood sugar, mood and recovery more than people expect."},{"id":"conv-health#3","title":"Sleep as a performance tool","tags":["sleep","rest","recovery","energy","insomnia"],"ask":["How many hours are you actually getting?","What's your evening routine like?"],"body":"Most adults need at least seven hours a night, and many perform best closer to eight. Short sleep drags down everything: mood, focus, appetite control, blood pressure, testosterone, workout recovery and patience with people.\n\nHabits that help most:\n- A consistent wake time, even on weekends — it anchors your body clock.\n- Morning daylight for ten to twenty minutes.\n- Caffeine cut off about eight to ten hours before bed; it lingers longer than you think.\n- Alcohol limited, especially close to bedtime — it knocks you out but wrecks sleep quality.\n- A cool, dark, quiet bedroom.\n- A wind-down routine: dim lights, phone away, something boring or relaxing.\n- If your mind races, write tomorrow's to-do list before bed.\n\nLoud snoring, gasping, or waking unrefreshed despite enough hours can signal sleep apnea, which is common in men over 40 and very treatable — worth bringing up with a doctor."},{"id":"conv-health#4","title":"Protein and eating for strength","tags":["nutrition","protein","diet","muscle","eating"],"ask":["What does a typical day of eating look like for you?","Want easy high-protein meal ideas?"],"body":"If you lift, protein matters. A well-supported target for building and keeping muscle is around 1.6 grams per kilogram of body weight a day — roughly 0.7 grams per pound — and many lifters go a bit higher with no problem for healthy kidneys. Spread it across three or four meals of about 30 to 50 grams each.\n\nEasy sources: eggs, Greek yogurt, cottage cheese, chicken, turkey, lean beef, fish, beans and lentils, tofu, and a protein shake when you're busy. Build meals around a protein, add vegetables or fruit, then add carbs to match your activity.\n\nBeyond protein, the boring basics win: mostly minimally processed food, plenty of fiber (vegetables, fruit, beans, whole grains), healthy fats, and fewer sugary drinks and ultra-processed snacks. Don't overthink supplements — creatine monohydrate is one of the most researched and useful for strength, and vitamin D is worth testing — but food quality, protein and consistency do the heavy lifting. Anyone with kidney disease or other conditions should check with their doctor."},{"id":"conv-health#5","title":"Losing fat without losing your mind","tags":["weight loss","fat loss","calories","diet","body composition"],"ask":["What's worked or failed for you before?","Is it more about energy or appearance for you?"],"body":"Fat loss comes down to eating somewhat less energy than you burn, but how you do it decides whether it lasts.\n\nWhat works for most guys: a modest deficit (losing roughly half a pound to a pound a week), high protein so you keep muscle and stay full, strength training so the weight lost is fat, daily steps, and good sleep — short sleep makes you hungrier. Track for a couple of weeks to learn what your portions really are; you don't have to track forever.\n\nPractical tricks: a high-protein breakfast, vegetables at every meal, water or zero-calorie drinks instead of sugary ones, fewer liquid calories from alcohol, a plan for the 9 p.m. snack attack, and not keeping trigger foods at home. Expect plateaus and scale noise; watch the trend over weeks, plus your waist measurement and how clothes fit.\n\nPrescription weight-loss medications have changed the landscape for people with obesity or related conditions; that's a conversation for your doctor, ideally alongside strength training to protect muscle."},{"id":"conv-health#6","title":"Energy slumps and feeling tired","tags":["fatigue","energy","tiredness","afternoon slump"],"ask":["When during the day do you crash?","How's your sleep and caffeine?"],"body":"Feeling tired all the time is common at 40, and usually has a few ordinary causes stacked together: not enough or poor-quality sleep, too much caffeine late in the day masking it, big carb-heavy lunches, dehydration, sitting for hours, stress, and alcohol.\n\nThings to try for two weeks: a consistent sleep schedule, caffeine only before noon or early afternoon, a protein-forward lunch, a ten-minute walk after meals, water through the day, and a short movement break every hour.\n\nIf you're still exhausted despite fixing the basics — or you notice weight changes, low mood, snoring, shortness of breath or other new symptoms — get checked. Doctors commonly look at things like thyroid function, iron and vitamin levels, blood sugar, sleep apnea and mood. Persistent fatigue is a real symptom worth a visit, not something to push through forever."},{"id":"conv-health#7","title":"Checkups and screenings at 40","tags":["checkups","screening","preventive care","doctor","physical"],"ask":["When was your last physical?","Any family history that worries you?"],"body":"Your 40s are the decade to get a baseline and stay on schedule. Commonly recommended for men, based on U.S. preventive guidelines — your doctor tailors it to your history:\n- Blood pressure checked at least yearly; high blood pressure often has no symptoms.\n- Cholesterol and overall heart-risk assessment.\n- Diabetes or prediabetes screening, especially if you carry extra weight.\n- Colorectal cancer screening starting at 45 for average risk — earlier with family history.\n- Skin checks if you've had lots of sun or have many moles.\n- Eye exams, dental cleanings and a hearing check if you've worked around noise.\n- Vaccines kept current (tetanus booster, flu, and others your doctor recommends).\n- Prostate cancer screening is a shared decision usually discussed from about 55, earlier for higher-risk men, such as those with a family history.\n- Mental health check-ins count too.\nBring your family history to the appointment — it changes what's recommended."},{"id":"conv-health#8","title":"Joints, back pain and desk life","tags":["back pain","joints","mobility","posture","desk work","knees"],"ask":["Where does it hurt — back, knees, shoulders?","How many hours do you sit a day?"],"body":"Aches become more common in your 40s, but most respond well to movement rather than rest. For everyday low back stiffness from sitting: get up every 30 to 60 minutes, walk daily, and strengthen the hips and trunk — glute bridges, bird-dogs, side planks, carries, and hip hinges. There's no single perfect posture; the best posture is your next one.\n\nFor knees and shoulders, strength training with good form and gradual progress usually helps rather than hurts. Warm up, and adjust exercises around pain instead of stopping everything — for example box squats instead of deep squats, or neutral-grip presses.\n\nMobility work for ten minutes a few times a week (hips, upper back, ankles) keeps you moving well.\n\nSee a doctor or physical therapist for pain after a fall or injury, pain that's getting worse or lasts more than a few weeks, numbness, tingling or weakness down a leg, or pain with fever or unexplained weight loss. Sudden severe symptoms or loss of bladder or bowel control need urgent care."},{"id":"conv-health#9","title":"Stress, burnout and staying level","tags":["stress","burnout","mental health","resilience","work pressure"],"ask":["What's piling on most right now?","What helps you decompress?"],"body":"Running operations, going to school and having a life is a heavy load. Burnout shows up as exhaustion, cynicism and feeling ineffective — and it creeps.\n\nWhat helps:\n- Physical outlets: training, walks and time outside lower stress hormones and clear your head.\n- Recovery that's real: protect sleep, take actual days off, and have evenings where work stays shut.\n- Boundaries: fewer commitments, delegating, and saying no to the good-but-not-important.\n- Breathing: a few minutes of slow breathing (longer exhales than inhales) settles the nervous system fast — useful before a tough meeting.\n- Connection: talk to friends, a partner, or a mentor. Men often isolate under stress, which makes it worse.\n- Perspective: write down what you control and act on one thing.\n\nIf stress turns into persistent low mood, anxiety, irritability, drinking more, or losing interest in things you enjoy, talking to a doctor or therapist is a smart, strong move. If you ever have thoughts of harming yourself, call or text 988 in the U.S. — the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline — any time."},{"id":"conv-health#10","title":"Testosterone: myth and reality","tags":["testosterone","hormones","low T","men's health","energy"],"ask":["What made you curious about testosterone?","How are sleep, weight and training going?"],"body":"Testosterone does decline gradually with age — roughly about 1% a year after 30 on average — but most men don't have clinically low levels. Symptoms people blame on \"low T\" (fatigue, low drive, weight gain, low mood) often come from poor sleep, extra body fat, stress, alcohol, inactivity or other health issues.\n\nThe things that support healthy levels are the same fundamentals: seven to nine hours of sleep, losing excess fat, strength training, enough calories and protein, managing stress, and moderating alcohol. Sleep apnea and obesity are big, fixable drivers.\n\nIf symptoms persist, ask a doctor for proper testing — typically morning blood tests repeated to confirm — and a look at causes. Testosterone therapy is a medical treatment with real benefits for men who need it and real considerations (fertility, blood counts, monitoring). Steer clear of \"T booster\" supplements and clinics that prescribe without proper testing."},{"id":"conv-health#11","title":"Alcohol, honestly","tags":["alcohol","drinking","beer","health","sleep"],"ask":["How much are you drinking in a typical week?","Want ideas for cutting back without being the odd one out?"],"body":"Alcohol is woven into social life, but it costs more as you get older: worse sleep, slower recovery, extra calories, higher blood pressure, and next-day mood and focus. Recent research has moved away from the idea that moderate drinking is good for your heart; in general, less is better for health.\n\nIf you want to cut back without drama: set drink-free days, decide your number before you go out, alternate with water or sparkling water, try good non-alcoholic beers (they've gotten genuinely good), skip the nightcap since it hurts sleep most, and notice which situations trigger the extra drinks — stress, boredom, certain people.\n\nTrack a month and see how you feel; many guys notice better sleep, energy and weight within a couple of weeks. If cutting back is harder than expected, or drinking is causing problems at home or work, that's worth talking to a doctor about — there are good, confidential options."},{"id":"conv-health#12","title":"Building habits that stick","tags":["habits","consistency","motivation","routines","behavior change"],"ask":["What habit do you want to build first?","What usually derails you?"],"body":"Motivation is unreliable; systems aren't. A few principles from behavior science:\n- Start small enough that it's almost silly: ten push-ups, a ten-minute walk, one glass of water before coffee. Grow it once it's automatic.\n- Anchor it to an existing routine: \"After I park at work, I walk one lap of the building.\"\n- Make it obvious and easy: gym bag packed by the door, healthy food at eye level, shoes out.\n- Make the bad habits harder: phone charging outside the bedroom, snacks not in the house.\n- Track it simply — a check on a calendar or in BlackLab's tasks with a repeat rule — and don't miss twice in a row.\n- Plan for obstacles: \"If I work late, I do a 15-minute home workout instead.\"\n- Identity helps: \"I'm someone who trains\" beats \"I'm trying to work out.\"\nExpect a few weeks to a couple of months before a habit feels automatic. Missing a day doesn't reset you; quitting does."},{"id":"conv-health#13","title":"Recovery, rest days and avoiding injury","tags":["recovery","rest","injury prevention","overtraining","soreness"],"ask":["How sore are you after workouts?","Do you take full rest days?"],"body":"Training breaks you down; recovery is where you get stronger. After 40, recovery takes a bit longer, so plan for it.\n\nBasics: sleep, protein and total calories matter more than any gadget. Space hard sessions for the same muscles about 48 hours apart. Take at least one or two easier days a week — walking and light mobility count. Every six to eight weeks, a lighter \"deload\" week helps joints and motivation.\n\nSigns you're overdoing it: lingering soreness, nagging joint pain, worse sleep, irritability, stalled progress and a higher resting heart rate. Back off for a few days rather than grinding through.\n\nInjury prevention: warm up, increase load gradually (avoid big jumps after time off), keep good form over heavier weight, and include single-leg and core work. Muscle soreness is normal; sharp or joint pain isn't. Pain that changes how you move, swelling, or anything that doesn't improve in a week or two deserves a look from a doctor or physical therapist."},{"id":"conv-health#14","title":"Hydration and caffeine","tags":["hydration","water","caffeine","coffee","energy drinks"],"ask":["How much coffee are you drinking?","Do you get headaches in the afternoon?"],"body":"Most people do fine drinking water with meals and when thirsty, with more in heat, during workouts, and if you're outdoors on the job. Pale-yellow urine is a simple check. Mild dehydration shows up as headaches, fatigue and poor focus — easy to mistake for needing more coffee.\n\nCaffeine is useful: it improves alertness and workout performance. For most healthy adults, up to about 400 milligrams a day — roughly four cups of brewed coffee — is generally considered safe, but watch energy drinks and pre-workouts, which can stack a lot quickly. Caffeine's half-life is around five hours, so a 3 p.m. coffee can still be in your system at bedtime.\n\nIf you rely on caffeine to get through every afternoon, that's usually a sleep signal. Try a short walk, water and daylight before the second or third cup. Anyone with heart rhythm issues, high blood pressure or anxiety should talk to their doctor about caffeine limits."}]},{"id":"conv-money-home","title":"Money, Home & Family","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"Budgets, retirement, insurance, the house, the car, kids, parents and the people who matter — practical talk for the middle of life.","icon":"","starters":["Am I on track for retirement at 40?","How big should my emergency fund be?","What should I keep up with around the house each season?","How do I talk money with my partner?","What insurance do I actually need?"],"draws":["conv-money-home"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-money-home#1","title":"Retirement check-in at 40","tags":["retirement","401k","IRA","saving","investing","on track"],"ask":["Are you getting your full employer match?","Roughly what percent of income are you saving now?"],"body":"Forty is a great time for a retirement check-in: you still have a couple of decades of compounding, and course corrections are cheap.\n\nRules of thumb (rough benchmarks, not verdicts): many planners suggest saving about 15% of income toward retirement including any employer match, and a common milestone is having around two to three times your annual salary saved by 40. If you're behind, you're in good company — raising your savings rate a point or two each year closes the gap surprisingly fast.\n\nPriorities often look like this: contribute enough to your 401(k) or 403(b) to get the full employer match (it's free money); pay off high-interest debt; then consider a Roth or traditional IRA and more workplace contributions; and if eligible, a Health Savings Account. Low-cost, diversified index funds or target-date funds keep things simple. Contribution limits change each year — check the current IRS figures — and catch-up contributions open up at 50. A fee-only fiduciary planner can run real numbers for your situation."},{"id":"conv-money-home#2","title":"Emergency fund","tags":["emergency fund","savings","cash","rainy day"],"ask":["How stable is your income?","Where are you keeping your cash now?"],"body":"An emergency fund turns crises into inconveniences. The common guideline is three to six months of essential expenses — rent or mortgage, utilities, food, insurance, transportation and minimum debt payments — not your full income. Lean toward six months or more if your income is variable, you're the sole earner, or your industry is cyclical.\n\nKeep it boring and reachable: a high-yield savings account or money market fund, separate from everyday checking so it isn't spent by accident. If you're starting from zero, build a starter cushion of one month first, then grow it while you pay down high-interest debt.\n\nWhat counts as an emergency: job loss, medical bills, urgent car or home repairs. What doesn't: sales, vacations or the new grill — those get their own savings buckets. Refill it after you use it. Knowing it's there lowers stress in a way few other financial moves do."},{"id":"conv-money-home#3","title":"Budgeting that actually works","tags":["budget","budgeting","spending","50/30/20","zero-based"],"ask":["Do you know roughly where your money goes each month?","Want to try a simple budget for a month?"],"body":"The best budget is the one you'll keep using. A few approaches:\n- 50/30/20: about 50% of take-home pay to needs, 30% to wants, and 20% to savings and extra debt payments. Simple guardrails rather than tracking every coffee.\n- Zero-based: give every dollar a job each month (the YNAB approach). Very effective for getting control, but takes more attention.\n- Pay yourself first: automate savings and investments on payday, then spend what's left freely. Low effort, high results.\n\nStart by looking at three months of statements and categorizing spending — subscriptions and food delivery usually surprise people. Automate the important stuff: savings transfers, retirement contributions and bill payments. Do a 20-minute monthly money check-in, ideally with your partner, and an annual review of insurance, subscriptions and big goals. Budgets fail when they're too strict; build in fun money so you don't blow it up in frustration."},{"id":"conv-money-home#4","title":"Paying off debt","tags":["debt","credit cards","avalanche","snowball","loans","interest"],"ask":["What kinds of debt are you carrying and at what rates?","Do you prefer quick wins or the math-optimal path?"],"body":"List every debt with its balance, interest rate and minimum payment. Pay minimums on all of them, then throw every extra dollar at one target:\n- Avalanche: highest interest rate first. Mathematically saves the most money.\n- Snowball: smallest balance first. Quick wins build momentum, which keeps many people going.\nEither beats doing nothing; choose the one you'll stick with.\n\nCredit card debt at typical card rates is an emergency in slow motion — attacking it often beats investing. Options to speed things up: a 0% balance-transfer card (watch the transfer fee and the end date), a lower-rate personal loan for consolidation, or calling issuers to ask for a lower rate. Avoid running balances back up after consolidating.\n\nLower-rate debts like a mortgage or older auto loans can be paid on schedule while you invest, though paying them early is a fine, guaranteed-return choice if it gives you peace of mind."},{"id":"conv-money-home#5","title":"Investing basics","tags":["investing","index funds","stocks","bonds","diversification","fees","asset allocation"],"ask":["How comfortable are you with market drops?","Do you know what fees you're paying?"],"body":"For most people, simple beats clever:\n- Diversify broadly with low-cost index funds — total U.S. stock market, total international, and bonds — or a single target-date fund that rebalances for you.\n- Asset allocation (the mix of stocks and bonds) drives most of your results and your risk. More stocks means more growth and bigger swings; the right mix depends on your timeline and how well you sleep during a 30% drop.\n- Keep costs low: expense ratios under about 0.2% are common for index funds. High fees compound against you for decades.\n- Automate contributions and stay invested; trying to time the market usually backfires.\n- Rebalance once a year or when your mix drifts significantly.\n- Mind taxes: use tax-advantaged accounts first, and hold tax-inefficient assets inside them where possible.\n\nIndividual stocks, crypto and hot tips can be a small \"fun money\" slice if you want, but the core should be boring. If someone promises high returns with low risk, walk away."},{"id":"conv-money-home#6","title":"Saving for kids' college","tags":["college savings","529 plan","kids","education","financial aid"],"ask":["How old are your kids?","Is your own retirement on track first?"],"body":"A 529 plan is the go-to college savings account in the U.S.: money grows tax-free and withdrawals for qualified education expenses are tax-free. Many states also give a state tax deduction for contributions to their plan. Grandparents can contribute too.\n\nRecent rules added flexibility: limited amounts of 529 money can pay for K–12 tuition, apprenticeships and student loans, and leftover funds can, under certain conditions and lifetime limits, roll into the beneficiary's Roth IRA. Check current rules before relying on any of these.\n\nThe classic advice still holds: fund your own retirement first. Kids can borrow for college; you can't borrow for retirement. A middle path is to save a steady amount for college while keeping retirement on track, and talk honestly with your kids about costs, community college, in-state options, scholarships and work.\n\nFinancial aid formulas treat parent-owned 529s fairly gently compared with money in the child's name."},{"id":"conv-money-home#7","title":"Insurance you actually need","tags":["insurance","life insurance","disability","umbrella","health","coverage"],"ask":["Does anyone depend on your income?","Do you know what your disability coverage is at work?"],"body":"Insurance protects against financial catastrophes, not small expenses. The core list for a working parent:\n- Health insurance, with an out-of-pocket maximum you could handle.\n- Term life insurance if anyone depends on your income. A common rule of thumb is coverage of around ten to twelve times income, for a term that lasts until the kids are grown and the mortgage is manageable. Term is much cheaper than whole life for most needs.\n- Long-term disability insurance: your ability to earn is your biggest asset, and disability is more likely than early death. Check what your employer provides and whether it's enough.\n- Homeowners or renters insurance with adequate replacement cost coverage.\n- Auto insurance with liability limits well above state minimums.\n- An umbrella policy (often $1 million or more) once you have assets to protect — it's surprisingly affordable.\n\nRaise deductibles to lower premiums once you have an emergency fund. Review coverage yearly and after big life changes. Skip extended warranties and most small add-ons."},{"id":"conv-money-home#8","title":"The house: seasonal maintenance","tags":["home maintenance","house","seasonal checklist","HVAC","roof","gutters"],"ask":["What season are we heading into?","What's the oldest major system in your house?"],"body":"You run facilities for a living, so you know preventive maintenance beats emergency repairs. A homeowner's version:\n- Spring: HVAC tune-up before cooling season and replace filters; clean gutters and check downspouts drain away from the foundation; inspect the roof for damaged shingles and flashing; check exterior caulk and paint; test sump pump; service the lawn mower.\n- Summer: check irrigation; clean dryer vent and lint path (a fire risk); inspect decks and fences; watch for pests.\n- Fall: heating system service; clean gutters again after leaves drop; shut off and drain exterior hose bibs; seal drafts around doors and windows; sweep and inspect the chimney if you have one; test smoke and carbon monoxide detectors.\n- Winter: watch for ice dams; keep pipes in unheated spaces from freezing; check the attic for leaks.\nMonthly: HVAC filters as needed and a quick walk-around. Yearly: water heater flush, test GFCI outlets. BlackLab tasks with repeat rules make a great home PM schedule."},{"id":"conv-money-home#9","title":"Cars: owning smart","tags":["car ownership","auto maintenance","car buying","total cost","vehicle"],"ask":["Are you keeping your current car long-term?","Want to set up maintenance reminders in the Garage?"],"body":"The cheapest car is usually the one you already own, well maintained. Following the maintenance schedule, rotating tires, and fixing small issues early lets a modern vehicle run well past 150,000 miles. BlackLab's Garage tracks your Santa Fe's schedule, fuel economy and service history so nothing slips.\n\nWhen buying, think total cost of ownership: depreciation (the biggest cost on new cars), insurance, fuel, maintenance, financing and taxes. Common guidance is to keep total car costs to a modest share of income and to finance for as short a term as you can afford — long loans mean paying interest while the car depreciates. Get pre-approved by a credit union before visiting a dealer, negotiate the out-the-door price rather than the monthly payment, and skip most add-ons.\n\nTires, brakes and battery are the usual surprise expenses; budget a few hundred dollars a year for maintenance and tuck it into a sinking fund so repairs don't hit the emergency fund."},{"id":"conv-money-home#10","title":"Taxes and the HSA","tags":["taxes","HSA","tax-advantaged accounts","deductions","tax planning"],"ask":["Are you on a high-deductible health plan?","Do you do your own taxes?"],"body":"A few tax ideas worth knowing (rules change, so verify current details or ask a tax pro):\n- Use tax-advantaged accounts: 401(k) or 403(b), IRAs, 529s and HSAs.\n- Health Savings Accounts are uniquely powerful if you have a qualifying high-deductible health plan: contributions are tax-deductible, growth is tax-free, and withdrawals for qualified medical expenses are tax-free. Many people pay current medical costs out of pocket, keep receipts, and let the HSA grow invested as a stealth retirement account.\n- Roth versus traditional: traditional saves taxes now; Roth saves taxes later. Having both gives flexibility in retirement.\n- Adjust withholding so you're not giving the government a big interest-free loan or facing a surprise bill.\n- Keep records: receipts for deductible expenses, charitable gifts, education credits, and home improvement costs (they can reduce taxes when you sell).\n- Tuition and education credits may apply to your own college courses.\nIf your situation is complex — a side business, rental property, equity compensation — a good CPA or enrolled agent pays for themselves."},{"id":"conv-money-home#11","title":"Estate basics everyone should have","tags":["estate planning","will","beneficiaries","power of attorney","guardianship"],"ask":["Do you have a will and named guardians for the kids?","When did you last check your beneficiaries?"],"body":"Estate planning isn't just for the wealthy; it's about making things easier for the people you love.\n- A will names guardians for minor children and says who gets what. Without one, state law decides.\n- Beneficiary designations on retirement accounts, life insurance and some bank accounts override your will — check them after marriage, divorce or new kids.\n- Durable power of attorney for finances: someone you trust can manage money if you can't.\n- Health care directive and health care power of attorney: your wishes and who decides medical care if you can't.\n- A revocable living trust can help avoid probate and manage assets for kids, depending on your state and situation.\n- A \"where everything is\" document: accounts, insurance, passwords approach (a password manager with emergency access), and contacts.\nAn estate attorney can set up the core documents affordably. Review every few years or after big life changes."},{"id":"conv-money-home#12","title":"Talking money with your partner","tags":["relationships","money conversations","marriage","couples","finances"],"ask":["Do you two have a regular money check-in?","Where do you and your partner differ most on money?"],"body":"Money is one of the top sources of tension for couples, usually because of different values and habits rather than math. A few practices help:\n- A regular, low-key money date — monthly, with coffee, not during a stressful moment. Review spending, upcoming expenses and progress on goals.\n- Start with goals and values: what do you each want life to look like in five and twenty years? Security, travel, the kids' education, early retirement?\n- Agree on a structure: fully joint, fully separate, or a hybrid with a joint account for shared expenses plus personal fun money for each of you, no questions asked. The hybrid works well for many couples.\n- Set a threshold for purchases you discuss first.\n- Share the work: both partners should know where accounts are and how bills get paid.\n- Talk about money backgrounds — how each of your families handled it shapes your instincts.\nLead with curiosity rather than blame. You're on the same team against the problem."},{"id":"conv-money-home#13","title":"Parenting in the middle years","tags":["parenting","kids","fatherhood","family","teenagers"],"ask":["How old are your kids?","What's the toughest part of parenting right now?"],"body":"Busy dads in their 40s often feel stretched between work, school and family. What tends to matter most to kids is surprisingly simple: regular, undistracted time and feeling known.\n\nIdeas that work:\n- Small rituals: a weekly breakfast, a drive together, a shared show or game, bedtime check-ins. Consistency beats grand gestures.\n- Side-by-side time works especially well with sons and teens — car rides, projects, fishing — conversation flows when you're not face to face.\n- Ask better questions than \"how was school?\": \"What was the best and worst part of your day?\" or \"Who made you laugh today?\"\n- Model what you want: how you handle stress, mistakes, money and your partner teaches more than lectures.\n- Teach real skills as they grow: cooking, basic repairs, money (paying them to do bigger chores and helping them save), driving.\n- Screens: agree on family rules and follow them yourself.\n- Apologize when you blow it. Repair builds trust.\nYou don't need to be perfect; you need to be present and keep showing up."},{"id":"conv-money-home#14","title":"Aging parents","tags":["aging parents","caregiving","elder care","family","planning"],"ask":["How are your parents doing these days?","Have you talked with them about their wishes?"],"body":"Many people in their 40s become part of the \"sandwich generation,\" caring for kids and aging parents at once. Getting ahead of it reduces crisis decisions later.\n\nStart the conversation early and gently: their wishes for where to live as they age, their health care preferences, and whether they have a will, powers of attorney and health care directives. Ask where important documents and accounts are. Frame it as \"I want to make sure I can do what you'd want.\"\n\nPractical steps: know their doctors and medications; look at home safety (grab bars, lighting, removing trip hazards — your facilities eye is useful here); understand their finances enough to spot scams, which target older adults heavily; and learn what Medicare does and doesn't cover, especially for long-term care. Share responsibilities with siblings clearly, and use local resources like your Area Agency on Aging.\n\nTake care of yourself too — caregiver burnout is real. Accept help and protect some time for your own health and family."},{"id":"conv-money-home#15","title":"Friendships and connection as an adult","tags":["friendship","connection","loneliness","social life","men"],"ask":["Who do you call when something big happens?","When did you last see your friends in person?"],"body":"Many men find friendships fade in their 30s and 40s — not from conflict, but from schedules, kids, moves and work. Loneliness quietly affects health as much as many better-known risks, so it's worth effort.\n\nWhat helps:\n- Recurring plans beat one-offs: a monthly poker night, a weekly gym time with a buddy, a standing golf or fishing day, a yearly guys' trip. Put it on the calendar and protect it.\n- Shared activities make it easy: rec leagues, a running or cycling group, volunteering, a class, a church or community group.\n- Be the organizer. Most people are glad someone took the initiative.\n- Small touches keep friendships alive: a text about a game, a quick call on the drive home — Android Auto makes that easy and safe.\n- Go a little deeper sometimes: ask how someone's really doing, and share honestly yourself.\n- Reconnect with old friends; it's rarely as awkward as you expect.\nFriendship is a skill and a habit, not luck."}]},{"id":"conv-peer-lab","title":"Shift & Peer Practice Lab","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"Talk through hard shifts, boundaries, documentation, groups and your own wellbeing as a new MHT and Peer Support Specialist — with a seasoned colleague who's been there.","icon":"","starters":["Help me debrief a hard shift","How do I handle working with staff I supervise in my facilities role?","What do I say when someone asks for my personal number?","Help me tell my recovery story for the first time","I'm nervous about leading my first group","How do I write a good DAP note?"],"draws":["conv-peer-lab","peer-support","mht-practice"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-peer-lab#1","title":"Debriefing a hard shift","tags":["debrief","hard shift","reflection","incident","processing","after shift"],"ask":["What part of the shift keeps replaying?","Did you get a chance to debrief with the team?"],"body":"A hard shift deserves a few minutes of real processing before you carry it home. Walk through it in order: what happened, what you noticed in yourself, what you did, what worked and what you'd try differently. Separate what was in your control from what wasn't — on a behavioral unit a lot isn't, and good care can still end in a rough outcome.\n\nNotice your body: tension, racing thoughts, adrenaline that hasn't burned off. A short walk, slow breathing or a transition ritual on the drive home helps the nervous system settle. If the shift involved an incident, ask for a formal debrief; it's a normal part of safe practice. And log a quick after-shift check-in in BlackLab — over weeks it shows you patterns you can't see in a single day."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#2","title":"Wearing two hats at the same facility","tags":["dual role","facilities director","coworkers","supervisor","boundaries at work","role clarity"],"ask":["Do any of the staff you work beside on the floor report to you in your facilities role?","Has anyone treated you differently since you started on the unit?"],"body":"Working as an MHT or peer specialist in the building where you're also the Director of Facilities is a real dual relationship, and it can be managed well. Be explicit about which role you're in during each shift: on the floor, you're a tech or peer who takes direction from the charge nurse, even if that nurse files work orders to your department on weekdays.\n\nAvoid using one role's access or authority for the other — don't let unit staff route maintenance requests to you personally mid-shift, and don't use director-level knowledge to bypass unit processes. With patients, your peer relationship should never be shaped by your administrative role. Talk with your supervisor and HR about any staff you directly supervise. The upside is huge: frontline time gives you insight into ligature risks, workflow problems and environmental triggers that can shape better capital and maintenance decisions."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#3","title":"When someone asks for your personal number","tags":["boundaries","personal number","social media","contact","friendship","dual relationship"],"ask":["What does your program allow for contact outside work?","How do you want to say no while keeping the relationship warm?"],"body":"This comes up a lot, especially near discharge when people are scared to lose a connection. A kind, clear answer keeps the relationship and the boundary: \"I'm really glad our talks have helped. I can't give out my personal number — that's a boundary that keeps this work safe for both of us. Let's make sure you have people and places you can reach after you leave.\"\n\nThen actually build that bridge: the community peer program, a recovery center, mutual-aid meetings, warmlines, 988 and the follow-up appointment. If your program offers post-discharge peer calls through a work phone, explain how that works. The same goes for social-media friend requests and running into someone in the community. Bring any gray areas to supervision before they become problems."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#4","title":"Telling your recovery story for the first time","tags":["recovery story","self-disclosure","lived experience","sharing","storytelling","nervous"],"ask":["What's the one thing you want people to take from your story?","Which parts of your story feel private?"],"body":"Your story is a tool, not a confession. Shape it around hope: what it was like, what helped you turn a corner, and what life is like now. Keep the hardest details general — people need to see the path, not relive your worst moments. Decide in advance what's off-limits, and remember you can always say \"that part I keep for myself.\"\n\nPractice out loud a few times until it takes two or three minutes. Then practice the turn back to the other person: \"That's what worked for me — what's been helpful for you?\" Notice how you feel after sharing; if it stirs things up, plan some self-care and mention it in supervision. Over time you'll learn which pieces fit which moments — a one-sentence share is often more powerful than the whole story."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#5","title":"Nerves before leading a first group","tags":["group facilitation","first group","nervous","group planning","co-facilitation"],"ask":["Which group topic are you thinking about?","Will you have a co-facilitator?"],"body":"Nerves before a first group are normal and usually fade within the first five minutes. Pick a well-structured plan — BlackLab's group library has openers, activities, discussion questions and closings ready to go — and rehearse just the opening and the first activity. Arrive early, set up the room, and greet people as they come in.\n\nSet simple ground rules together: one person speaks at a time, what's shared stays in the room, passing is always okay. Expect some silence; count to ten before jumping in. If someone dominates, thank them and invite others (\"Let's hear from someone who hasn't shared yet\"). If someone becomes distressed, check in privately and involve staff if needed. Afterward, write your group note while it's fresh and jot down one thing to change next time."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#6","title":"Writing a good DAP note","tags":["DAP note","documentation","notes","charting","peer note","writing"],"ask":["Do you want to walk through a recent session together, without names?","What format does your program require?"],"body":"A DAP note has three parts. Data: what happened — the type and length of the contact, what the person said (in quotes when it matters), what you observed and what you discussed. Assessment: your peer observations — engagement, progress toward their goals, strengths you noticed and the peer support you provided — without diagnoses or clinical judgments. Plan: next steps for the person and for you, and when you'll meet next.\n\nKeep it factual, brief and respectful; write as if the person will read it, because they may. Document any safety concern and exactly who you told and when. BlackLab's peer note form turns your dropdown choices into a DAP, SOAP, BIRP, GIRP or narrative note you can copy into the EHR — then edit it into your own voice."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#7","title":"When a participant returns to use","tags":["relapse","return to use","recurrence","setback","shame","overdose risk"],"ask":["How are they talking about it — with shame, or with curiosity?","Is there any overdose risk right now?"],"body":"A return to use is a moment for connection, not judgment. Many recovery stories include it, and how people are met afterward shapes whether they come back to support. Start with relief that they're here and talking to you, then get curious: what led up to it, what they noticed, what they want now.\n\nWatch for safety. After a period without opioids, tolerance drops and overdose risk rises sharply, so make sure they know about naloxone and not using alone, and tell the clinical team if there are safety concerns. Revisit their wellness or relapse-prevention plan together and look for the small next step. If it stirs your own feelings — fear, frustration, your own history — take it to supervision. Their recovery is theirs; your job is to stay alongside."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#8","title":"When someone says they want to die","tags":["suicide","safety","crisis","reporting","staying present","988"],"ask":["Did you tell the nurse or your supervisor right away?","How are you doing after that conversation?"],"body":"If someone tells you they want to die, the most important things are to stay with them, stay calm and connect them to clinical help right away. Thank them for trusting you. You can ask directly whether they're thinking about suicide; it won't put the idea in their head. Then tell the nurse or clinician immediately — even if they asked you not to — and stay with them until someone takes over.\n\nAfterward, write down what they said in their words and who you told, and when. Then check on yourself. These conversations are heavy, and it's normal to replay them. Talk with your supervisor, use your team, and if it's hitting hard, use your EAP. If you ever find yourself in that place, please call or text 988 — helpers need help too."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#9","title":"Staying inside the role when you know a lot","tags":["role clarity","scope","advice","medication questions","clinical questions"],"ask":["What question did someone ask that you weren't sure how to handle?","Who on your team is good to bring these to?"],"body":"Life experience and certification training give you a lot of knowledge, and people will ask you things that belong to clinicians: \"Should I stop this medication?\" \"Do you think I'm bipolar?\" \"Will the judge let me go home?\" Answering from your own experience can feel helpful but can cause harm and confuse roles.\n\nTry: \"That's a really important question, and it's one your doctor (or nurse, or lawyer) needs to answer. Want to write it down together so you're ready when you see them?\" Then follow through — help them get the answer. You can share what helped you to ask good questions, or how you handled a side effect conversation with your own prescriber, without advising. This keeps trust high with both the person and the team."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#10","title":"Balancing a new role with a demanding day job","tags":["work-life balance","burnout","part-time","schedule","energy","two jobs"],"ask":["How many hours are you working across both roles this week?","What's one thing you could take off your plate?"],"body":"Adding part-time frontline shifts to a director-level job is a big load, physically and emotionally. The two roles use different energy: facilities work is planning, budgets, vendors and problem-solving; unit work is presence, vigilance and emotion. Back-to-back days can drain you faster than the hours suggest.\n\nProtect recovery time between roles — sleep, food, movement and at least one block each week with nothing scheduled. Use BlackLab's Shift Hub to see your hours over 7 and 30 days, and look at your check-in trends; if stress keeps climbing or \"drained\" scores stay high, adjust your schedule before it adjusts you. Tell your supervisors your limits early. Sustainable beats heroic, and the people you support need you around for the long run."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#11","title":"Handling a boundary push from a coworker","tags":["coworkers","team conflict","boundaries","role drift","staff dynamics"],"ask":["What exactly did they ask you to do?","Is this a one-time thing or a pattern?"],"body":"Sometimes the pressure comes from staff, not patients: being asked to enforce rules in ways that break trust (\"You're the peer, go tell him he lost his phone\"), to report on things a participant shared in confidence beyond safety concerns, or to do tasks outside your role because the unit is short. A calm, clear response helps: \"I want to help the unit. As the peer, I can support him in hearing this — can the nurse deliver the decision and I'll be there with him?\"\n\nIf it's a pattern, bring it to your supervisor with specific examples and ask for help clarifying the peer role with the team. Peers are most valuable when patients trust them; protecting that trust is part of your job, not a lack of teamwork."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#12","title":"Preparing for certification renewal and CEUs","tags":["certification","renewal","CEUs","continuing education","credential","planning"],"ask":["When does your certification renew?","Which topics do you want to grow in this year?"],"body":"Don't wait for the renewal deadline. Put your credential in BlackLab's Education section with the renewal date so you get reminders at 90, 30 and 7 days, and log CEUs as you earn them with the certificate attached. Check your board's rules for how many hours you need, how many must be in ethics, and what providers count.\n\nBuild your learning plan around what you're seeing at work: if de-escalation is stressful, take advanced crisis training; if groups are new, look for facilitation or WRAP training; if you're seeing lots of opioid use, take harm-reduction and MOUD courses. Supervision hours often count toward certification as well — keep them logged with the supervisor's name and credentials so the paperwork is easy."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#13","title":"Turning floor insight into facility improvements","tags":["facilities","environment of care","ligature","improvements","safety committee","capital planning"],"ask":["What have you noticed on the floor that you'd never seen from the facilities office?","Which fix would have the biggest safety impact?"],"body":"Working shifts gives you a view of the building most facility directors never get: which doors get propped, where sightlines fail, which fixtures keep breaking, how noise builds in the day room, which furniture becomes a weapon. That's gold for your environment-of-care program and capital planning.\n\nCapture it systematically. Use BlackLab's Safety Rounds to log findings by area and turn them into work orders, and check the recurring-findings list to spot patterns. Bring themes — not individual patient stories — to the safety committee or environment-of-care meetings, and connect them to your environmental risk assessment. Keep the two roles clean: report what you see through normal channels, and let unit leaders hear about it from facilities in a way that respects their authority on the floor."},{"id":"conv-peer-lab#14","title":"Running on empty","tags":["compassion fatigue","burnout","self-care","exhausted","numb","support"],"ask":["What's been refilling your tank lately — anything?","Have you talked with anyone about how you're feeling?"],"body":"Feeling numb, irritable, dreading shifts or pulling away from people you love are signs your tank is low. That's common in this work, and it's information, not failure. Peers have an extra layer: the work can brush against your own history and your own recovery.\n\nStart small and concrete: one evening this week with nothing scheduled, one call to someone in your support network, one meeting or practice that's part of your recovery. Bring it to supervision and consider your EAP. Look at your check-in trends in BlackLab; if stress and drained scores keep climbing, it may be time to talk with your supervisors about schedule or load. And if things ever get dark, call or text 988."}]},{"id":"conv-ship-it","title":"Ship It: DevOps & GitHub","kind":"CONVERSATION","summary":"Get code from laptop to users safely — CI/CD, GitHub workflows, releases, incidents, security hardening and building a public portfolio.","icon":"","starters":["Set up a new repo the right way","My GitHub Actions workflow keeps failing","Plan a release checklist for an Android app","How do I run a blameless postmortem?","How do I make my GitHub profile stand out?"],"draws":["github","software-development","software-engineering","ai-engineering","ai-development"],"entries":[{"id":"conv-ship-it#1","title":"Setting up a new repository the right way","tags":["repository setup","GitHub","branch protection","templates","checklist"],"ask":["Is this repo public or private?","Solo project or a team?"],"body":"A checklist for a fresh repository:\n- Create it with a README, a .gitignore for your stack, and a license (if public).\n- Main branch protection or a ruleset: pull requests required, status checks required, no force pushes.\n- Merge settings: pick one strategy (squash is a good default) and enable automatic deletion of head branches.\n- CI from day one: a workflow that builds, lints and tests on every pull request. It's far easier to keep green than to make green later.\n- Security: Dependabot alerts and updates, secret scanning with push protection, CodeQL default setup, and a SECURITY.md.\n- Templates: an issue form for bugs, a pull request template with a short checklist.\n- Project hygiene: description, topics and website link; a CHANGELOG; CODEOWNERS once more than one person is involved.\n- Secrets: never in the repo — use Actions secrets, environment secrets, or keep signing keys local and gitignored, as BlackLab does with its keystore.\nFifteen minutes of setup saves weeks of cleanup."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#2","title":"Fixing a failing GitHub Actions workflow","tags":["GitHub Actions","CI failures","debugging","troubleshooting"],"ask":["What does the failing step's log say?","Did it ever pass, and what changed since?"],"body":"Debugging a red workflow:\n- Read the failing step's log from the bottom up; the first error is usually the real one. gh run view --log-failed gets it in the terminal.\n- Ask what changed: your code, a dependency (unpinned versions drift), the runner image (ubuntu-latest moves forward), an action's version, or an expired secret or token.\n- Reproduce locally with the same commands and versions. For tricky cases, tools like act run workflows locally, or temporarily add a debug step that prints environment details (never secrets).\n- Enable step debug logging by setting the ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG secret to true, or re-run with debug logging.\n- Check permissions: many failures are a GITHUB_TOKEN missing a permission (contents: write to push tags, packages: write to publish).\n- Caching problems: a corrupted or mismatched cache can cause weird failures — change the cache key to bust it.\n- Flaky tests: re-run once to confirm, then fix the flake rather than adding retries.\nPin action versions and runner images for repeatability, and update them deliberately."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#3","title":"Release checklist for an Android app","tags":["Android release","APK","signing","versioning","checklist","GitHub Releases"],"ask":["Are you releasing through the Play Store or GitHub?","Do you have a staged rollout plan?"],"body":"A dependable Android release routine:\n- Bump versionCode (must always increase) and versionName (semantic versioning).\n- Update the CHANGELOG and release notes in plain language.\n- Run the full checks: lint, unit tests, instrumented tests on an emulator, and a secret scan.\n- Build the release variant with R8 minification, and test that exact build on a real device — minification bugs only show up there.\n- Sign with the same release key every time; losing it means existing installs can't update. Back the keystore up securely and never commit it.\n- Generate a SHA-256 checksum for the APK.\n- Tag the commit (v1.2.0), push the tag, and create a GitHub Release with the APK and its .sha256 file attached — BlackLab's in-app updater checks both.\n- For the Play Store, use an app bundle and staged rollouts, and watch Android vitals for crashes before widening.\n- Verify the update path from the previous version, not just a fresh install, especially with database migrations."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#4","title":"Incident response basics","tags":["incident response","outage","on-call","communication","mitigation"],"ask":["Are you in an incident right now or planning ahead?","Who needs updates during an outage?"],"body":"When something breaks in production:\n- Declare it early. A clear \"we have an incident\" beats people quietly investigating in parallel.\n- Assign roles even on a small team: an incident commander who coordinates and decides, someone hands-on fixing, and someone communicating with users and stakeholders.\n- Mitigate first, diagnose later. Roll back the last deploy, flip the feature flag, fail over, or scale up — restore service, then find the root cause.\n- Communicate on a cadence: what's affected, what we're doing, and when the next update comes, even if nothing has changed.\n- Keep a timeline as you go — actions, observations, timestamps. It makes the postmortem accurate.\n- Close it formally when service is restored and monitoring confirms it.\n\nThis mirrors facility emergency response: stabilize the situation, protect people, communicate, then investigate. The same calm structure works whether it's a burst pipe or a broken deploy."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#5","title":"Running a blameless postmortem","tags":["postmortem","retrospective","blameless","root cause","learning"],"ask":["What incident do you want to review?","How do you make action items actually happen?"],"body":"A blameless postmortem assumes people acted reasonably given what they knew, and asks how the system let the failure happen. Blame makes people hide information; curiosity surfaces it.\n\nStructure: a summary of impact (who was affected, for how long), a timeline of detection, response and recovery, contributing factors (usually several — a latent bug, a missing alert, a confusing runbook, a risky deploy process), what went well, where we got lucky, and action items.\n\nAsk \"how\" and \"what made sense at the time\" rather than \"who\" and \"why didn't you.\" Five Whys helps go deeper, but real incidents rarely have one root cause.\n\nAction items need owners and dates, and should target prevention (tests, safeguards), detection (alerts) and mitigation (faster rollback). Track them like any other work. Share postmortems widely — they're some of the most valuable documents an organization writes."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#6","title":"Designing a CI/CD pipeline","tags":["CI/CD","pipeline design","deployment","environments","automation"],"ask":["How long does your pipeline take today?","How do you deploy now — by hand?"],"body":"A solid pipeline flows from fast to slow checks and from safe to risky environments:\n- On every pull request: format and lint, compile, unit tests, security scans, and a build of the artifact. Aim for under ten minutes.\n- On merge to main: the same plus integration tests, then build the release artifact once, version it, and store it.\n- Deploy that same artifact to staging automatically; run smoke tests.\n- Promote to production with an approval (GitHub environments with required reviewers) or automatically if your tests and monitoring are strong — through a canary or staged rollout.\n- After deploy: automated health checks and an easy, rehearsed rollback.\n\nPrinciples: build once and promote, keep configuration separate from code, make every step reproducible, cache dependencies, run independent jobs in parallel, and make failures loud and specific. Treat the pipeline itself as code — reviewed, versioned and tested — because it's production infrastructure for your team."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#7","title":"Security hardening for a GitHub project","tags":["security","hardening","GitHub","supply chain","secrets","checklist"],"ask":["Has a secret ever leaked from one of your repos?","Are your actions pinned?"],"body":"A practical hardening pass:\n- Account: two-factor authentication with a passkey or security key; review authorized OAuth apps and personal access tokens; prefer fine-grained, expiring tokens.\n- Repository: branch protection or rulesets on main; required reviews; signed commits if you can; secret scanning with push protection; Dependabot; CodeQL.\n- Actions: set GITHUB_TOKEN permissions to read-only by default; pin third-party actions to commit SHAs; avoid pull_request_target with untrusted code; pass untrusted input through environment variables, not inline scripts; use OIDC for cloud credentials; protect deployment environments.\n- Secrets: never in code; rotate on any suspicion; keep signing keys out of the repo entirely.\n- Dependencies: lockfiles, regular small updates, review new packages, and an SBOM for releases.\n- Releases: checksums and, ideally, attestations so users can verify what they download.\nRun a local secret scan before pushing — BlackLab's scripts/secret-scan.sh is a good habit worth copying."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#8","title":"Semantic versioning and changelogs","tags":["semantic versioning","versioning","changelog","releases","communication"],"ask":["How do you decide between a minor and a major bump?","Who reads your changelog?"],"body":"Semantic versioning communicates impact: MAJOR for breaking changes, MINOR for new backward-compatible features, PATCH for backward-compatible fixes. For apps rather than libraries, \"breaking\" means users must change how they work — a removed feature or a data migration they'll notice.\n\nChangelogs are for humans. Group entries under Added, Changed, Fixed, Removed and Security (the Keep a Changelog format), write them in user terms (\"Summaries can now be saved as notes\") rather than commit-speak, and link to issues or pull requests. Keep an Unreleased section at the top that fills up as work merges.\n\nAutomation helps: conventional commit messages let tools like release-please generate changelog entries and version bumps, and GitHub's auto-generated release notes summarize merged pull requests by label. Still, a human pass that highlights what matters most is worth five minutes per release."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#9","title":"Feature flags and safe rollouts","tags":["feature flags","rollout","canary","dark launch","risk"],"ask":["Do you use flags today?","How do you clean up old flags?"],"body":"Feature flags separate deploying code from releasing features. Merge unfinished work behind a flag that's off, ship it, then turn it on — for yourself first, then a small group, then everyone. If something goes wrong, flip it off in seconds without a deploy.\n\nKinds of flags: release flags (temporary, for rollout), experiment flags (A/B tests), ops flags or kill switches (to disable expensive features under load), and permission flags (features for certain users or plans).\n\nCosts: every flag doubles the paths through code. Keep them few and short-lived, name them clearly, give each an owner and an expiry, and remove release flags soon after full rollout. Test both states in CI for important flags.\n\nFor mobile apps, flags are even more valuable, because you can't roll back an installed binary quickly — server-controlled flags or remote configuration let you disable a broken feature without waiting for users to update."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#10","title":"On-call without burning out","tags":["on-call","alerts","burnout","sustainability","operations"],"ask":["How noisy are your alerts?","What happens when you get paged at 3 a.m.?"],"body":"On-call is sustainable when alerts are rare, actionable and owned.\n- Every page should mean a user-impacting problem that needs a human now. Everything else becomes a ticket or a dashboard.\n- Alert on symptoms (error rates, latency, SLO burn) rather than causes (CPU at 80%).\n- Every alert links to a runbook: what it means, how to check, how to mitigate.\n- Review pages weekly: delete noisy alerts, fix recurring causes, automate common fixes.\n- Rotate fairly, hand off cleanly with notes, and compensate or give time back after rough nights.\n- Make escalation normal — asking for help is a strength, not a failure.\n\nFacility directors know this world: the 2 a.m. call about a flooded basement. The same principles apply — clear escalation paths, good documentation, trusted vendors on call, and fixing root causes so the same call doesn't come again next month."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#11","title":"Building a GitHub portfolio that stands out","tags":["portfolio","GitHub profile","career","open source","personal brand"],"ask":["Which of your projects are you proudest of?","Who do you want the portfolio to impress?"],"body":"Recruiters and hiring managers skim. Make it easy for them:\n- A profile README: who you are in two lines, what you build, your stack, and links to your best two or three projects.\n- Pin your best repositories — finished, useful projects rather than many half-done tutorials.\n- Each pinned repo gets an excellent README: the problem, screenshots or a demo GIF, how to run it, key design decisions, and what you learned. BlackLab's README with screenshots and a feature list is a solid model.\n- Show engineering maturity: tests, CI badges, releases, issues and a changelog — signs you ship like a professional.\n- Write about your work: short posts or docs explaining a tricky problem you solved.\n- Real-world angle: projects that solve problems from your actual job (facility operations, maintenance, energy) stand out more than another clone app, because they show domain judgment.\nConsistency over time — steady commits and finished releases — tells a better story than a burst of activity."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#12","title":"Contributing to open source","tags":["open source","contributing","community","pull requests","maintainers"],"ask":["Is there a project you use that you'd like to give back to?","What's held you back from contributing?"],"body":"Contributing to open source builds skills and reputation. A low-stress path:\n- Start with projects you use. You already understand the problem.\n- Read CONTRIBUTING.md and the code of conduct; look for labels like \"good first issue\" or \"help wanted.\"\n- Begin small: documentation fixes, clearer error messages, a missing test, a reproducible bug report. Maintainers love those.\n- Comment on an issue before starting bigger work so you don't duplicate effort or build something they won't accept.\n- Follow the project's conventions exactly, keep pull requests focused, and include tests.\n- Be patient and gracious with review; maintainers are often volunteers.\n\nMaintaining your own project is a different job: set expectations in the README, use issue templates, automate CI and releases, and say no kindly to features that don't fit. Sustainable maintenance beats heroic bursts."},{"id":"conv-ship-it#13","title":"Automating the boring stuff with GitHub","tags":["automation","GitHub Actions","gh CLI","scheduled jobs","productivity"],"ask":["What chore do you repeat every week?","Want ideas for scheduled workflows?"],"body":"GitHub can run your chores:\n- Scheduled workflows (cron) to generate weekly reports, check links in docs, clean up stale branches, or refresh data files.\n- Labeling and triage: automatically label pull requests by changed paths, assign reviewers, and close stale issues politely after a warning.\n- Release automation: draft release notes as pull requests merge, publish on tag push, and attach build artifacts and checksums.\n- Dependency upkeep: Dependabot with grouped updates and auto-merge for safe patches.\n- Notifications: post to chat or email when deploys finish or nightly jobs fail.\n- The gh CLI in scripts: bulk-edit issues, create repos from templates, and query the API with jq for reports.\n\nA personal example: a weekly workflow that summarizes merged pull requests and open issues into a markdown report, committed to the repo or sent by email. Start with the chore that annoys you most; one small automation often pays for itself within a month."}]}];
